      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    idx = 0\n    \n    H_A = int(input[idx]); idx += 1\n    W_A = int(input[idx]); idx += 1\n    A = []; \n    for _ in range(H_A):\n        A.append(input[idx]); idx += 1\n        \n    H_B = int(input[idx]); idx += 1\n    W_B = int(input[idx]); idx += 1\n    B = []\n    for _ in range(H_B):\n        B.append(input[idx]); idx += 1\n        \n    H_X = int(input[idx]); idx += 1\n    W_X = int(input[idx]); idx += 1\n    X = []\n    for _ in range(H_X):\n        X.append(input[idx]); idx += 1\n\n    # Find bounding box of black squares in A\n    min_rA, max_rA, min_cA, max_cA = H_A, -1, W_A, -1\n    for r in range(H_A):\n        for c in range(W_A):\n            if A[r][c] == '#':\n                if r < min_rA: min_rA = r\n                if r > max_rA: max_rA = r\n                if c < min_cA: min_cA = c\n                if c > max_cA: max_cA = c\n                \n    # Find bounding box of black squares in B\n    min_rB, max_rB, min_cB, max_cB = H_B, -1, W_B, -1\n    for r in range(H_B):\n        for c in range(W_B):\n            if B[r][c] == '#':\n                if r < min_rB: min_rB = r\n                if r > max_rB: max_rB = r\n                if c < min_cB: min_cB = c\n                if c > max_cB: max_cB = c\n\n    # Find bounding box of black squares in X\n    min_rx, max_rx, min_cx, max_cx = H_X, -1, W_X, -1\n    for r in range(H_X):\n        for c in range(W_X):\n            if X[r][c] == '#':\n                if r < min_rx: min_rx = r\n                if r > max_rx: max_rx = r\n                if c < min_cx: min_cx = c\n                if c > max_cx: max_cx = c\n                \n    # If X has no black squares, but problem says at least one black square exists in each.\n    \n    # We need to place A and B on a large canvas C such that:\n    # 1. The union of their black squares fits within an H_X x W_X window.\n    # 2. The pattern of black squares in that window exactly matches X.\n    # 3. All black squares of A and B are inside that window.\n    \n    # Let's define the relative position of A and B.\n    # Let A be placed at (0,0) on canvas C for reference.\n    # Let B be placed at (dr, dc) on canvas C relative to A's top-left.\n    # So if A's top-left is at (rA, cA) on C, B's top-left is at (rA+dr, cA+dc).\n    # Since we can translate the whole assembly arbitrarily, we can fix A's position relative to the cut-out window.\n    \n    # Alternatively, let's iterate over all possible translations of B relative to A.\n    # Let delta_r be the row offset of B's top-left relative to A's top-left.\n    # Let delta_c be the col offset of B's top-left relative to A's top-left.\n    \n    # The extent of the combined shape of A and B determines the minimal window size needed to contain all black squares.\n    # Specifically, if we fix A at (0,0) and B at (dr, dc), the black squares of A are in rows [0, H_A-1], cols [0, W_A-1].\n    # The black squares of B are in rows [dr, dr+H_B-1], cols [dc, dc+W_B-1].\n    # The overall bounding box of ALL black squares from A and B must fit within H_X x W_X.\n    # Moreover, the specific pattern formed by the union must match X.\n    \n    # Since H, W <= 10, the number of relative positions is small.\n    # dr can range from roughly -(H_B) to H_A, similarly for dc.\n    # More precisely, the top-left of the combined bounding box can be shifted.\n    \n    # Let's iterate over all possible placements of B relative to A.\n    # Let A's top-left be at (0,0).\n    # Let B's top-left be at (i, j).\n    # i can range from -H_B + 1 to H_A - 1 ? No, B can be anywhere.\n    # But if B is too far away, the bounding box might exceed X's size.\n    # The total height of the union of bounding boxes of black squares must be <= H_X.\n    # The total width must be <= W_X.\n    \n    # Range for i (row offset of B relative to A):\n    # Min i: If B is completely above A, i could be negative.\n    # Max i: If B is completely below A, i could be positive.\n    # However, we only care about cases where the resulting union fits in H_X x W_X.\n    # The vertical span of A is H_A, of B is H_B.\n    # If B is at row i, its rows are [i, i+H_B-1].\n    # The union of row ranges is [min(0, i), max(H_A-1, i+H_B-1)].\n    # Height = max(H_A-1, i+H_B-1) - min(0, i) + 1.\n    # This height must be <= H_X.\n    # Similarly for width.\n    \n    # Let's determine valid ranges for i and j.\n    # i_min: such that max(H_A-1, i+H_B-1) - min(0, i) + 1 <= H_X\n    # i_max: same constraint.\n    \n    # Brute force i and j.\n    # Since H_X, W_X <= 10, and H_A, W_A etc <= 10.\n    # The relative shift i can be roughly in [-10, 20].\n    \n    possible_positions = []\n    \n    # Iterate over all possible top-left positions of B relative to A's top-left at (0,0)\n    # Range for i: from -H_B to H_A (broadly speaking, if outside this, the height likely exceeds 10+10=20 which is > 10)\n    # Actually, let's just bound it by H_X + H_A + H_B to be safe, but tighter bounds are better.\n    # If the bounding box height > H_X, skip.\n    \n    for i in range(-H_B - 5, H_A + 5):\n        for j in range(-W_B - 5, W_A + 5):\n            # Calculate bounding box of all black squares\n            # Black squares of A: rows 0..H_A-1, cols 0..W_A-1\n            # Black squares of B: rows i..i+H_B-1, cols j..j+W_B-1\n            \n            # Collect all black square coordinates\n            blacks = []\n            for r in range(H_A):\n                for c in range(W_A):\n                    if A[r][c] == '#':\n                        blacks.append((r, c))\n            for r in range(H_B):\n                for c in range(W_B):\n                    if B[r][c] == '#':\n                        blacks.append((i + r, j + c))\n            \n            if not blacks:\n                continue # Should not happen per constraints\n            \n            min_r = min(p[0] for p in blacks)\n            max_r = max(p[0] for p in blacks)\n            min_c = min(p[1] for p in blacks)\n            max_c = max(p[1] for p in blacks)\n            \n            h_span = max_r - min_r + 1\n            w_span = max_c - min_c + 1\n            \n            if h_span > H_X or w_span > W_X:\n                continue\n            \n            # Now, we need to check if this configuration can form X.\n            # The \"cut out\" sheet is an H_X x W_X area.\n            # The problem says: \"Cut out an H_X x W_X area... The black squares... may overlap\".\n            # \"The cut-out sheet coincides with sheet X\".\n            # This implies that the union of black squares from A and B, when shifted to start at (0,0) of the H_X x W_X window, must match X exactly.\n            # And importantly, ANY cell in the H_X x W_X window that is NOT covered by a black square from A or B must be transparent ('.') in X.\n            # And ANY cell in the H_X x W_X window that IS covered by a black square from A or B must be black ('#') in X.\n            \n            # So, construct the H_X x W_X grid based on the current placement (i, j).\n            # The grid G is initialized to '.'.\n            # For each black square in blacks, mark G[r-min_r_offset, c-min_c_offset] as '#'?\n            # Wait. The cut-out area is H_X x W_X. It doesn't have to start at (min_r, min_c).\n            # However, condition 1 says: \"The cut-out sheet includes all black squares of sheets A and B.\"\n            # This means the bounding box of black squares must be contained within the H_X x W_X window.\n            # Condition 2 says: \"The cut-out sheet coincides with sheet X\".\n            # This means the pattern of black/transparent in the H_X x W_X window must match X.\n            \n            # So, we need to find an offset (offset_r, offset_c) within the H_X x W_X window such that:\n            # 1. All black squares (relative to some global origin) fall within the window defined by offset_r, offset_c.\n            # 2. The set of black squares in the window matches the '#' positions in X.\n            # 3. The set of transparent squares in the window matches the '.' positions in X.\n            \n            # Actually, simpler view:\n            # The final result is a binary matrix M of size H_X x W_X.\n            # M[r][c] = '#' if there is a black square from A or B at that location in the window.\n            # M[r][c] = '.' otherwise.\n            # We require M == X.\n            \n            # The window can be positioned anywhere on the infinite canvas C.\n            # Let the top-left of the window on the canvas be (W_r, W_c).\n            # Then for any (r, c) in 0..H_X-1, 0..W_X-1:\n            # Canvas pos: (W_r + r, W_c + c).\n            # M[r][c] is '#' if Canvas(W_r+r, W_c+c) is black, else '.'.\n            # Canvas is black if it falls under a black square of A or B.\n            \n            # We already fixed A at (0,0) and B at (i,j).\n            # So Canvas(r,c) is black if:\n            #   (0<=r<H_A and 0<=c<W_A and A[r][c]=='#') OR\n            #   (0<=r-i<H_B and 0<=c-j<W_B and B[r-i][c-j]=='#')\n            \n            # We need to find (W_r, W_c) such that the resulting H_X x W_X grid matches X.\n            # And all black squares from A and B must be inside the window [W_r, W_r+H_X-1] x [W_c, W_c+W_X-1].\n            \n            # Let's iterate over all possible (W_r, W_c).\n            # Since the window must contain all black squares, W_r <= min_r and W_r + H_X - 1 >= max_r.\n            # So W_r can range from max_r - H_X + 1 to min_r.\n            # Similarly for W_c.\n            \n            # If min_r > max_r + H_X - 1 (i.e., span > H_X), we already skipped.\n            # Range for W_r:\n            start_wr = max_r - H_X + 1\n            end_wr = min_r\n            start_wc = max_c - W_X + 1\n            end_wc = min_c\n            \n            found = False\n            \n            # Check if ranges are valid\n            if start_wr > end_wr or start_wc > end_wc:\n                pass # No valid window position for this (i,j)\n            else:\n                for wr in range(start_wr, end_wr + 1):\n                    for wc in range(start_wc, end_wc + 1):\n                        # Construct the grid for this window\n                        match = True\n                        for r in range(H_X):\n                            for c in range(W_X):\n                                cr = wr + r\n                                cc = wc + c\n                                \n                                # Is this cell black?\n                                is_black = False\n                                # Check A\n                                if 0 <= cr < H_A and 0 <= cc < W_A:\n                                    if A[cr][cc] == '#':\n                                        is_black = True\n                                # Check B\n                                elif 0 <= cr - i < H_B and 0 <= cc - j < W_B:\n                                    if B[cr - i][cc - j] == '#':\n                                        is_black = True\n                                \n                                # Compare with X\n                                if is_black and X[r][c] != '#':\n                                    match = False\n                                    break\n                                if not is_black and X[r][c] != '.':\n                                    match = False\n                                    break\n                            if not match:\n                                break\n                        \n                        if match:\n                            print(\"Yes\")\n                            return\n\n    print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
