    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere are N rectangular cuboids in a three-dimensional space.\nThese cuboids do not overlap.  Formally, for any two different cuboids among them, their intersection has a volume of 0.\nThe diagonal of the i-th cuboid is a segment that connects two points (X_{{i,1}},Y_{{i,1}},Z_{{i,1}}) and (X_{{i,2}},Y_{{i,2}},Z_{{i,2}}), and its edges are all parallel to one of the coordinate axes.\nFor each cuboid, find the number of other cuboids that share a face with it.\r\nFormally, for each i, find the number of j with 1\\leq j \\leq N and j\\neq i such that the intersection of the surfaces of the i-th and j-th cuboids has a positive area.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\r\nX_{{1,1}} Y_{{1,1}} Z_{{1,1}} X_{{1,2}} Y_{{1,2}} Z_{{1,2}}\r\n\\vdots\r\nX_{{N,1}} Y_{{N,1}} Z_{{N,1}} X_{{N,2}} Y_{{N,2}} Z_{{N,2}}\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\leq N \\leq 10^5\n- 0 \\leq X_{{i,1}} < X_{{i,2}} \\leq 100\n- 0 \\leq Y_{{i,1}} < Y_{{i,2}} \\leq 100\n- 0 \\leq Z_{{i,1}} < Z_{{i,2}} \\leq 100\n- Cuboids do not have an intersection with a positive volume.\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n4\r\n0 0 0 1 1 1\r\n0 0 1 1 1 2\r\n1 1 1 2 2 2\r\n3 3 3 4 4 4\n\nSample Output 1\n\n1\r\n1\r\n0\r\n0\r\n\nThe 1-st and 2-nd cuboids share a rectangle whose diagonal is the segment connecting two points (0,0,1) and (1,1,1).\r\nThe 1-st and 3-rd cuboids share a point (1,1,1), but do not share a surface.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3\r\n0 0 10 10 10 20\r\n3 4 1 15 6 10\r\n0 9 6 1 20 10\n\nSample Output 2\n\n2\r\n1\r\n1\n\nSample Input 3\n\n8\r\n0 0 0 1 1 1\r\n0 0 1 1 1 2\r\n0 1 0 1 2 1\r\n0 1 1 1 2 2\r\n1 0 0 2 1 1\r\n1 0 1 2 1 2\r\n1 1 0 2 2 1\r\n1 1 1 2 2 2\n\nSample Output 3\n\n3\r\n3\r\n3\r\n3\r\n3\r\n3\r\n3\r\n3",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(data)\n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    cuboids = []\n    for i in range(N):\n        x1 = int(next(iterator))\n        y1 = int(next(iterator))\n        z1 = int(next(iterator))\n        x2 = int(next(iterator))\n        y2 = int(next(iterator))\n        z2 = int(next(iterator))\n        cuboids.append((x1, y1, z1, x2, y2, z2))\n\n    # For each cuboid, we want to count how many other cuboids share a face.\n    # Two cuboids share a face if they are adjacent along one axis.\n    # Since cuboids don't overlap with positive volume, sharing a face means:\n    # They are adjacent in X, Y, or Z direction.\n    # Specifically, for two cuboids A and B:\n    # - Share face perpendicular to X-axis: \n    #   A's x-range [ax1, ax2] and B's x-range [bx1, bx2] touch at one value (e.g., ax2 == bx1 or bx2 == ax1)\n    #   AND their projections on Y-Z plane overlap with positive area.\n    #   Overlap in Y: max(ay1, by1) < min(ay2, by2)\n    #   Overlap in Z: max(az1, bz1) < min(az2, bz2)\n    \n    # Given N up to 10^5, O(N^2) is too slow.\n    # However, coordinates are small (0 to 100). This suggests we can use a grid-based approach.\n    \n    # Let's create a 3D grid to mark occupied cells? No, cuboids can be larger than 1x1x1.\n    # But the coordinates are integers from 0 to 100. The cuboids are defined by integer coordinates.\n    # We can think of the space as being divided into unit cubes.\n    # Each cuboid occupies a set of unit cubes [x1, x2) x [y1, y2) x [z1, z2).\n    \n    # Since coordinates are <= 100, the maximum dimension is 100.\n    # Total unit cubes is at most 100*100*100 = 1,000,000. This is manageable.\n    \n    # We can map each unit cube (ix, iy, iz) to the cuboid index that contains it.\n    # Then, for each pair of adjacent unit cubes (sharing a face), check if they belong to different cuboids.\n    # If so, increment the count for both cuboids.\n    # But we need to be careful: if two cuboids share multiple faces, we might double count?\n    # No, the problem asks for the number of OTHER cuboids that share A FACE.\n    # So if cuboid A and B share a face, it counts as 1 for A and 1 for B.\n    # Even if they share multiple disjoint faces? The problem says \"share a face\".\n    # \"intersection of the surfaces ... has a positive area\".\n    # If two cuboids are adjacent, their intersection is a rectangle (possibly degenerate to line or point, but here positive area).\n    # Can two non-overlapping cuboids share more than one disjoint face?\n    # In 3D, if they don't overlap, they can touch at most at one connected component of the boundary?\n    # Actually, consider L-shapes. But these are rectangular cuboids.\n    # Two rectangular cuboids that do not overlap can share at most one face (connected region).\n    # Proof: If they shared two disjoint faces, they would have to wrap around each other, which implies overlap or non-cuboid shape.\n    # So, we just need to detect adjacency.\n    \n    # Approach:\n    # 1. Create a 3D array `grid[101][101][101]` initialized to -1.\n    #    grid[i][j][k] stores the cuboid index that contains the unit cube [i, i+1) x [j, j+1) x [k, k+1).\n    #    Note: coordinates are up to 100, so indices 0..99 are valid for unit cubes.\n    #    A cuboid from x1 to x2 covers unit cubes x1, ..., x2-1.\n    \n    # 2. Fill the grid.\n    # 3. Iterate through all internal faces between adjacent unit cubes.\n    #    There are three directions: X, Y, Z.\n    #    For each direction, iterate over all possible faces.\n    #    If the two unit cubes across the face belong to different cuboids A and B, then A and B share a face.\n    #    Add B to A's neighbors and A to B's neighbors. Use sets to avoid duplicates.\n    \n    # 4. Print the size of the neighbor set for each cuboid.\n    \n    MAX_COORD = 100\n    grid = [[[-1] * MAX_COORD for _ in range(MAX_COORD)] for __ in range(MAX_COORD)]\n    \n    for idx, (x1, y1, z1, x2, y2, z2) in enumerate(cuboids):\n        for x in range(x1, x2):\n            for y in range(y1, y2):\n                for z in range(z1, z2):\n                    grid[x][y][z] = idx\n                    \n    neighbor_counts = [set() for _ in range(N)]\n    \n    # Check adjacency in X direction\n    # Face between x and x+1, for y in 0..99, z in 0..99\n    for x in range(MAX_COORD - 1):\n        for y in range(MAX_COORD):\n            for z in range(MAX_COORD):\n                c1 = grid[x][y][z]\n                c2 = grid[x+1][y][z]\n                if c1 != -1 and c2 != -1 and c1 != c2:\n                    neighbor_counts[c1].add(c2)\n                    neighbor_counts[c2].add(c1)\n                    \n    # Check adjacency in Y direction\n    # Face between y and y+1, for x in 0..99, z in 0..99\n    for y in range(MAX_COORD - 1):\n        for x in range(MAX_COORD):\n            for z in range(MAX_COORD):\n                c1 = grid[x][y][z]\n                c2 = grid[x][y+1][z]\n                if c1 != -1 and c2 != -1 and c1 != c2:\n                    neighbor_counts[c1].add(c2)\n                    neighbor_counts[c2].add(c1)\n                    \n    # Check adjacency in Z direction\n    # Face between z and z+1, for x in 0..99, y in 0..99\n    for z in range(MAX_COORD - 1):\n        for x in range(MAX_COORD):\n            for y in range(MAX_COORD):\n                c1 = grid[x][y][z]\n                c2 = grid[x][y][z+1]\n                if c1 != -1 and c2 != -1 and c1 != c2:\n                    neighbor_counts[c1].add(c2)\n                    neighbor_counts[c2].add(c1)\n                    \n    for i in range(N):\n        print(len(neighbor_counts[i]))\n\nsolve()\n```",
