    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_436",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere is a grid of H rows and W columns. Some cells (possibly zero) contain magnets.\nThe state of the grid is represented by H strings S_1, S_2, \\ldots, S_H of length W. If the j-th character of S_i is #, it indicates that there is a magnet in the cell at the i-th row from the top and j-th column from the left; if it is ., it indicates that the cell is empty.\nTakahashi, wearing an iron armor, can move in the grid as follows:\n\n- If any of the cells vertically or horizontally adjacent to the current cell contains a magnet, he cannot move at all.\n- Otherwise, he can move to any one of the vertically or horizontally adjacent cells.\nHowever, he cannot exit the grid.\n\nFor each cell without a magnet, define its degree of freedom as the number of cells he can reach by repeatedly moving from that cell. Find the maximum degree of freedom among all cells without magnets in the grid.\nHere, in the definition of degree of freedom, \"cells he can reach by repeatedly moving\" mean cells that can be reached from the initial cell by some sequence of moves (possibly zero moves). It is not necessary that there is a sequence of moves that visits all such reachable cells starting from the initial cell. Specifically, each cell itself (without a magnet) is always included in the cells reachable from that cell.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nH W\nS_1\nS_2\n\\vdots\nS_H\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the maximum degree of freedom among all cells without magnets.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\leq H, W \\leq 1000\n- H and W are integers.\n- S_i is a string of length W consisting of . and #.\n- There is at least one cell without a magnet.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3 5\n.#...\n.....\n.#..#\n\nSample Output 1\n\n9\n\nLet (i,j) denote the cell at the i-th row from the top and j-th column from the left. If Takahashi starts at (2,3), possible movements include:\n\n- (2,3) \\to (2,4) \\to (1,4) \\to (1,5) \\to (2,5)\n- (2,3) \\to (2,4) \\to (3,4)\n- (2,3) \\to (2,2)\n- (2,3) \\to (1,3)\n- (2,3) \\to (3,3)\n\nThus, including the cells he passes through, he can reach at least nine cells from (2,3).\nActually, no other cells can be reached, so the degree of freedom for (2,3) is 9.\nThis is the maximum degree of freedom among all cells without magnets, so print 9.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3 3\n..#\n#..\n..#\n\nSample Output 2\n\n1\n\nFor any cell without a magnet, there is a magnet in at least one of the adjacent cells.\nThus, he cannot move from any of these cells, so their degrees of freedom are 1.\nTherefore, print 1.",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    \n    if not input_data:\n        return\n\n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    \n    try:\n        H = int(next(iterator))\n        W = int(next(iterator))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    S = []\n    for _ in range(H):\n        S.append(next(iterator))\n\n    # Identify which cells have magnets\n    # grid[i][j] is True if there is a magnet, False otherwise\n    # Also identify \"blocked\" cells: empty cells adjacent to any magnet\n    # If a cell is blocked, its degree of freedom is 1 (itself) because it cannot move.\n    \n    # We need to compute connected components of \"safe\" cells.\n    # A cell is safe if it is '.' AND it has NO adjacent '#'.\n    # If a cell is '.', but has an adjacent '#', it is isolated in terms of movement \n    # (cannot move out), so its reachable set is just itself.\n    \n    # Let's mark all cells.\n    # 0: Empty cell that is adjacent to a magnet (Blocked/Isolated)\n    # 1: Empty cell NOT adjacent to any magnet (Safe/Movable)\n    # 2: Magnet\n    \n    cell_type = [[0] * W for _ in range(H)]\n    \n    # Directions for neighbors\n    dirs = [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]\n    \n    # First pass: Identify magnets\n    for r in range(H):\n        for c in range(W):\n            if S[r][c] == '#':\n                cell_type[r][c] = 2\n    \n    # Second pass: Identify blocked empty cells (adjacent to magnets)\n    for r in range(H):\n        for c in range(W):\n            if cell_type[r][c] == 0: # It's an empty cell\n                has_magnet_neighbor = False\n                for dr, dc in dirs:\n                    nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n                    if 0 <= nr < H and 0 <= nc < W:\n                        if cell_type[nr][nc] == 2:\n                            has_magnet_neighbor = True\n                            break\n                if has_magnet_neighbor:\n                    cell_type[r][c] = 0 # Blocked, stays 0\n                else:\n                    cell_type[r][c] = 1 # Safe\n    \n    # Now, find connected components of '1's.\n    # The size of the component is the degree of freedom for any cell in that component.\n    # For '0' cells, the degree of freedom is 1.\n    # For '2' cells, they are magnets, so we ignore them (question asks for max among cells WITHOUT magnets).\n    \n    visited = [[False] * W for _ in range(H)]\n    max_degree = 0\n    \n    # Check if there are any safe cells. If not, max degree is 1 (from blocked cells).\n    # But constraints say at least one cell without magnet.\n    \n    has_safe_cell = False\n    \n    for r in range(H):\n        for c in range(W):\n            if cell_type[r][c] == 1 and not visited[r][c]:\n                has_safe_cell = True\n                # BFS/DFS to find component size\n                queue = deque([(r, c)])\n                visited[r][c] = True\n                component_size = 0\n                \n                while queue:\n                    cr, cc = queue.popleft()\n                    component_size += 1\n                    \n                    for dr, dc in dirs:\n                        nr, nc = cr + dr, cc + dc\n                        if 0 <= nr < H and 0 <= nc < W:\n                            if cell_type[nr][nc] == 1 and not visited[nr][nc]:\n                                visited[nr][nc] = True\n                                queue.append((nr, nc))\n                \n                if component_size > max_degree:\n                    max_degree = component_size\n\n    if not has_safe_cell:\n        # All non-magnet cells are blocked (adjacent to magnets).\n        # Their degree of freedom is 1.\n        print(1)\n    else:\n        # Max degree could also be 1 if a safe cell is isolated? \n        # No, if it's safe, it can move to other safe cells.\n        # However, we initialized max_degree = 0.\n        # If there are safe cells, max_degree will be updated to at least 1.\n        # But wait, if a safe cell is isolated (no neighbors are safe), component_size is 1.\n        # Is it possible for a safe cell to have no safe neighbors?\n        # Yes. Example: \n        # ...\n        # #.#\n        # ...\n        # Center (1,0) is safe? No, (1,0) is magnet.\n        # Let's look at sample 2.\n        # ..#\n        # #..\n        # ..#\n        # Cells without magnets:\n        # (0,0): adj to (1,0)# -> Blocked\n        # (0,1): adj to (0,2)#, (1,1). Wait (1,1) is '.'. \n        # Let's trace Sample 2 carefully.\n        # S = [\". . #\", \"# . .\", \". . #\"]\n        # (0,0) '.': neighbors (0,1)'.', (1,0)'#'. Has magnet neighbor. Blocked.\n        # (0,1) '.': neighbors (0,0)'.', (0,2)'#', (1,1)'.'. Has magnet neighbor (0,2). Blocked.\n        # (1,1) '.': neighbors (0,1)'.', (1,0)'#', (1,2)'.', (2,1)'.'. Has magnet neighbor (1,0). Blocked.\n        # (1,2) '.': neighbors (0,2)'#', (1,1)'.', (2,2)'#'. Has magnet neighbors. Blocked.\n        # (2,0) '.': neighbors (1,0)'#', (2,1)'.'. Has magnet neighbor (1,0). Blocked.\n        # (2,1) '.': neighbors (2,0)'.', (1,1)'.', (2,2)'#'. Has magnet neighbor (2,2). Blocked.\n        # All non-magnet cells are blocked. So max degree is 1.\n        \n        # What if we have a single safe cell?\n        # e.g.\n        # .\n        # It has no neighbors. cell_type[0][0] = 1. Component size 1.\n        # So max_degree will be at least 1 if has_safe_cell is true.\n        \n        # Just ensure max_degree is at least 1 if there are ANY non-magnet cells.\n        # Since constraints say at least one cell without magnet, and if none are safe, we printed 1.\n        # If there are safe cells, max_degree >= 1.\n        \n        print(max_degree)\n\nsolve()\n```",
