      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to:\n1. Find letters that appear in both lowercase and uppercase forms in the word\n2. Count the unique letters that qualify\n\n**My approach:**\n- Create a set of all characters in the word\n- Iterate through the set, and for each character, check if its opposite case version also exists in the set\n- Count those that match the condition\n\n```python\ndef numberOfSpecialChars(word: str) -> int:\n    seen = set(word)\n    count = 0\n    for c in seen:\n        if c.islower() and c.upper() in seen:\n            count += 1\n    return count\n```\n\n**Why this works:**\n- `set(word)` gives unique characters, preventing double counting when a letter appears multiple times\n- By only checking lowercase letters, I avoid counting the same letter twice (once from lowercase check, once from uppercase check)\n- For each lowercase letter, I verify its uppercase counterpart exists in the word\n\n**Verification with examples:**\n- `\"aaAbcBC\"`: seen = {a, A, b, B, c, C} → 'a' (A exists ✓), 'b' (B exists ✓), 'c' (C exists ✓) → **3** ✓\n- `\"abc\"`: no uppercase letters present → **0** ✓\n- `\"abBCab\"`: seen = {a, b, B, C} → 'a' (A not in set ✗), 'b' (B exists ✓) → **1** ✓",
