      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    \n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n        A = int(next(iterator))\n        B = int(next(iterator))\n        \n        D = []\n        for _ in range(N):\n            D.append(int(next(iterator)))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    # The week has L = A + B days.\n    # Days 1..A are holidays, A+1..A+B are weekdays.\n    # Let today be day T (1-indexed within the week cycle).\n    # Then the i-th plan is on day (T + D[i] - 1) % L + 1 ? \n    # Actually, if today is day X (1 <= X <= L), then D_i days later is day:\n    # (X + D_i - 1) % L + 1? No, let's think carefully.\n    # If today is day 1, 1 day later is day 2.\n    # If today is day L, 1 day later is day 1.\n    # So the day of the week for plan i is ((X - 1 + D[i]) % L) + 1.\n    # We need this value to be <= A for all i.\n    # So ((X - 1 + D[i]) % L) < A for all i.\n    # Let Y = X - 1. Y ranges from 0 to L-1.\n    # Condition: (Y + D[i]) % L < A for all i=1..N.\n    \n    # Let's analyze the condition for each D[i].\n    # For a specific D[i], the values of Y such that (Y + D[i]) % L < A are:\n    # Y + D[i] mod L is in [0, A-1].\n    # This means Y mod L is in [-D[i], -D[i] + A - 1] mod L.\n    # Let's define intervals modulo L.\n    # An interval [s, e] modulo L where s <= e is just {s, s+1, ..., e}.\n    # If s > e, it wraps around: {s, ..., L-1, 0, ..., e}.\n    \n    # Here, the \"start\" offset for Y based on D[i] is (-D[i]) mod L.\n    # The length of the valid interval is A.\n    # So valid Y for plan i are those where (Y + D[i]) % L in [0, A-1].\n    # Let R_i = (-D[i]) % L.\n    # Then Y must satisfy (Y + D[i]) % L < A\n    # <=> Y % L in [R_i, R_i + A - 1] (modulo L).\n    \n    # We need to find if there exists a Y in [0, L-1] that satisfies this for ALL i.\n    # This is equivalent to finding if the intersection of all these intervals is non-empty.\n    \n    L = A + B\n    \n    # Instead of intersecting N intervals, we can check if the constraints are compatible.\n    # Note that D[i] are sorted.\n    # Let's consider the differences between D[i].\n    # If we shift Y by D[j] - D[i], the condition for D[j] relates to D[i].\n    \n    # Alternative approach:\n    # The condition is: for all i, (Y + D[i]) % L < A.\n    # This implies that no two plans can fall into the \"weekday\" region relative to each other in a way that conflicts?\n    # Not exactly.\n    \n    # Let's look at the gaps.\n    # The \"bad\" values for (Y + D[i]) % L are [A, L-1]. Length B.\n    # So Y % L must NOT be in [A - D[i], L - 1 - D[i]] mod L.\n    # Let Bad_i be the set of Y such that plan i falls on a weekday.\n    # Bad_i = { Y | (Y + D[i]) % L >= A }\n    #         = { Y | (Y + D[i]) % L in [A, L-1] }\n    # Let Z = Y + D[i]. Z % L in [A, L-1].\n    # Y % L in [A - D[i], L - 1 - D[i]] mod L.\n    \n    # We need Y NOT in Union(Bad_i) for all i.\n    # So we need to check if Union(Bad_i) covers the entire circle [0, L-1].\n    # If it does, answer is No. Otherwise Yes.\n    \n    # Each Bad_i is an interval (possibly wrapping) of length B on the circle of size L.\n    # We have N such intervals. We want to know if their union is the whole circle [0, L-1].\n    \n    # Since N is up to 2*10^5, we can merge intervals.\n    \n    bad_intervals = []\n    for d in D:\n        # Start of bad interval for Y: (A - d) % L\n        # End of bad interval for Y: (L - 1 - d) % L\n        start = (A - d) % L\n        end = (L - 1 - d) % L\n        \n        if start <= end:\n            bad_intervals.append((start, end))\n        else:\n            # Wraps around: [start, L-1] and [0, end]\n            bad_intervals.append((start, L - 1))\n            bad_intervals.append((0, end))\n            \n    if not bad_intervals:\n        # No bad intervals? This happens if A=L (B=0, but B>=1) or N=0.\n        # Constraints say B>=1, so bad intervals exist if N>=1.\n        # If N=0, trivially Yes? But N>=1.\n        pass\n\n    # Merge intervals\n    bad_intervals.sort()\n    \n    merged = []\n    if bad_intervals:\n        curr_start, curr_end = bad_intervals[0]\n        for next_start, next_end in bad_intervals[1:]:\n            if next_start <= curr_end + 1:\n                curr_end = max(curr_end, next_end)\n            else:\n                merged.append((curr_start, curr_end))\n                curr_start, curr_end = next_start, next_end\n        merged.append((curr_start, curr_end))\n        \n    # Check if union covers [0, L-1]\n    # Since it's a circle, we need to handle wrap-around of the merged intervals if necessary.\n    # However, our split handled wrapping by creating two intervals for one bad region.\n    # So the merged list contains intervals within [0, L-1].\n    # We just need to check if they cover [0, L-1] completely.\n    \n    total_covered_length = 0\n    for s, e in merged:\n        total_covered_length += (e - s + 1)\n        \n    if total_covered_length == L:\n        print(\"No\")\n    else:\n        print(\"Yes\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
