    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    \n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n        M = int(next(iterator))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    wheels = []\n    for i in range(N):\n        C_i = int(next(iterator))\n        P_i = int(next(iterator))\n        S_i = []\n        for j in range(P_i):\n            S_i.append(int(next(iterator)))\n        wheels.append((C_i, P_i, S_i))\n\n    # Precompute expected cost per point? No, we need dynamic programming.\n    # State: current_points (0 to M-1, since >= M is terminal)\n    # Value: min_expected_additional_cost to reach >= M points.\n    # Let dp[p] be the minimum expected cost to reach at least M points starting from p points.\n    # Base case: dp[p] = 0 for p >= M.\n    # We want dp[0].\n    \n    # For a state p < M, we can choose any wheel i.\n    # If we choose wheel i:\n    # Cost incurred immediately: C_i\n    # Next state distribution:\n    # For each outcome s in S_i, probability 1/P_i, new points = min(p + s, M) effectively? \n    # Actually, if p + s >= M, we stop. So the \"next state\" for DP purposes is M (or any value >= M).\n    # Let's define dp[p] for p in 0..M-1.\n    # dp[p] = min over i in 0..N-1 of { C_i + sum_{s in S_i} (1/P_i) * (0 if p+s >= M else dp[min(p+s, M)]) }\n    # Note: Since S_i are non-negative, p+s increases. If p+s >= M, the additional cost is 0.\n    \n    # Initialize dp array with infinity\n    dp = [float('inf')] * M\n    dp[M-1] = 0.0 # Wait, if we have M-1 points, and we play a wheel that gives 0 points, we stay at M-1?\n                   # But S_i sums > 0, so there is always some positive gain? \n                   # Constraints: sum(S_ij) > 0. This means not all S_ij are 0.\n                   # However, it is possible to get 0 points.\n                   # If we are at M-1, and we pick a wheel, we might get 0 points and stay at M-1.\n                   # This creates a system of linear equations if we have cycles of 0-cost transitions?\n                   # No, every play costs C_i > 0. So the expected cost is finite.\n    \n    # Let's refine the recurrence.\n    # dp[p] = min_i [ C_i + (1/P_i) * sum_{j=1}^{P_i} (0 if p + S_{i,j} >= M else dp[min(p + S_{i,j}, M-1)]) ]\n    # Wait, if p + S_{i,j} >= M, the process stops, so additional cost is 0.\n    # If p + S_{i,j} < M, we continue from state min(p + S_{i,j}, M-1).\n    # Since S_{i,j} >= 0, p + S_{i,j} >= p.\n    # If S_{i,j} == 0, then next state is p.\n    # This leads to: dp[p] = C_i + (1/P_i) * [ count_0 * dp[p] + sum_{s>0, p+s<M} dp[p+s] ]\n    # Rearranging for dp[p]:\n    # dp[p] * (1 - count_0 / P_i) = C_i + (1/P_i) * sum_{s>0, p+s<M} dp[p+s]\n    # dp[p] = (C_i + (1/P_i) * sum_{s>0, p+s<M} dp[p+s]) / (1 - count_0 / P_i)\n    # Denominator is (P_i - count_0) / P_i.\n    # So dp[p] = (C_i * P_i + sum_{s>0, p+s<M} dp[p+s]) / (P_i - count_0)\n    \n    # This formula holds for a fixed wheel i. We take the minimum over all wheels i.\n    \n    # Since the dependencies are only on states >= p (specifically p+s where s>=0),\n    # and for s=0 we handle it algebraically, for s>0, p+s > p.\n    # Thus, we can compute dp[p] from p = M-1 down to 0.\n    \n    # Precompute wheel statistics\n    wheel_stats = []\n    for i in range(N):\n        C_i, P_i, S_i = wheels[i]\n        count_zero = sum(1 for s in S_i if s == 0)\n        # If all outcomes are 0, this wheel is useless/infinite loop?\n        # Constraint: sum(S_ij) > 0, so not all are 0. Thus P_i - count_zero > 0.\n        non_zero_outcomes = [s for s in S_i if s > 0]\n        wheel_stats.append({\n            'C': C_i,\n            'P': P_i,\n            'count_zero': count_zero,\n            'non_zeros': non_zero_outcomes\n        })\n\n    # DP initialization\n    # dp[p] represents min expected cost to reach >= M points from p points.\n    # For p >= M, cost is 0.\n    # We only need dp[0]...dp[M-1].\n    \n    dp = [0.0] * M\n    \n    # Iterate backwards from M-1 to 0\n    for p in range(M - 1, -1, -1):\n        best_val = float('inf')\n        \n        for ws in wheel_stats:\n            C_i = ws['C']\n            P_i = ws['P']\n            count_zero = ws['count_zero']\n            non_zeros = ws['non_zeros']\n            \n            # Calculate numerator: C_i * P_i + sum(dp[min(p+s, M-1)] for s in non_zeros)\n            # Note: since s > 0, p+s > p.\n            # If p+s >= M, the term is 0 (already stopped).\n            # So we only sum dp[p+s] if p+s < M.\n            \n            sum_future_costs = 0.0\n            for s in non_zeros:\n                next_p = p + s\n                if next_p < M:\n                    sum_future_costs += dp[next_p]\n                # else: contributes 0\n            \n            numerator = C_i * P_i + sum_future_costs\n            denominator = P_i - count_zero\n            \n            val = numerator / denominator\n            if val < best_val:\n                best_val = val\n        \n        dp[p] = best_val\n\n    print(f\"{dp[0]:.15f}\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
