      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nsys.setrecursionlimit(2000000)\n\ndef solve():\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    H = int(data[0])\n    W = int(data[1])\n    grid = []\n    for i in range(H):\n        grid.append(data[2+i])\n        \n    MOD = 998244353\n    \n    # Directions for adjacency\n    dirs = [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]\n    \n    # Identify all cells\n    # '.' is red, '#' is green\n    # We need to count green connected components initially\n    # And then for each red cell, calculate the change in the number of components if it turns green.\n    \n    # Step 1: Label initial green connected components\n    comp_id = [[-1] * W for _ in range(H)]\n    current_comp = 0\n    num_components = 0\n    \n    def dfs(r, c, cid):\n        stack = [(r, c)]\n        comp_id[r][c] = cid\n        while stack:\n            cr, cc = stack.pop()\n            for dr, dc in dirs:\n                nr, nc = cr + dr, cc + dc\n                if 0 <= nr < H and 0 <= nc < W:\n                    if comp_id[nr][nc] == -1 and grid[nr][nc] == '#':\n                        comp_id[nr][nc] = cid\n                        stack.append((nr, nc))\n\n    for r in range(H):\n        for c in range(W):\n            if grid[r][c] == '#' and comp_id[r][c] == -1:\n                dfs(r, c, current_comp)\n                num_components += 1\n                current_comp += 1\n                \n    # Count total red cells\n    red_cells = []\n    total_red = 0\n    for r in range(H):\n        for c in range(W):\n            if grid[r][c] == '.':\n                red_cells.append((r, c))\n                total_red += 1\n                \n    # If no red cells, though constraints say at least one exists.\n    if total_red == 0:\n        # Should not happen based on constraints\n        print(0)\n        return\n\n    # For each red cell, if we turn it green:\n    # The new component count will be:\n    # num_components - (number of distinct neighbor components merged) + 1\n    # Wait, let's analyze carefully.\n    # When a red cell (r,c) becomes green, it forms a new potential component.\n    # It connects to up to 4 neighbors.\n    # Let N(r,c) be the set of adjacent green cells.\n    # Let C(N(r,c)) be the set of unique component IDs among those adjacent green cells.\n    # Let k = |C(N(r,c))|.\n    # \n    # Case 1: k == 0. The new cell is isolated.\n    #   New components = num_components + 1\n    # Case 2: k > 0. The new cell merges k existing components into one.\n    #   The k components become 1 component.\n    #   So we lose k-1 components.\n    #   New components = num_components - (k - 1) = num_components - k + 1\n    \n    # In both cases, the formula is:\n    # New components = num_components - k + 1\n    # Where k is the number of distinct adjacent green components.\n    # If k=0, num_components + 1. Formula holds: num - 0 + 1.\n    \n    # So for each red cell, we calculate k, the number of distinct green neighbor components.\n    # Then the contribution is (num_components - k + 1).\n    # Sum these contributions and divide by total_red.\n    \n    sum_new_components = 0\n    \n    for r, c in red_cells:\n        neighbor_comps = set()\n        for dr, dc in dirs:\n            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n            if 0 <= nr < H and 0 <= nc < W:\n                if grid[nr][nc] == '#':\n                    neighbor_comps.add(comp_id[nr][nc])\n        \n        k = len(neighbor_comps)\n        val = num_components - k + 1\n        sum_new_components += val\n        \n    # Expected value = sum_new_components / total_red\n    # We need to print sum_new_components * mod_inverse(total_red) % MOD\n    \n    def mod_inverse(a, m):\n        return pow(a, m - 2, m)\n    \n    ans = (sum_new_components % MOD * mod_inverse(total_red, MOD)) % MOD\n    print(ans)\n\nsolve()\n```",
