      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef smallestString(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the lexicographically smallest string obtainable by applying\n    the operation exactly once on any substring of s.\n    \n    Operation: Select a non-empty substring, replace each character with\n    the previous character in the alphabet ('a' becomes 'z').\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    chars = list(s)\n    \n    # Strategy:\n    # We want to make the string lexicographically smaller.\n    # Decreasing a character makes it smaller, unless it's 'a' which wraps to 'z' (larger).\n    # So we should apply the operation on a substring of characters that are NOT 'a'.\n    # To get the lexicographically smallest result, we should start from the first non-'a' character\n    # and continue replacing until we hit an 'a' or the end of the string.\n    # Why? Because changing earlier characters has more impact on lexicographical order.\n    # And we want to change as many leading non-'a' characters as possible to decrease them.\n    # However, we must perform the operation EXACTLY once on a NON-EMPTY substring.\n    \n    # Find the first index where s[i] != 'a'\n    i = 0\n    while i < n and s[i] == 'a':\n        i += 1\n    \n    # If all characters are 'a', we must pick some substring. \n    # Picking any single 'a' turns it into 'z', which is bad.\n    # But we have no choice. To minimize damage, we should pick the LAST character (or shortest substring possible)\n    # because changing a later character has less impact. Actually, since all are 'a', any operation will turn some 'a's to 'z's.\n    # Lexicographically, we want the 'z's to appear as late as possible.\n    # So if all are 'a', we change the last character to 'z'.\n    if i == n:\n        chars[n - 1] = 'z'\n        return \"\".join(chars)\n    \n    # Now we start from index i (first non-'a')\n    # We continue replacing characters as long as they are not 'a'.\n    j = i\n    while j < n and chars[j] != 'a':\n        # Replace char with previous\n        # ord('a') is 97. So prev = chr(ord(c) - 1)\n        chars[j] = chr(ord(chars[j]) - 1)\n        j += 1\n        \n    return \"\".join(chars)\n```",
