      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef countServers(n: int, logs: list[list[int]], x: int, queries: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns an array where each element is the number of servers that did not receive\n    any requests during the time interval [query_i - x, query_i].\n    \"\"\"\n    # Sort logs by time\n    logs.sort(key=lambda log: log[1])\n    \n    # We need to answer queries. To do this efficiently, we can sort the queries\n    # and use a sliding window approach on the sorted logs.\n    \n    # Create a list of (query_time, original_index) and sort by query_time\n    sorted_queries = sorted([(q, i) for i, q in enumerate(queries)])\n    \n    # Initialize result array\n    res = [0] * len(queries)\n    \n    # Sliding window variables\n    left = 0\n    right = 0\n    # Active set: servers that have received a request in the current window\n    # We use a dictionary to count how many times each server appears in the current window\n    active_servers = {}\n    \n    # Process each query in sorted order\n    for q_time, q_idx in sorted_queries:\n        start_time = q_time - x\n        end_time = q_time\n        \n        # Expand right pointer to include all logs with time <= end_time\n        while right < len(logs) and logs[right][1] <= end_time:\n            server_id = logs[right][0]\n            active_servers[server_id] = active_servers.get(server_id, 0) + 1\n            right += 1\n        \n        # Shrink left pointer to exclude logs with time < start_time\n        while left < len(logs) and logs[left][1] < start_time:\n            server_id = logs[left][0]\n            active_servers[server_id] -= 1\n            if active_servers[server_id] == 0:\n                del active_servers[server_id]\n            left += 1\n        \n        # Number of servers that received at least one request in the interval\n        num_active = len(active_servers)\n        # Number of servers that did NOT receive any request\n        res[q_idx] = n - num_active\n    \n    return res\n```",
