    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef numberOfRightTriangles(grid: list[list[int]]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculate the number of right triangles formed by 1s in the grid.\n    \n    A right triangle is defined by three 1s where one is at the intersection\n    of the row and column of the other two. Specifically, if we pick a 'corner'\n    cell (r, c) which has a 1, then we need one other 1 in the same row (r, c')\n    and one other 1 in the same column (r', c).\n    \n    For a fixed corner cell (r, c) with value 1:\n    - Let row_count[r] be the number of 1s in row r.\n    - Let col_count[c] be the number of 1s in column c.\n    - The number of ways to choose the other two points is (row_count[r] - 1) * (col_count[c] - 1).\n    \n    Sum this over all cells (r, c) where grid[r][c] == 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0]) if rows > 0 else 0\n    \n    # Precompute the number of 1s in each row and each column\n    row_counts = [sum(row) for row in grid]\n    col_counts = [0] * cols\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if grid[r][c] == 1:\n                col_counts[c] += 1\n                \n    total_triangles = 0\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if grid[r][c] == 1:\n                # Number of other 1s in the same row\n                other_in_row = row_counts[r] - 1\n                # Number of other 1s in the same column\n                other_in_col = col_counts[c] - 1\n                total_triangles += other_in_row * other_in_col\n                \n    return total_triangles\n```",
