      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef countBeautifulIntegers(low: int, high: int, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the number of beautiful integers in the range [low, high].\n    A number is beautiful if:\n    1. The count of even digits equals the count of odd digits.\n    2. The number is divisible by k.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    def is_beautiful(n: int) -> bool:\n        s = str(n)\n        even_count = 0\n        odd_count = 0\n        for ch in s:\n            d = int(ch)\n            if d % 2 == 0:\n                even_count += 1\n            else:\n                odd_count += 1\n        return even_count == odd_count and n % k == 0\n\n    # Since high can be up to 10^9, we cannot iterate through all numbers directly.\n    # However, note that for a number to have equal even and odd digits, it must have an even number of digits.\n    # Also, the total number of digits must be even, say 2m, then m even and m odd.\n    # Max digits is 10 (since 10^9 has 10 digits, but 10^9 itself is 1 followed by 9 zeros -> 1 odd, 9 even -> not beautiful).\n    # Actually, 10^9 is the upper bound. Numbers with 10 digits: 1,000,000,000 to 1,000,000,000. Only one number.\n    # Let's check: 10^9 has digits: 1 (odd) and nine 0s (even). So 1 odd, 9 even -> not beautiful.\n    # So effectively, we need to consider numbers with 2, 4, 6, 8 digits. 10-digit numbers only 10^9 which is not beautiful.\n    \n    # But wait, low and high can be small. We need a digit DP approach.\n    \n    # Digit DP state:\n    # pos: current digit position (from left, 0-indexed)\n    # tight: whether we are restricted by the prefix of the target number\n    # even_cnt: count of even digits placed so far\n    # odd_cnt: count of odd digits placed so far\n    # remainder: current number modulo k\n    \n    # We will compute f(high) - f(low - 1)\n    \n    def solve(n: int) -> int:\n        if n < 0:\n            return 0\n        if n == 0:\n            return 0\n        \n        digits = list(