      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minimumTimeToInitialState(word: str, k: int) -> int:\n    n = len(word)\n    # We need to find the smallest t > 0 such that after t operations,\n    # the remaining suffix of the original word matches the prefix of the original word\n    # of length n - t*k.\n    # After t seconds, we have removed t*k characters from the front.\n    # The remaining part is word[t*k:].\n    # For the string to revert to its initial state, the added characters must complete\n    # the string back to the original. This means the suffix word[t*k:] must match\n    # the prefix of word of the same length.\n    \n    # Let's check t = 1, 2, ...\n    # At time t, the number of characters removed is t * k.\n    # If t * k >= n, then the entire string has been removed/shifted out, and we can\n    # always reconstruct the original string by adding the correct characters.\n    # So the maximum time needed is ceil(n / k). But we want the minimum t > 0.\n    \n    # For a given t, the condition is:\n    # word[t*k : n] == word[0 : n - t*k]\n    \n    t = 1\n    while True:\n        shifted = t * k\n        if shifted >= n:\n            return t\n        # Check if the suffix starting at shifted matches the prefix of length n - shifted\n        suffix = word[shifted:]\n        prefix_len = n - shifted\n        prefix = word[:prefix_len]\n        if suffix == prefix:\n            return t\n        t += 1\n```",
