      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    \n    try:\n        W = int(next(iterator))\n        H = int(next(iterator))\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n        \n        strawberries = []\n        for _ in range(N):\n            p = int(next(iterator))\n            q = int(next(iterator))\n            strawberries.append((p, q))\n            \n        A = int(next(iterator))\n        cuts_x = []\n        for _ in range(A):\n            cuts_x.append(int(next(iterator)))\n            \n        B = int(next(iterator))\n        cuts_y = []\n        for _ in range(B):\n            cuts_y.append(int(next(iterator)))\n            \n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    # Sort cuts to ensure they are ordered, though problem guarantees sorted order\n    cuts_x.sort()\n    cuts_y.sort()\n    \n    # Define the vertical boundaries of the columns\n    # Columns are intervals between x=0, cuts_x[0], cuts_x[1]... cuts_x[A-1], x=W\n    # Similarly for rows\n    \n    # We need to count strawberries in each rectangle formed by grid of cuts.\n    # The rectangles are defined by x-intervals [L_x, R_x) and y-intervals [L_y, R_y).\n    # Specifically:\n    # X-intervals: [0, cuts_x[0]), [cuts_x[0], cuts_x[1]), ..., [cuts_x[A-1], W)\n    # Y-intervals: [0, cuts_y[0]), [cuts_y[0], cuts_y[1]), ..., [cuts_y[B-1], H)\n    \n    # Since N and A, B can be up to 2*10^5, the grid size can be up to (A+1)*(B+1) ~ 4*10^10, which is too large to iterate over all cells.\n    # However, most cells will be empty. We only care about cells that contain at least one strawberry.\n    # But we need the min and max number of strawberries. Min could be 0. Max is the max in any non-empty cell.\n    \n    # Strategy:\n    # 1. Determine which column interval and row interval each strawberry falls into.\n    # 2. Count strawberries per (col_idx, row_idx) pair.\n    # 3. Find min and max counts.\n    #    - If there are any empty cells (which is likely if total_cells > N), min is 0.\n    #    - Max is the maximum count found.\n    \n    # To efficiently find the interval index for a coordinate, we can use binary search (bisect).\n    \n    import bisect\n    \n    # Precompute the boundaries for binary search\n    # For x-cuts: \n    # The intervals are defined by the cut points.\n    # bisect_right(cuts_x, p) gives the index i such that all cuts_x[:i] <= p.\n    # Let's verify:\n    # cuts_x = [2, 5], W=7. Intervals: [0,2), [2,5), [5,7).\n    # If p=1: bisect_right([2,5], 1) -> 0. Index 0 corresponds to [0,2). Correct.\n    # If p=3: bisect_right([2,5], 3) -> 1. Index 1 corresponds to [2,5). Correct.\n    # If p=6: bisect_right([2,5], 6) -> 2. Index 2 corresponds to [5,7). Correct.\n    \n    # So, col_idx = bisect.bisect_right(cuts_x, p)\n    # row_idx = bisect.bisect_right(cuts_y, q)\n    \n    # We will store counts in a dictionary or hash map keyed by (col_idx, row_idx)\n    counts = {}\n    \n    max_col_idx = len(cuts_x) # Indices 0 to A\n    max_row_idx = len(cuts_y) # Indices 0 to B\n    \n    for p, q in strawberries:\n        c_idx = bisect.bisect_right(cuts_x, p)\n        r_idx = bisect.bisect_right(cuts_y, q)\n        \n        key = (c_idx, r_idx)\n        counts[key] = counts.get(key, 0) + 1\n        \n    if not counts:\n        # No strawberries? Constraints say N >= 1, so this shouldn't happen based on constraints.\n        # But if it did, min=0, max=0.\n        print(\"0 0\")\n        return\n\n    # Calculate total number of pieces\n    total_pieces = (len(cuts_x) + 1) * (len(cuts_y) + 1)\n    num_non_empty = len(counts)\n    \n    # Minimum possible number of strawberries\n    # If there exists any piece with 0 strawberries, min is 0.\n    # This happens if total_pieces > num_non_empty.\n    if total_pieces > num_non_empty:\n        min_val = 0\n    else:\n        # All pieces have at least one strawberry.\n        min_val = min(counts.values())\n        \n    max_val = max(counts.values())\n    \n    print(f\"{min_val} {max_val}\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
