    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom bisect import bisect_left, insort\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    intervals = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        t = int(next(iterator))\n        d = int(next(iterator))\n        # The product is available from time t to time t + d.\n        # It can be printed at any time point in [t, t + d].\n        # Let start = t, end = t + d.\n        intervals.append((t, t + d))\n    \n    # We want to select the maximum number of intervals such that we can pick a point p_i in each interval [s_i, e_i]\n    # where |p_i - p_j| >= 1 for all selected distinct i, j.\n    # This is equivalent to finding the maximum size subset of intervals that can be \"stabbed\" by points with minimum separation 1.\n    \n    # Strategy: Greedy.\n    # Sort intervals by their end times.\n    # Iterate through sorted intervals and pick the earliest possible valid time point for the current interval.\n    \n    intervals.sort(key=lambda x: x[1])\n    \n    last_print_time = -float('inf')\n    count = 0\n    \n    for s, e in intervals:\n        # We need to pick a time p such that s <= p <= e and p >= last_print_time + 1 (if last_print_time was used).\n        # To maximize future chances, we should pick the smallest valid p.\n        # The smallest valid p is max(s, last_print_time + 1).\n        \n        candidate = max(s, last_print_time + 1)\n        \n        if candidate <= e:\n            count += 1\n            last_print_time = candidate\n            \n    print(count)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    solve()\n```",
