      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom itertools import permutations\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read input\n    grid = []\n    try:\n        lines = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    except Exception:\n        return\n    \n    if not lines:\n        return\n\n    # Parse the 3x3 grid\n    c = []\n    idx = 0\n    for i in range(3):\n        row = []\n        for j in range(3):\n            row.append(int(lines[idx]))\n            idx += 1\n        c.append(row)\n    \n    # Flatten the grid into a list of values with their positions\n    # Positions: (row, col) 0-indexed\n    cells = []\n    for r in range(3):\n        for col in range(3):\n            cells.append((c[r][col], r, col))\n            \n    # There are 9 cells total. Total permutations = 9!\n    total_permutations = 9 * 8 * 7 * 6 * 5 * 4 * 3 * 2 * 1\n    \n    # Define the 8 lines (rows, columns, diagonals)\n    # Each line is a list of 3 indices in the 'cells' list (0 to 8)\n    # Mapping from (r, col) to index in flattened list: r*3 + col\n    \n    lines_indices = [\n        # Rows\n        [0, 1, 2],   # Row 0\n        [3, 4, 5],   # Row 1\n        [6, 7, 8],   # Row 2\n        # Columns\n        [0, 3, 6],   # Col 0\n        [1, 4, 7],   # Col 1\n        [2, 5, 8],   # Col 2\n        # Diagonals\n        [0, 4, 8],   # Main diagonal\n        [2, 4, 6]    # Anti-diagonal\n    ]\n    \n    # Values in each cell\n    values = [cell[0] for cell in cells]\n    \n    # We need to count the number of permutations where Takahashi never gets disappointed.\n    # He gets disappointed if there is ANY line such that the first two squares he sees in that line have the same number, \n    # but the third square has a different number.\n    \n    # Since 9! = 362,880, we can iterate through all permutations.\n    \n    count_safe = 0\n    \n    # Generate all permutations of indices 0..8\n    # Using itertools.permutations\n    for p in permutations(range(9)):\n        # p is a tuple of length 9, representing the order in which cells are visited.\n        # p[k] is the index of the cell visited at step k+1 (1-based step).\n        \n        # For each line, check if it causes disappointment.\n        # A line consists of 3 cells. Let the cells be A, B, C.\n        # Let pos[X] be the position (index in p) where cell X appears.\n        # Disappointment occurs for line (A, B, C) if:\n        # 1. The first two seen among {A, B, C} have the same value.\n        # 2. The last one seen among {A, B, C} has a different value.\n        \n        # More precisely, let's look at the sequence of values for the line based on visit order.\n        # Suppose the visit times for A, B, C are t_A, t_B, t_C.\n        # Sort the cells by time: let them be L1, L2, L3 such that t_L1 < t_L2 < t_L3.\n        # If values[L1] == values[L2] and values[L2] != values[L3], then disappointment.\n        \n        is_disappointed = False\n        \n        for line in lines_indices:\n            # Get the visit times for the three cells in this line\n            times = [(p.index(cell_idx), values[cell_idx]) for cell_idx in line]\n            # Sort by time\n            times.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\n            \n            v1 = times[0][1]\n            v2 = times[1][1]\n            v3 = times[2][1]\n            \n            if v1 == v2 and v2 != v3:\n                is_disappointed = True\n                break\n        \n        if not is_disappointed:\n            count_safe += 1\n            \n    probability = count_safe / total_permutations\n    print(f\"{probability:.20f}\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
