    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere is an N \\times M grid and a player standing on it.\r\nLet (i,j) denote the square at the i-th row from the top and j-th column from the left of this grid.\r\nEach square of this grid is ice or rock, which is represented by N strings S_1,S_2,\\dots,S_N of length M as follows:\n\n- if the j-th character of S_i is ., square (i,j) is ice;\n- if the j-th character of S_i is #, square (i,j) is rock.\n\nThe outer periphery of this grid (all squares in the 1-st row, N-th row, 1-st column, M-th column) is rock.\nInitially, the player rests on the square (2,2), which is ice.\r\nThe player can make the following move zero or more times.\n\n- First, specify the direction of movement: up, down, left, or right.\n- Then, keep moving in that direction until the player bumps against a rock. Formally, keep doing the following:\n- if the next square in the direction of movement is ice, go to that square and keep moving;\n- if the next square in the direction of movement is rock, stay in the current square and stop moving.\n\n\n\nFind the number of ice squares the player can touch (pass or rest on).\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\r\nS_1\r\nS_2\r\n\\vdots\r\nS_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer as an integer.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 3 \\le N,M \\le 200\n- S_i is a string of length M consisting of # and ..\n- Square (i, j) is rock if i=1, i=N, j=1, or j=M.\n- Square (2,2) is ice.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n6 6\r\n######\r\n#....#\r\n#.#..#\r\n#..#.#\r\n#....#\r\n######\n\nSample Output 1\n\n12\r\n\nFor instance, the player can rest on (5,5) by moving as follows:\n\n- (2,2) \\rightarrow (5,2) \\rightarrow (5,5).\n\nThe player can pass (2,4) by moving as follows:\n\n- (2,2) \\rightarrow (2,5), passing (2,4) in the process.\n\nThe player cannot pass or rest on (3,4).\n\nSample Input 2\n\n21 25\r\n#########################\r\n#..............###...####\r\n#..............#..#...###\r\n#........###...#...#...##\r\n#........#..#..#........#\r\n#...##...#..#..#...#....#\r\n#..#..#..###...#..#.....#\r\n#..#..#..#..#..###......#\r\n#..####..#..#...........#\r\n#..#..#..###............#\r\n#..#..#.................#\r\n#........##.............#\r\n#.......#..#............#\r\n#..........#....#.......#\r\n#........###...##....#..#\r\n#..........#..#.#...##..#\r\n#.......#..#....#..#.#..#\r\n##.......##.....#....#..#\r\n###.............#....#..#\r\n####.................#..#\r\n#########################\n\nSample Output 2\n\n215",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    N = int(input_data[0])\n    M = int(input_data[1])\n    S = input_data[2:2+N]\n    \n    # Grid representation\n    # S[i][j] is the character at row i, col j (0-indexed)\n    \n    # The player starts at (2,2) which is (1,1) in 0-indexed.\n    # We need to find all reachable ice squares.\n    # Moves are sliding in one of 4 directions until hitting a rock.\n    # This is essentially a graph problem where nodes are ice squares.\n    # However, since N, M <= 200, we can use BFS/DFS.\n    # But the state space is N*M. For each state, there are 4 possible moves.\n    # Each move takes O(N) or O(M) time to simulate.\n    # Total complexity O(N*M*(N+M)) which is roughly 200*200*400 = 16,000,000.\n    # This might be a bit slow for Python if not optimized, but let's try.\n    # Actually, we can optimize by precomputing the stopping positions.\n    \n    # Let's use a set for visited squares.\n    # Queue for BFS: list of (r, c)\n    \n    visited = [[False for _ in range(M)] for _ in range(N)]\n    \n    start_r, start_c = 1, 1\n    queue = [(start_r, start_c)]\n    visited[start_r][start_c] = True\n    count = 0\n    \n    # Directions: up, down, left, right\n    directions = [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)]\n    \n    while queue:\n        r, c = queue.pop(0)\n        count += 1\n        \n        for dr, dc in directions:\n            # Slide in direction (dr, dc)\n            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n            while 0 <= nr < N and 0 <= nc < M and S[nr][nc] == '.':\n                if not visited[nr][nc]:\n                    visited[nr][nc] = True\n                    queue.append((nr, nc))\n                nr += dr\n                nc += dc\n            # After the loop, (nr, nc) is either out of bounds or a rock.\n            # The last valid ice square was (nr-dr, nc-dc).\n            # But wait, the rule says \"if the next square ... is rock, stay in the current square\".\n            # So the player stops at the last ice square before the rock.\n            # In my while loop, I am visiting every ice square along the path.\n            # Is that correct? \n            # \"Find the number of ice squares the player can touch (pass or rest on).\"\n            # Yes, passing through counts. So all ice squares on the slide path are touched.\n            \n            # However, notice that in the while loop above, I am adding to queue only if not visited.\n            # But I should also mark them as visited.\n            # Let's re-check the logic.\n            # When sliding from (r,c) in direction (dr,dc):\n            # The player moves step by step. Each intermediate square is 'touched'.\n            # So yes, all those squares should be marked visited.\n            \n            # One optimization: if we have already visited the entire slide path from some other node,\n            # we don't need to process it again? No, because we might reach new nodes from these nodes.\n            # But actually, if a node is visited, it means it has been processed as a starting point for a slide?\n            # Not necessarily. In standard BFS, when we pop a node, we process its neighbors.\n            # Here, the \"neighbors\" are all nodes reachable by a single slide.\n            # And all nodes on the slide path are also reachable.\n            \n            # Let's refine the BFS.\n            # When we are at (r,c), we slide in 4 directions.\n            # For each direction, we iterate through the ice squares.\n            # Each such square is a potential new state to explore from.\n            # So we add it to the queue if not visited.\n            \n            # The code above does exactly that.\n            \n    print(count)\n\nsolve()\n```",
