    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\n# Set recursion limit just in case, though we will use iterative DP\nsys.setrecursionlimit(2000)\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    \n    if not input_data:\n        return\n\n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    \n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n        A = []\n        for _ in range(N):\n            A.append(int(next(iterator)))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    MOD = 998244353\n\n    # We need to calculate the probability that there exists a subset of dice whose values sum to 10.\n    # It is easier to calculate the complement: the probability that NO subset sums to 10.\n    # Then P(answer) = 1 - P(no subset sums to 10).\n    \n    # Since the target sum is small (10), we can use Dynamic Programming.\n    # However, the dice values A_i can be large. But note: if a die shows a value v > 10, \n    # it cannot contribute to a sum of exactly 10 unless combined with negative numbers (impossible here) \n    # or if we just ignore it for the purpose of forming the sum 10? \n    # Wait, \"choose some ... dice\". If a die shows 11, it can't be part of a sum of 10 because all values are >= 1.\n    # So effectively, for the subset sum problem targeting 10, any die showing a value > 10 acts like a value that can never be used to form 10.\n    # But wait, if a die shows 1, 2, ..., 9, 10, it might be used.\n    # Specifically, a die outcome v contributes to the subset sum if v <= 10.\n    # If v > 10, it can never be part of a subset summing to 10.\n    \n    # Let's define the state.\n    # We process dice one by one.\n    # dp[i] = probability that a subset of the processed dice sums to exactly i.\n    # But we need to know if sum 10 is *reachable*.\n    # Actually, standard subset sum DP tracks reachability or counts. Here we have probabilities.\n    \n    # Let dp[s] be the probability that the sum s can be formed by a subset of the dice processed so far.\n    # This is tricky because events are not independent in a simple way for \"existence\".\n    # Better approach:\n    # Calculate the total number of outcomes where NO subset sums to 10.\n    # Total outcomes = Product(A_i).\n    # We want to count outcomes where 10 is NOT a subset sum.\n    \n    # Let's use generating functions or DP on the counts.\n    # Since N <= 100 and Target = 10, we can maintain a DP table.\n    # dp[s] could represent the number of ways (weighted by probability or just count) to have a set of reachable subset sums?\n    # No, the state needs to capture which sums are reachable.\n    # With target 10, the reachable sums are subsets of {1, 2, ..., 10}. There are 2^10 = 1024 possible masks.\n    # This is small enough!\n    \n    # Let mask be a bitmask where the j-th bit is 1 if sum j is achievable by a subset of the current dice.\n    # We start with mask = 1 (only sum 0 is achievable, bit 0 set). Note: usually bit 0 corresponds to sum 0.\n    # We want to avoid the state where bit 10 is set.\n    \n    # Transitions:\n    # For a new die with outcome v:\n    # If v > 10, the mask doesn't change (this die can't help form any new sum <= 10).\n    # If v <= 10, the new mask m' is derived from old mask m:\n    # For every bit j set in m, if j + v <= 10, then bit j+v is also set in m'.\n    # So m' = m | (m << v) & MASK_LIMIT\n    \n    # We need to sum over all possible outcomes of all dice.\n    # Since the outcomes are independent, we can iterate through dice and update the distribution of masks.\n    \n    # dp[mask] = number of ways (count) to reach this mask state after processing some dice.\n    # Initially, dp[1] = 1 (mask 1 means sum 0 is reachable, i.e., empty set). All other 0.\n    # Total ways initially 1.\n    \n    # However, A_i is large, so we can't iterate outcomes one by one.\n    # For each die i, it has outcomes 1..A_i.\n    # Outcomes > 10 act as identity (mask unchanged).\n    # Outcomes 1..10 change the mask.\n    \n    # Let's refine the DP state.\n    # dp[mask] stores the number of combinations of previous dice outcomes that result in the subset-sum-reachability 'mask'.\n    # When processing die i with limit A_i:\n    #   For each mask in current dp:\n    #     Count how many outcomes v in [1, A_i] lead to a new mask m'.\n    #     Add dp[mask] * count to new_dp[m'].\n    \n    # Optimization:\n    # Instead of iterating all masks for each outcome, notice that for a fixed mask and fixed v, the transition is deterministic.\n    # Also, outcomes v > 10 don't change the mask.\n    # Let K = 10.\n    # Masks are integers from 0 to 2^(K+1)-1? No, sums are 0..10. So bits 0..10. Mask size 11 bits. 2^11 = 2048 states.\n    # This is very small.\n    \n    # Steps:\n    # 1. Initialize dp array of size 2048 with 0. dp[1] = 1. (Bit 0 set for sum 0).\n    # 2. For each die A_i:\n    #    new_dp = [0] * 2048\n    #    Determine transitions for v = 1 to min(A_i, 10).\n    #    Determine \"identity\" count for v > 10 (if A_i > 10).\n    #    \n    #    For each mask m in 0..2047:\n    #       if dp[m] == 0: continue\n    #       \n    #       # Case 1: Die shows v > 10 (if applicable)\n    #       # This doesn't change the mask.\n    #       if A_i > 10:\n    #           count_identity = A_i - 10\n    #           new_dp[m] = (new_dp[m] + dp[m] * count_identity) % MOD\n    #\n    #       # Case 2: Die shows v in 1..min(A_i, 10)\n    #       limit_v = min(A_i, 10)\n    #       for v in range(1, limit_v + 1):\n    #           # Calculate new mask m'\n    #           # If bit j is set in m, then bit j+v is set in m', provided j+v <= 10.\n    #           # m' = m | (m << v)\n    #           # But we only care about bits 0..10.\n    #           shifted = (m << v)\n    #           # Mask out bits > 10. Bits are 0-indexed. Sum 10 is bit 10.\n    #           # We want to keep bits 0..10.\n    #           # Full mask of 11 bits is (1<<11) - 1 = 2047.\n    #           m_new = (m | shifted) & 2047\n    #           \n    #           new_dp[m_new] = (new_dp[m_new] + dp[m]) % MOD\n    #\n    #    dp = new_dp\n    \n    # 3. After processing all dice, sum up dp[m] for all masks m where bit 10 is NOT set.\n    #    These are the \"bad\" outcomes (sum 10 not reachable).\n    #    Let bad_ways = sum(dp[m] for m in 0..2047 if not (m & 1024)).\n    #    Total_ways = Product(A_i) % MOD.\n    #    Prob_bad = bad_ways * mod_inverse(Total_ways) % MOD.\n    #    Ans = (1 - Prob_bad) % MOD.\n\n    # Implementation details:\n    # Modulo arithmetic for multiplication and addition.\n    # Modular inverse using Fermat's Little Theorem since MOD is prime.\n\n    def mod_pow(base, exp, mod):\n        res = 1\n        base %= mod\n        while exp > 0:\n            if exp % 2 == 1:\n                res = (res * base) % mod\n            base = (base * base) % mod\n            exp //= 2\n        return res\n\n    def mod_inv(n, mod):\n        return mod_pow(n, mod - 2, mod)\n\n    # Initial DP\n    # Mask represents reachable sums. Bit k is 1 if sum k is reachable.\n    # Initially, only sum 0 is reachable (empty subset). So mask = 1 (binary ...001).\n    num_states = 1 << 11  # 2048\n    dp = [0] * num_states\n    dp[1] = 1\n\n    total_ways = 1\n\n    for val in A:\n        new_dp = [0] * num_states\n        \n        # Calculate identity multiplier for outcomes > 10\n        # If val > 10, outcomes 11, 12, ..., val do not change the mask.\n        # There are max(0, val - 10) such outcomes.\n        identity_count = max(0, val - 10)\n        \n        # Max v that affects mask is min(val, 10)\n        max_v = min(val, 10)\n        \n        # Precompute transitions for each v in 1..max_v to optimize inner loop?\n        # Given small state space and small max_v, direct computation is fine.\n        # State space 2048. Val loop N=100. Inner loop v=10.\n        # Operations: 100 * 2048 * 10 approx 2 million. Very fast.\n        \n        for m in range(num_states):\n            if dp[m] == 0:\n                continue\n            \n            current_prob_ways = dp[m]\n            \n            # Add contribution from identity outcomes (v > 10)\n            if identity_count > 0:\n                new_dp[m] = (new_dp[m] + current_prob_ways * identity_count) % MOD\n            \n            # Add contributions from v in 1..max_v\n            for v in range(1, max_v + 1):\n                # New mask calculation\n                # m has bits set for reachable sums.\n                # Adding v allows reaching sum s+v if s was reachable.\n                # Shift left by v.\n                shifted = m << v\n                # Keep only bits 0..10\n                m_new = shifted & 2047\n                # Union with original mask (old sums are still reachable)\n                m_new |= m\n                \n                new_dp[m_new] = (new_dp[m_new] + current_prob_ways) % MOD\n        \n        dp = new_dp\n        total_ways = (total_ways * val) % MOD\n\n    # Sum bad ways: masks where bit 10 is 0\n    # Bit 10 corresponds to value 1024 (1 << 10)\n    bad_ways = 0\n    for m in range(num_states):\n        if not (m & 1024):\n            bad_ways = (bad_ways + dp[m]) % MOD\n\n    # Probability that sum 10 is NOT reachable\n    prob_bad = (bad_ways * mod_inv(total_ways, MOD)) % MOD\n    \n    # Probability that sum 10 IS reachable\n    ans = (1 - prob_bad + MOD) % MOD\n    \n    print(ans)\n\nsolve()\n```",
