ero_idx = 1.\n    # Part 1: i in [1, 1]. j-last_zero = 1-1=0 (even). contrib1 = 1.\n    # Part 2: i in [2, 1] -> empty. L=0. contrib2 = 0.\n    # Sum += 1. Total = 1.\n    # Check f(1,1)=0? Wait.\n    # My logic: val(i, j) = 1 if (j - K_j) even.\n    # For i=1, j=1, K_j=1. j-K_j=0 even. So val(1,1) should be 1?\n    # But sample says f(1,1)=0.\n    # Why?\n    # f(i, i) = A[i].\n    # If A[i]=0, f(i,i)=0.\n    # My derivation: \"If there is a 0 at position k... val(i, k) = 1\".\n    # This assumes the operation NAND with 0 makes it 1.\n    # f(i, i) = A[i].\n    # f(i, i+1) = A[i] NAND A[i+1].\n    # If A[i]=0, A[i+1]=0. f(i,i)=0. f(i,i+1) = 0 NAND 0 = 1.\n    # In my logic, for j=i+1, last_zero is i+1.\n    # For i, last_zero is i+1.\n    # val(i, i+1) depends on last_zero i+1.\n    # j - K_j = (i+1) - (i+1) = 0 even. So val=1. Correct.\n    # But for j=i, if A[i]=0, last_zero is i.\n    # val(i, i) should be A[i]=0.\n    # My formula says: if last_zero exists, for i <= last_zero, val=1 if (j-last_zero) even.\n    # Here i = last_zero = j. j-j=0 even. Says 1. Contradiction.\n    \n    # The issue is the base case.\n    # The rule \"encounter 0 -> reset to 1\" applies when we perform NAND.\n    # f(i, i) = A[i].\n    # f(i, i+1) = f(i, i) NAND A[i+1].\n    # If A[i+1]=0, f(i, i+1) = 1.\n    # So the \"reset to 1\" happens at the step *after* seeing the 0.\n    # The value AT the 0 position is just A[pos].\n    # So, if the rightmost 0 is at K_j, then for i = K_j, val(K_j, j) is NOT determined by the reset rule alone.\n    # It is determined by the chain from K_j to j.\n    # But wait, if i = K_j, then the substring starts with 0.\n    # val(K_j, K_j) = 0.\n    # Then we apply flips for 1s after K_j.\n    # So val(K_j, j) = 0 flipped (j - K_j) times.\n    # If (j - K_j) is even, val = 0.\n    # If (j - K_j) is odd, val = 1.\n    \n    # For i < K_j, the rightmost 0 is still K_j.\n    # val(i, K_j) = 1 (because A[K_j]=0 resets the previous chain to 1).\n    # Then val