      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Increase recursion depth just in case, though we try to avoid deep recursion\n    sys.setrecursionlimit(10**6)\n    \n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    iterator = iter(data)\n    \n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n        Q = int(next(iterator))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    # We need to efficiently track:\n    # 1. The set of isolated vertices.\n    # 2. The edges connected to each vertex, to support removal.\n    \n    # Let's maintain:\n    # - degree[v]: number of edges connected to vertex v.\n    # - adj[v]: a list (or set) of neighbors of v. Since we need to remove all edges incident to v,\n    #   storing neighbors is useful. However, removing an edge from u's perspective also requires updating u's neighbor list.\n    #   Using sets for adjacency lists allows O(1) average time for removals, but iterating might be costly if we don't manage carefully.\n    #   Actually, query type 2 removes ALL edges incident to v. So we can iterate over v's current neighbors, remove v from their neighbor sets, and clear v's set.\n    \n    # To count isolated vertices quickly:\n    # An isolated vertex has degree 0.\n    # We can maintain a counter `isolated_count`.\n    # Initially, all N vertices are isolated.\n    \n    adj = [set() for _ in range(N + 1)]\n    degree = [0] * (N + 1)\n    isolated_count = N\n    \n    output = []\n    \n    for _ in range(Q):\n        q_type = int(next(iterator))\n        \n        if q_type == 1:\n            u = int(next(iterator))\n            v = int(next(iterator))\n            \n            # Add edge u-v\n            # Before adding, check if they were isolated. If so, decrement isolated_count.\n            \n            # Update u\n            if degree[u] == 0:\n                isolated_count -= 1\n            adj[u].add(v)\n            degree[u] += 1\n            \n            # Update v\n            if degree[v] == 0:\n                isolated_count -= 1\n            adj[v].add(u)\n            degree[v] += 1\n            \n        elif q_type == 2:\n            v = int(next(iterator))\n            \n            # Remove all edges incident to v\n            # Get current neighbors\n            neighbors = list(adj[v])\n            \n            # If v was not isolated, it will become isolated unless it has no neighbors left (which is true here).\n            # But wait, we are removing ALL edges. So v's degree becomes 0.\n            # If degree[v] > 0, it means v was connected to someone, so it wasn't isolated.\n            # After removal, it becomes isolated. So if it wasn't isolated before, we increment isolated_count.\n            \n            if degree[v] > 0:\n                isolated_count += 1\n            \n            # Remove v from each neighbor's adjacency set\n            for u in neighbors:\n                # Remove edge u-v\n                # Check if u was isolated before? No, u is connected to v, so degree[u] >= 1.\n                # We need to decrement degree[u]. If degree[u] becomes 0, then u becomes isolated.\n                \n                # Remove v from u's adj\n                if v in adj[u]:\n                    adj[u].remove(v)\n                    degree[u] -= 1\n                    if degree[u] == 0:\n                        isolated_count += 1\n            \n            # Clear v's adjacency set and degree\n            adj[v].clear()\n            degree[v] = 0\n            \n        output.append(str(isolated_count))\n        \n    print('\\n'.join(output))\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    solve()\n```",
