      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    \n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n        M = int(next(iterator))\n        K = int(next(iterator))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    # Build adjacency list\n    adj = [[] for _ in range(N + 1)]\n    \n    for _ in range(M):\n        u = int(next(iterator))\n        v = int(next(iterator))\n        adj[u].append(v)\n        adj[v].append(u)\n        \n    # Read guards\n    guards = []\n    for _ in range(K):\n        p = int(next(iterator))\n        h = int(next(iterator))\n        guards.append((p, h))\n        \n    # We need to find all vertices reachable from any guard's starting position\n    # within their stamina limit.\n    # Since N and M are up to 2*10^5, we can't run BFS from each guard independently\n    # if we do it naively (worst case O(K*N)).\n    # However, note that we want the union of all vertices v such that dist(v, p_i) <= h_i.\n    \n    # A multi-source BFS approach won't work directly because different sources have different limits.\n    # But we can use a modified Dijkstra or BFS with state (vertex, remaining_stamina)? \n    # No, that's too big.\n    \n    # Alternative: For each guard, we want to mark all nodes within distance h_i.\n    # If we just run BFS from each guard, the total complexity could be O(K * (N + M)) in worst case.\n    # Given constraints, K can be up to N=2*10^5, so O(N*(N+M)) is too slow.\n    \n    # We need a more efficient way.\n    # Let's consider the maximum depth each node can be reached from any guard.\n    # Actually, this is equivalent to: for each node v, does there exist a guard i such that dist(v, p_i) <= h_i?\n    # This is equivalent to: min_i (dist(v, p_i) - h_i) <= 0 ? Not quite.\n    \n    # Let's reframe: We want to compute for each node v, the value min_{i} (dist(v, p_i) + h_i)? No.\n    # Condition: dist(v, p_i) <= h_i  <==>  dist(v, p_i) - h_i <= 0.\n    \n    # Consider running a BFS from ALL guards simultaneously, but keeping track of the \"best\" guard reaching a node?\n    # Or, we can think of it as: each guard i provides coverage up to distance h_i.\n    # We can initialize a queue with all guards, along with their remaining stamina.\n    # When we visit a node u from guard i with remaining stamina s, we can move to neighbor v with remaining stamina s-1.\n    # If we reach node v with remaining stamina s', and we've already visited v with remaining stamina s_max >= s', then we don't need to process v again with s'.\n    # So for each node, we only need to keep track of the maximum remaining stamina with which we reached it.\n    \n    # Algorithm:\n    # 1. Initialize an array `max_stamina` of size N+1 with -1 (or 0, since stamina is positive).\n    # 2. Priority Queue (or Deque if weights were uniform, but here \"weights\" are 1 edge per step, but initial stamina varies).\n    #    Actually, since edge weights are 1, we can use a bucket queue or just multiple queues?\n    #    But simpler: Use a standard BFS-like approach but with a priority queue where the key is the remaining stamina?\n    #    Wait, if we use a priority queue ordered by remaining stamina descending, we can ensure that when we first pop a node, \n    #    it's with the maximum possible remaining stamina. Any subsequent visit to that node will have less or equal stamina, so we skip it.\n    \n    # Let's use a max-heap (priority queue) storing tuples (-remaining_stamina, current_node). \n    # Why negative? Because heapq is a min-heap.\n    # Or store (remaining_stamina, current_node) and use a max-heap? Python's heapq doesn't support max-heap directly.\n    # So store (-remaining_stamina, current_node).\n    \n    # Initialization:\n    # For each guard i at p_i with stamina h_i, push (-h_i, p_i) into the heap.\n    # Also, update max_stamina[p_i] = h_i.\n    \n    # While heap is not empty:\n    #   Pop (-s, u). Current remaining stamina is s.\n    #   If s < max_stamina[u], continue (we already found a better path to u).\n    #   For each neighbor v of u:\n    #     new_s = s - 1\n    #     If new_s > 0 and new_s > max_stamina[v]:\n    #       max_stamina[v] = new_s\n    #       push (-new_s, v)\n       \n    # Note: The guard's own vertex is covered if h_i >= 0, which is always true since h_i >= 1.\n    # The condition is dist(v, p_i) <= h_i.\n    # If we start with stamina h_i at p_i, then at distance d, remaining stamina is h_i - d.\n    # We can cover the node if h_i - d >= 0, i.e., d <= h_i.\n    # In the algorithm above, we push neighbors only if new_s > 0. \n    # Wait, if new_s == 0, it means the distance is exactly h_i. The node is still covered!\n    # So we should allow new_s >= 0.\n    \n    # Let's refine:\n    # max_stamina[v] stores the maximum remaining stamina when reaching v.\n    # Initially, for guard at p_i with stamina h_i, we set max_stamina[p_i] = max(max_stamina[p_i], h_i).\n    # And push (-h_i, p_i).\n    \n    # When expanding from u with remaining stamina s:\n    # For neighbor v:\n    #   new_s = s - 1\n    #   If new_s >= 0:\n    #      If new_s > max_stamina[v]:\n    #          max_stamina[v] = new_s\n    #          push (-new_s, v)\n    \n    # Finally, all vertices v with max_stamina[v] >= 0 are guarded.\n    \n    import heapq\n    \n    max_stamina = [-1] * (N + 1)\n    pq = []\n    \n    for p, h in guards:\n        if h > max_stamina[p]:\n            max_stamina[p] = h\n            heapq.heappush(pq, (-h, p))\n            \n    while pq:\n        neg_s, u = heapq.heappop(pq)\n        s = -neg_s\n        \n        # If this entry is stale\n        if s < max_stamina[u]:\n            continue\n            \n        for v in adj[u]:\n            new_s = s - 1\n            if new_s >= 0:\n                if new_s > max_stamina[v]