    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    try:\n        t = int(next(iterator))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n        \n    results = []\n    \n    for _ in range(t):\n        s = next(iterator)\n        \n        # Check if it's already abc\n        if s == \"abc\":\n            results.append(\"YES\")\n            continue\n            \n        # Check if one swap can make it abc\n        # Count mismatches between s and \"abc\"\n        mismatches = []\n        for i in range(3):\n            if s[i] != \"abc\"[i]:\n                mismatches.append(i)\n                \n        # If 0 mismatches, YES (already handled above, but good for logic)\n        # If 2 mismatches, swapping the two mismatched positions might fix it.\n        # If 3 mismatches, one swap can only fix 2 positions at best, so impossible.\n        # Actually, let's just simulate all possible single swaps.\n        \n        possible = False\n        \n        # Option 0: No swap needed (already checked, but included for completeness)\n        if s == \"abc\":\n            possible = True\n        else:\n            # Try all pairs of indices to swap\n            indices = [0, 1, 2]\n            for i in range(3):\n                for j in range(i + 1, 3):\n                    # Create new string by swapping s[i] and s[j]\n                    lst = list(s)\n                    lst[i], lst[j] = lst[j], lst[i]\n                    new_s = \"\".join(lst)\n                    if new_s == \"abc\":\n                        possible = True\n                        break\n                if possible:\n                    break\n                    \n        if possible:\n            results.append(\"YES\")\n        else:\n            results.append(\"NO\")\n            \n    print('\\n'.join(results))\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```",
