    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_350",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nTakahashi will embark on an adventure.\nDuring the adventure, N events will occur.\r\nThe i-th event (1\\leq i\\leq N) is represented by a pair of integers (t _ i,x _ i) (1\\leq t _ i\\leq 2,1\\leq x _ i\\leq N) and is as follows:\n\n- If t _ i=1, he finds one potion of type x _ i. He can choose to pick it up or discard it.\n- If t _ i=2, he encounters one monster of type x _ i. If he has a potion of type x _ i, he can use one to defeat the monster. If he does not defeat it, he will be defeated.\n\nDetermine whether he can defeat all the monsters without being defeated.\nIf he cannot defeat all the monsters, print -1.\nOtherwise, let K be the maximum number of potions he has at some point during the adventure.\r\nLet K _ {{\\min}} be the minimum value of K across all strategies where he will not be defeated.\r\nPrint the value of K _ {{\\min}} and the actions of Takahashi that achieve K _ {{\\min}}.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\r\nt _ 1 x _ 1\r\nt _ 2 x _ 2\r\n\\vdots\r\nt _ N x _ N\n\nOutput\n\nIf Takahashi cannot defeat all the monsters, print -1.\r\nIf he can, print the value of K _ {{\\min}} in the first line, and in the second line, for each i such that t _ i=1 in ascending order, print 1 if he picks up the potion found at the i-th event, and 0 otherwise, separated by spaces.\r\nIf multiple sequences of actions achieve K _ {{\\min}} and allow him to finish the adventure without being defeated, you may print any of them.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq N\\leq2\\times10^5\n- 1\\leq t _ i\\leq2\\ (1\\leq i\\leq N)\n- 1\\leq x _ i\\leq N\\ (1\\leq i\\leq N)\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n13\r\n1 2\r\n1 3\r\n1 1\r\n1 3\r\n1 2\r\n2 3\r\n1 3\r\n1 3\r\n2 3\r\n1 3\r\n2 2\r\n2 3\r\n2 1\n\nSample Output 1\n\n3\r\n1 1 1 0 0 1 0 1\r\n\nThe sample output corresponds to the following actions:\n\n- Find potions of types 2,3,1 in this order. Pick up all of them.\n- Find potions of types 3,2 in this order. Do not pick up any of them.\n- Encounter a type-3 monster. Use one type-3 potion to defeat it.\n- Find a type-3 potion. Pick it up.\n- Find a type-3 potion. Do not pick it up.\n- Encounter a type-3 monster. Use one type-3 potion to defeat it.\n- Find a type-3 potion. Pick it up.\n- Encounter a type-2 monster. Use one type-2 potion to defeat it.\n- Encounter a type-3 monster. Use one type-3 potion to defeat it.\n- Encounter a type-1 monster. Use one type-1 potion to defeat it.\n\nIn this sequence of actions, the value of K is 3.\nThere is no way to avoid defeat with K\\leq 2, so the sought value of K _ {{\\min}} is 3.\r\nThere are multiple sequences of actions that satisfy K=3 and allow him to avoid defeat; you may print any of them.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n4\r\n2 3\r\n1 4\r\n2 1\r\n1 2\n\nSample Output 2\n\n-1\r\n\nHe will inevitably be defeated by the first monster he encounters.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n30\r\n1 25\r\n1 2\r\n1 10\r\n1 18\r\n2 18\r\n1 11\r\n2 11\r\n1 21\r\n1 6\r\n2 2\r\n2 10\r\n1 11\r\n1 24\r\n1 11\r\n1 3\r\n1 2\r\n1 18\r\n2 25\r\n1 8\r\n1 10\r\n1 11\r\n2 18\r\n2 10\r\n1 10\r\n2 2\r\n1 24\r\n1 10\r\n2 10\r\n1 25\r\n2 6\n\nSample Output 3\n\n4\r\n1 1 1 1 1 0 1 0 0 0 0 1 1 0 1 0 1 0 0 0",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict, deque\n\ndef solve():\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(data)\n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    events = []\n    for i in range(N):\n        t = int(next(iterator))\n        x = int(next(iterator))\n        events.append((t, x))\n\n    # First pass: Check if it's possible to defeat all monsters.\n    # We need to ensure that for every monster of type X encountered,\n    # there is a corresponding potion of type X picked up before it.\n    # To minimize inventory, we should greedily pick up potions only when needed?\n    # Actually, the problem asks for min max_inventory.\n    # But first, feasibility check.\n    \n    # Let's track required potions vs available potions.\n    # A simple greedy strategy to check feasibility:\n    # Maintain a count of available potions for each type.\n    # When a monster appears, if count[type] > 0, use one. Else, impossible.\n    # When a potion appears, we can choose to pick it up or not.\n    # To maximize chance of survival, we should pick up potions if they might be needed later.\n    # However, since picking up doesn't hurt feasibility (it only increases inventory),\n    # we can assume we pick up ALL potions for feasibility check.\n    # If even picking up all potions isn't enough, then it's impossible.\n    \n    inventory = defaultdict(int)\n    possible = True\n    for t, x in events:\n        if t == 1:\n            inventory[x] += 1\n        else: # t == 2, monster\n            if inventory[x] > 0:\n                inventory[x] -= 1\n            else:\n                possible = False\n                break\n    \n    if not possible:\n        print(\"-1\")\n        return\n\n    # Now, find the minimum K (max inventory size) and the strategy.\n    # This is a classic \"minimum capacity\" problem which can be solved with binary search on K.\n    # For a fixed K, can we survive?\n    # Strategy for a fixed K:\n    # We process events. We maintain current inventory counts.\n    # Constraint: sum(inventory.values()) <= K at all times.\n    # When a potion (type x) appears:\n    #   We MUST pick it up IF we don't have it and we need it later? \n    #   Actually, the decision is: pick up or discard.\n    #   If we pick up, inventory[x] increases. Sum must stay <= K.\n    #   If we discard, inventory[x] stays same.\n    # When a monster (type x) appears:\n    #   We MUST have inventory[x] > 0. Then inventory[x] decreases.\n    \n    # Greedy strategy for feasibility check with limit K:\n    # We want to keep potions that are \"useful\".\n    # A potion of type x is useful if there is a future monster of type x that hasn't been matched yet.\n    # Let's precalculate the last occurrence of each monster type? Or rather, match monsters to potions.\n    \n    # Alternative approach:\n    # Binary search for K.\n    # Check(K): Can we survive with max inventory K?\n    # Inside Check(K):\n    #   We need to decide for each potion event whether to pick it up.\n    #   We should pick it up ONLY if it helps us defeat a future monster AND we have space.\n    #   If we have space, we should pick up potions that are necessary.\n    #   Which ones are necessary? Those that correspond to upcoming monsters.\n    #   If we don't have space, we must discard. Which one to discard? The one that is least urgently needed?\n    #   Actually, standard greedy for this type of problem:\n    #   Use a priority queue or similar structure to manage what's in inventory.\n    #   But here types matter.\n    \n    # Let's refine Check(K):\n    # Iterate through events.\n    # Maintain current inventory counts.\n    # Also, for each potion type, know how many monsters of that type are remaining in the future.\n    # Precompute suffix counts of monsters for each type.\n    \n    # Let suffi