r the grid as concentric square rings. The center is a 1x1 ring.\n    # We can fill the rings from outside in, or inside out.\n    # If we fill from outside in, the last number will be adjacent to the center.\n    \n    # Let's verify:\n    # Outer ring: cells where max(|i-cx|, |j-cy|) == (N-1)/2.\n    # Next ring: ...\n    # Inner ring: cells where max(|i-cx|, |j-cy|) == 1.\n    # Center: (cx, cy).\n    \n    # If we fill the outermost ring, then the next inner ring, ..., down to the ring \n    # surrounding the center, the last cell of the innermost ring (surrounding center) \n    # must be adjacent to the center.\n    \n    # Let's implement this.\n    \n    k = (N - 1) // 2  # Radius of the outermost ring\n    \n    # We will fill rings from radius k down to radius 1.\n    # For each ring, we traverse it in a specific direction (e.g., clockwise).\n    # We need to ensure that the end of one ring is adjacent to the start of the next ring?\n    # No, the condition is only that x and x+1 are adjacent.\n    # So within a ring, the traversal must be continuous.\n    # Between rings, the last cell of the previous ring (higher radius) must be adjacent \n    # to the first cell of the next ring (lower radius).\n    \n    # Let's check adjacency between rings.\n    # Ring r consists of cells (i,j) such that max(|i-cx|, |j-cy|) == r.\n    # The cells in ring r are on the boundary of the square centered at (cx,cy) with side length 2r+1.\n    # The cells in ring r-1 are on the boundary of the square with side length 2(r-1)+1 = 2r-1.\n    \n    # Let's try traversing each ring clockwise.\n    # Start of ring r: (cx - r, cy - r) ? Or (cx - r, cy + r)?\n    # Let's define the path for a ring of radius r > 0.\n    # Top row: from (cx-r, cy-r) to (cx-r, cy+r)\n    # Right col: from (cx-r+1, cy+r) to (cx+r, cy+r)\n    # Bottom row: from (cx+r, cy+r-1) to (cx+r, cy-r)\n    # Left col: from (cx+r-1, cy-r) to (cx-r+1, cy-r)\n    \n    # Let's see if the end of ring r is adjacent to 