    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef countPairs(n: int, m: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the number of pairs (x, y) such that:\n    - 1 <= x <= n\n    - 1 <= y <= m\n    - Alice wins the game starting with x flowers clockwise and y flowers anti-clockwise.\n    \n    Game Rules:\n    - Alice goes first.\n    - In each turn, a player picks one flower from either clockwise or anti-clockwise side.\n    - The player who takes the last flower wins (captures opponent).\n    \n    This is equivalent to a Nim-like game with two piles of sizes x and y.\n    However, note that players can choose WHICH pile to take from.\n    This is exactly the game of Nim with two piles.\n    \n    In Nim with two piles of sizes x and y:\n    - The losing positions (P-positions) are those where x == y.\n    - The winning positions (N-positions) are those where x != y.\n    \n    Why?\n    - If x == y, whatever move Alice makes (taking k from one pile), Bob can mirror it on the other pile, keeping them equal until Bob takes the last one.\n    - If x != y, Alice can make them equal by taking |x-y| from the larger pile, putting Bob in a losing position.\n    \n    So Alice wins if and only if x != y.\n    \n    We need to count pairs (x, y) with 1 <= x <= n, 1 <= y <= m, such that x != y.\n    \n    Total pairs = n * m\n    Pairs where x == y = min(n, m)\n    \n    Result = n * m - min(n, m)\n    \"\"\"\n    total = n * m\n    same = min(n, m)\n    return total - same\n```",
