      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n        T_prime = next(iterator)\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    S_list = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        S_list.append(next(iterator))\n\n    len_Tp = len(T_prime)\n    \n    # Precompute hashes for T_prime to allow O(1) substring comparison\n    # Using double hashing to avoid collisions\n    MOD1 = 10**9 + 7\n    BASE1 = 31\n    MOD2 = 10**9 + 9\n    BASE2 = 37\n    \n    def compute_hash(s, mod, base):\n        h = 0\n        for c in s:\n            h = (h * base + ord(c)) % mod\n        return h\n\n    def get_prefix_hashes(s, mod, base):\n        n = len(s)\n        h = [0] * (n + 1)\n        p = [1] * (n + 1)\n        for i in range(n):\n            h[i+1] = (h[i] * base + ord(s[i])) % mod\n            p[i+1] = (p[i] * base) % mod\n        return h, p\n\n    def get_substring_hash(h, p, l, r, mod, base):\n        # hash of s[l:r]\n        return (h[r] - h[l] * p[r-l]) % mod\n\n    # Compute prefix hashes for T_prime\n    hp1, pp1 = get_prefix_hashes(T_prime, MOD1, BASE1)\n    hp2, pp2 = get_prefix_hashes(T_prime, MOD2, BASE2)\n\n    results = []\n\n    for idx, S in enumerate(S_list):\n        i = idx + 1\n        len_S = len(S)\n        \n        # Case 1: T' == S\n        if len_S == len_Tp:\n            if S == T_prime:\n                results.append(i)\n                continue\n            # Check if they differ by exactly one character\n            diff_count = 0\n            for c1, c2 in zip(S, T_prime):\n                if c1 != c2:\n                    diff_count += 1\n                    if diff_count > 1:\n                        break\n            if diff_count == 1:\n                results.append(i)\n                continue\n            continue\n        \n        # Case 2: len_S == len_Tp + 1\n        # T' obtained by deleting one char from S => S obtained by inserting one char into T'\n        # So we check if T' can be obtained by deleting one char from S.\n        if len_S == len_Tp + 1:\n            # Check if S with one deletion equals T'\n            found = False\n            # Find first mismatch\n            j = 0\n            while j < len_Tp and S[j] == T_prime[j]:\n                j += 1\n            \n            # If no mismatch up to len_Tp, then the extra char is at the end\n            if j == len_Tp:\n                found = True\n            else:\n                # Mismatch at index j in both strings (S has index j, T_prime has index j)\n                # Option A: Delete S[j]\n                # Then S[j+1:] must match T_prime[j:]\n                if S[j+1:] == T_prime[j:]:\n                    found = True\n                # Option B: Delete some later char? No, if we skip S[j], the rest must align.\n                # Actually, since we found the first mismatch at j, any valid deletion must happen at or before j.\n                # If we delete S[k] where k < j, then S[0...k-1] matches T'[0...k-1], but S[k] != T'[k] implies T'[k] would be S[k+1].\n                # But S[0...j-1] == T'[0...j-1]. So if we deleted before j, say at k, then T'[k] would be S[k+1].\n                # However, we established S[0...j-1] == T'[0...j-1]. This means for all m < j, S[m] == T'[m].\n                # If we deleted S[k] with k < j, then T'[k] should be S[k+1]. But T'[k] == S[k]. So S[k] == S[k+1].\n                # And T'[k+1] should be S[k+2]... eventually T'[j-1] should be S[j].\n                # But T'[j-1] == S[j-1]. So S[j-1] == S[j].\n                # This gets complicated. Let's just use the standard two-pointer or slicing check.\n                \n                # Simpler logic:\n                # We need to remove exactly one character from S to get T'.\n                # Since S is longer by 1, we iterate through T' and S.\n                # Find the first index where they differ.\n                # If no difference, the last char of S is the extra one.\n                # If difference at index k, then either:\n                # 1. Remove S[k]. Then S[k+1...] must equal T'[k...].\n                # 2. Remove T'[k]? No, we are modifying S.\n                # Wait, the condition is \"T' is obtained by deleting one character from S\".\n                # So we look at S. We remove one char from S to get T'.\n                \n                # Let's refine the check:\n                # Find first index k such that S[k] != T_prime[k].\n                # If no such k exists (prefix matches fully), then S[len_Tp] is the extra char. Valid.\n                # If such k exists, then the removed char must be S[k].\n                # Why? Because if we removed S[m] with m < k, then T'[m] would be S[m+1].\n                # But T'[m] == S[m] for all m < k. So S[m] == S[m+1].\n                # This implies S[k-1] == S[k].\n                # Generally, if we remove S[m], the suffix S[m+1:] shifts left.\n                # For the prefix S[0:k] to match T'[0:k], we must have removed a character at index >= k?\n                # No. If we remove S[m] with m < k, then T'[0:m] = S[0:m], T'[m] = S[m+1], ..., T'[k-1] = S[k].\n                # We know T'[0:k] = S[0:k]. So S[m+1...k] must equal S[m...k-1].\n                # This is possible.\n                # Example: S = \"aa\", T' = \"a\". k=0? S[0]='a', T'[0]='a'. Match.\n                # S=\"aba\", T'=\"ab\". S[0]='a', T'[0]='a'. S[1]='b', T'[1]='b'. Match.\n                # S[2]='a', T' ends. Extra char at end.\n                \n                # Let's just do the direct check:\n                # Try removing each character from S? Too slow O(N).\n                # But we know S and T' are close.\n                \n                # Correct approach for \"Can S become T' by deleting one char?\":\n                # Two pointers.\n                i_s = 0\n                i_tp = 0\n                deleted = False\n                while i_s < len_S and i_tp < len_Tp:\n                    if S[i_s] == T_prime[i_tp]:\n                        i_s += 1\n                        i_tp += 1\n                    else:\n                        if deleted:\n                            found = False\n                            break\n                        deleted = True\n                        i_s += 1 # Skip character in S\n                \n                if not deleted:\n                    # All matched, one char remains at end of S\n                    found = True\n                elif i_s < len_S:\n                    # One char skipped, remaining chars in S must match nothing?\n                    # Since len_S = len_Tp + 1, if we skipped one, i_s should be len_S and i_tp should be len_Tp.\n                    # If loop finished with deleted=True, we expect i_s == len_S and i_tp == len_Tp.\n                    # If i_s < len_S, it means we still have chars in S but T' is exhausted?\n                    # No, if deleted=True, we incremented i_s once without i_tp.\n                    # So i_s increased by len_Tp + 1, i_tp by len_Tp.\n                    # len_S = len_Tp + 1. So i_s reaches len_S exactly when i_tp reaches len_Tp.\n                    # So if we exit loop, check if we consumed all of S and T'.\n                    if i_tp == len_Tp and i_s == len_S:\n                        found = True\n                    else:\n                        found = False\n                else:\n                    # Loop ended because i_s reached len_S?\n                    # If deleted was false, handled above.\n                    # If deleted was true, and i_s reached len_S, did i_tp reach len_Tp?\n                    pass\n                \n                # Re-evaluating the loop logic for robustness:\n                # Reset\n                i_s = 0\n                i_tp = 0\n                diff = 0\n                while i_s < len_S and i_tp < len_Tp:\n                    if S[i_s] != T_prime[i_tp]:\n                        diff += 1\n                        if diff > 1:\n                            found = False\n                            break\n                        # Assume deletion from S\n                        i_s += 1\n                    else:\n                        i_s += 1\n                        i_tp += 1\n                \n                if diff <= 1:\n                    # If diff is 0, we might have 1 char left in S\n                    if diff == 0 and i_s < len_S:\n                        found = True\n                    elif diff == 1:\n                        # We skipped one char in S. i_tp must have caught up to len_Tp?\n                        # If i_s didn't reach end, but i_tp did, that's fine.\n                        # Actually, if diff=1, we incremented i_s extra time.\n                        # Remaining chars in S must match remaining in T'?\n                        # The loop breaks or finishes.\n                        # If loop finishes normally:\n                        # If diff=1, we skipped one S char. The rest must match.\n                        # Since lengths differ by 1, if we skip one, the rest length is equal.\n                        # So if we didn't break due to diff>1, it's valid.\n                        found = True\n                    else:\n                        found = False # Should not happen if loop completes\n                \n                # Let's stick to the simpler slice check which is O(L) and fast enough given constraints\n                # Total length of S_i is limited. Sum of lengths <= 5*10^5.\n                # So O(L) per string is acceptable.\n                \n                # Slice check for deletion:\n                # Find first mismatch index k\n                k = 0\n                while k < len_Tp and S[k] == T_prime[k]:\n                    k += 1\n                \n                if k == len_Tp:\n                    # S has one extra char at the end\n                    found = True\n                else:\n                    # Mismatch at k\n                    # Option 1: Delete S[k]. Then S[k+1:] must equal T_prime[k:]\n                    if S[k+1:] == T_prime[k:]:\n                        found = True\n                    else:\n                        found = False\n                        \n            if found:\n                results.append(i)\n            continue\n\n        # Case 3: len_S == len_Tp - 1\n        # T' obtained by inserting one char into S => S obtained by deleting one char from T'\n        # Or: T' is obtained by deleting one char from S? No, S is shorter.\n        # Condition: T' is obtained by deleting one character from S.\n        # This implies len(T') < len(S). Here len(S) < len(T').\n        # So this case corresponds to: T' is obtained by inserting one char into S.\n        # Which is equivalent to: S is obtained by deleting one char from T'.\n        \n        if len_S == len_Tp - 1:\n            # Check if T' with one deletion equals S\n            k = 0\n            while k < len_S and T_prime[k] == S[k]:\n                k += 1\n            \n            if k == len_S:\n                # S matches prefix of T', extra char at end of T'\n                found = True\n            else:\n                # Mismatch at k\n                # Option: Delete T_prime[k]. Then T_prime[k+1:] must equal S[k:]\n                if T_prime[k+1:] == S[k:]:\n                    found = True\n                else:\n                    found = False\n            \n            if found:\n                results.append(i)\n            continue\n\n        # Case 4: len_S == len_Tp\n        # Already handled equality and single char change in Case 1 logic?\n        # Case 1 handled exact equality.\n        # Single char change:\n        # We already checked exact equality.\n        # Now check if exactly one character differs.\n        if len_S == len_Tp:\n            diff_count = 0\n            for c1, c2 in zip(S, T_prime):\n                if c1 != c2:\n                    diff_count += 1\n                    if diff_count > 1:\n                        break\n            if diff_count == 1:\n                results.append(i)\n            continue\n\n        # Other lengths are impossible\n        # len_S < len_Tp - 1 or len_S > len_Tp + 1\n\n    # Output\n    print(len(results))\n    if results:\n        print(*(results))\n\nsolve()\n```",
