nt_s['@'] = len(s) ? No.\n    # The wildcards in s can become any special char.\n    # So, for each c in special_chars, the number of c's provided by s is count_s[c]. The remaining needed x_c - count_s[c] must be covered by @s in s.\n    # So sum_{c} max(0, x_c - count_s[c]) <= count_s['@']\n    # Similarly, sum_{c} max(0, x_c - count_t[c]) <= count_t['@']\n    \n    # However, we don't know x_c. But note that we can choose x_c freely as long as they are consistent with the constraints.\n    # Actually, a simpler condition is:\n    # For each c in special_chars, count_s[c] <= count_t[c] + count_t['@']\n    # AND count_t[c] <= count_s[c] + count_s['@']\n    # Is this sufficient?\n    # Let's see. If these hold, can we find such x_c?\n    # Yes. We can set x_c = max(count_s[c], count_t[c]).\n    # Then check if sum(max(0, x_c - count_s[c])) <= count_s['@']\n    # and sum(max(0, x_c - count_t[c])) <= count_t['@'].\n    \n    # Let's test this hypothesis.\n    # Sample 1: s=\"ch@ku@ai\", t=\"choku@@i\"\n    # special_chars = {a,t,c,o,d,e,r}\n    # count_s: c:1, h:1, @:2, k:1, u:1, a:1, i:1 -> non-special: h,k,u,i\n    # count_t: c:1, h:1, o:1, k:1, u:1, @:2, i:1 -> non-special: h,k,u,i\n    # For c in special:\n    # a: s=1, t=0. t+@ = 2 >= 1. OK.\n    # t: s=0, t=0. OK.\n    # c: s=1, t=1. OK.\n    # o: s=0, t=1. s+@ = 2 >= 1. OK.\n    # d: s=0, t=0. OK.\n    # e: s=0, t=0. OK.\n    # r: s=0, t=0. OK.\n    # All OK. Output Yes. Correct.\n    \n    # Sample 3: s=\"aoki\", t=\"@ok@\"\n    # count_s: a:1, o:1, k:1, i:1\n    # count_t: @:2, o:1, k:1\n    # Check a: s=1, t=0. t+@=2 >= 1. OK.\n    # Check o: s=1, t=1. OK.\n    # Check k: s=1, t=1. OK.\n    # Check i: s=1, t=0. t+@=2 >= 1. OK.\n    # Wait, Sample 3 output is No. Why?\n    # Because i is NOT a special character! It cannot be produced by @.\n    # My previous logic assumed all non-@ characters must be in special_chars.\n    # But the problem says: \"Replace each card with @ with one of the following cards: a, t