    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_481",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a string s. s[i] is either a lowercase English letter or '?'.\nFor a string t having length m containing only lowercase English letters, we define the function cost(i) for an index i as the number of characters equal to t[i] that appeared before it, i.e. in the range [0, i - 1].\nThe value of t is the sum of cost(i) for all indices i.\nFor example, for the string t = \"aab\":\n\ncost(0) = 0\ncost(1) = 1\ncost(2) = 0\nHence, the value of \"aab\" is 0 + 1 + 0 = 1.\n\nYour task is to replace all occurrences of '?' in s with any lowercase English letter so that the value of s is minimized.\nReturn a string denoting the modified string with replaced occurrences of '?'. If there are multiple strings resulting in the minimum value, return the lexicographically smallest one.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput:   s = \"???\" \nOutput:   \"abc\" \nExplanation:  In this example, we can replace the occurrences of '?' to make s equal to \"abc\".\nFor \"abc\", cost(0) = 0, cost(1) = 0, and cost(2) = 0.\nThe value of \"abc\" is 0.\nSome other modifications of s that have a value of 0 are \"cba\", \"abz\", and, \"hey\".\nAmong all of them, we choose the lexicographically smallest.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:  s = \"a?a?\"\nOutput:  \"abac\"\nExplanation:  In this example, the occurrences of '?' can be replaced to make s equal to \"abac\".\nFor \"abac\", cost(0) = 0, cost(1) = 0, cost(2) = 1, and cost(3) = 0.\nThe value of \"abac\" is 1.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= s.length <= 10^5\ns[i] is either a lowercase English letter or '?'.",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef modifyString(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Replace all '?' in s with lowercase English letters to minimize the 'value' of the string.\n    The value is defined as the sum of cost(i) for all i, where cost(i) is the number of times\n    s[i] appears in s[0...i-1].\n    If multiple solutions yield the minimum value, return the lexicographically smallest one.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    # We need to build the result character by character.\n    # Since the choice of character at index i affects the cost for future indices,\n    # but also the cost for index i itself depends on previous choices.\n    # However, note that minimizing the total cost is equivalent to minimizing the sum of costs.\n    # cost(i) is the count of char s[i] in s[0..i-1].\n    # To minimize the total sum, we want each s[i] to appear as few times as possible before it.\n    # Ideally, we want s[i] to be a character that has NOT appeared before, so cost(i)=0.\n    # If all 26 letters have appeared, then cost(i) will be at least 1.\n    \n    # Strategy:\n    # Iterate through the string. For each position i:\n    # If s[i] is not '?', it's fixed. We record its count and add its current cost (which is the count so far) to our mental total, but we don't need to track the total explicitly for construction, just the counts.\n    # If s[i] is '?', we should pick the lexicographically smallest character that minimizes the cost at this step.\n    # Wait, does picking the character that minimizes the immediate cost (i.e., makes cost(i) as small as possible) also lead to the global minimum?\n    # Yes, because the cost contribution of index i is independent of future choices. It only depends on s[0...i-1].\n    # So, greedy approach works: for each '?', pick the character that has the minimum count in s[0...i-1]. If there's a tie, pick the lexicographically smallest one.\n    \n    # Let's maintain the frequency of each character seen so far.\n    freq = [0] * 26\n    \n    res = list(s)\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        if res[i] != '?':\n            idx = ord(res[i]) - ord('a')\n            freq[idx] += 1\n        else:\n            # Find the character with the minimum frequency\n            min_freq = float('inf')\n            best_char_idx = -1\n            \n            for c in range(26):\n                if freq[c] < min_freq:\n                    min_freq = freq[c]\n                    best_char_idx = c\n                elif freq[c] == min_freq:\n                    # Since we iterate from 'a' to 'z', the first one we find with min_freq is already the lexicographically smallest\n                    break\n            \n            # Assign the best character\n            res[i] = chr(best_char_idx + ord('a'))\n            freq[best_char_idx] += 1\n            \n    return \"\".join(res)\n```",
