    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_95",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed integer array receiver of length n and an integer k.\nThere are n players having a unique id in the range [0, n - 1] who will play a ball passing game, and receiver[i] is the id of the player who receives passes from the player with id i. Players can pass to themselves, i.e. receiver[i] may be equal to i.\nYou must choose one of the n players as the starting player for the game, and the ball will be passed exactly k times starting from the chosen player.\nFor a chosen starting player having id x, we define a function f(x) that denotes the sum of x and the ids of all players who receive the ball during the k passes, including repetitions. In other words, f(x) = x + receiver[x] + receiver[receiver[x]] + ... + receiver^(k)[x].\nYour task is to choose a starting player having id x that maximizes the value of f(x).\nReturn an integer denoting the maximum value of the function.\nNote: receiver may contain duplicates.\n \nExample 1:\n\n\n\nPass Number\nSender ID\nReceiver ID\nx + Receiver IDs\n\n\n \n \n \n2\n\n\n1\n2\n1\n3\n\n\n2\n1\n0\n3\n\n\n3\n0\n2\n5\n\n\n4\n2\n1\n6\n\n\n\n\nInput: receiver = [2,0,1], k = 4\nOutput: 6\nExplanation: The table above shows a simulation of the game starting with the player having id x = 2. \nFrom the table, f(2) is equal to 6. \nIt can be shown that 6 is the maximum achievable value of the function. \nHence, the output is 6. \n\nExample 2:\n\n\n\nPass Number\nSender ID\nReceiver ID\nx + Receiver IDs\n\n\n \n \n \n4\n\n\n1\n4\n3\n7\n\n\n2\n3\n2\n9\n\n\n3\n2\n1\n10\n\n\n\n\nInput: receiver = [1,1,1,2,3], k = 3\nOutput: 10\nExplanation: The table above shows a simulation of the game starting with the player having id x = 4. \nFrom the table, f(4) is equal to 10. \nIt can be shown that 10 is the maximum achievable value of the function. \nHence, the output is 10. \n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= receiver.length == n <= 10^5\n0 <= receiver[i] <= n - 1\n1 <= k <= 10^10",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef maximumScore(receiver: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    n = len(receiver)\n    \n    # Since k can be up to 10^10, we cannot simulate step-by-step for each start node.\n    # We need a faster approach. This problem involves finding paths of length k in a functional graph.\n    # A functional graph consists of components, each containing exactly one cycle with some trees rooted on the cycle nodes directing towards the cycle.\n    \n    # Approach:\n    # 1. Detect cycles and compute sums for traversing cycles.\n    # 2. For each node, we want to find the max score starting from it after k steps.\n    # 3. Use binary lifting (doubling) to jump k steps efficiently? \n    #    However, k is large (10^10), so binary lifting is suitable. We need to precompute:\n    #    - parent[i][j]: the node reached after 2^j steps from i.\n    #    - sum_val[i][j]: the sum of node IDs encountered during the 2^j steps starting from i (including the intermediate nodes, but NOT including the start node i itself? Or including?)\n    #    Let's define carefully.\n    #    f(x) = x + receiver[x] + ... + receiver^(k)(x).\n    #    So if we jump 2^j steps from x, we get to next_node, and the sum added is the sum of the 2^j nodes visited along the way (excluding x, including the last node? No, let's look at the definition).\n    #    The path is: x -> r1 -> r2 -> ... -> rk.\n    #    Sum = x + r1 + r2 + ... + rk.\n    #    \n    #    Let dp[j][i] be the node reached after 2^j steps starting from i.\n    #    Let sum_dp[j][i] be the sum of the nodes visited in the 2^j steps, EXCLUDING the starting node i, but INCLUDING the final node reached after 2^j steps.\n    #    Wait, if we chain two jumps:\n    #    Jump 2^a from i to mid, sum added is S_a.\n    #    Jump 2^b from mid to end, sum added is S_b.\n    #    Total sum from i over 2^a + 2^b steps (excluding i) is S_a + S_b.\n    #    Then f(i) = i + total_sum_excluding_i.\n    \n    #    Base case j=0 (1 step):\n    #    dp[0][i] = receiver[i]\n    #    sum_dp[0][i] = receiver[i]\n    \n    #    Recursive step:\n    #    dp[j][i] = dp[j-1][ dp[j-1][i] ]\n    #    sum_dp[j][i] = sum_dp[j-1][i] + sum_dp[j-1][ dp[j-1][i] ]\n    \n    #    Max number of bits in k: log2(10^10) approx 34. So we need up to j=34 or 35.\n    \n    LOG_K = 35  # 2^34 > 10^10\n    \n    # Initialize tables\n    # dp[j][i]\n    # sum_dp[j][i]\n    \n    # To save memory, we can process bit by bit or store the whole table. \n    # n=10^5, LOG_K=35 => 3.5 million entries. Each entry is an int. This fits in memory.\n    \n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(LOG_K)]\n    sum_dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(LOG_K)]\n    \n    # Level 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[0][i] = receiver[i]\n        sum_dp[0][i] = receiver[i]\n        \n    # Build higher levels\n    for j in range(1, LOG_K):\n        for i in range(n):\n            prev_node = dp[j-1][i]\n            dp[j][i] = dp[j-1][prev_node]\n            sum_dp[j][i] = sum_dp[j-1][i] + sum_dp[j-1][prev_node]\n            \n    max_f = -1\n    \n    for start_node in range(n):\n        current_node = start_node\n        current_sum = 0\n        \n        # Decompose k into binary\n        temp_k = k\n        for j in range(LOG_K):\n            if temp_k & 1:\n                current_sum += sum_dp[j][current_node]\n                current_node = dp[j][current_node]\n            temp_k >>= 1\n            if temp_k == 0:\n                break\n                \n        f_x = start_node + current_sum\n        if f_x > max_f:\n            max_f = f_x\n            \n    return max_f\n```",
