      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n        \n    bases = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        W = int(next(iterator))\n        X = int(next(iterator))\n        bases.append((W, X))\n    \n    # We need to find a UTC start time T (integer hour, 0 <= T < 24) such that\n    # the number of participants is maximized.\n    # A meeting from T to T+1 UTC is valid for base i if:\n    # The local time at base i during [T, T+1) is within [9, 18).\n    # Local time at base i for UTC time t is (t + X_i) % 24.\n    # So we need: 9 <= (T + X_i) % 24 < 18.\n    # Since the meeting is 1 hour long, if the start local time is L, the end local time is L+1.\n    # For the entire meeting to be within 9:00-18:00, we need:\n    # Start local time >= 9 AND End local time <= 18.\n    # End local time = (Start local time + 1) % 24? No, let's think carefully.\n    # If local start time is 17:00, local end time is 18:00. This is within [9, 18].\n    # If local start time is 18:00, local end time is 19:00. This is NOT within [9, 18] because 18:00 is the start of the excluded period?\n    # The problem says \"completely within the 9:00-18:00 time slot\".\n    # Usually this means [9, 18). Or [9, 18]?\n    # Sample 1: Base 3 has X=18. Meeting 14-15 UTC.\n    # Base 3 local time: 14+18 = 32 -> 8:00. Meeting 8:00-9:00.\n    # Output says 2 employees CANNOT participate.\n    # So 8:00-9:00 is NOT within 9:00-18:00.\n    # This implies the interval is [9, 18). i.e., 9:00 <= start < end <= 18:00.\n    # Or rather, the meeting time [S, E) must satisfy 9 <= S and E <= 18.\n    # Here S=8, E=9. 8 < 9, so it fails.\n    \n    # So condition: 9 <= (T + X_i) % 24 AND ((T + X_i) % 24) + 1 <= 18?\n    # Let L = (T + X_i) % 24.\n    # We need L >= 9 and L + 1 <= 18 => L <= 17.\n    # So valid local start times are 9, 10, ..., 17.\n    \n    # We can iterate over all possible UTC start times T from 0 to 23.\n    # For each T, calculate the total employees.\n    \n    max_employees = 0\n    \n    for T in range(24):\n        current_employees = 0\n        for W, X in bases:\n            local_start = (T + X) % 24\n            if 9 <= local_start <= 17:\n                current_employees += W\n        if current_employees > max_employees:\n            max_employees = current_employees\n            \n    print(max_employees)\n\nsolve()\n```",
