      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minChanges(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculate the minimum number of changes required to make the binary string s beautiful.\n    \n    A string is beautiful if it can be partitioned into substrings where each substring:\n    - Has even length\n    - Contains only '0's or only '1's\n    \n    The approach is to process the string in pairs of adjacent characters (indices 0-1, 2-3, etc.).\n    For each pair, if the two characters are different, we must change one of them to match the other.\n    This costs 1 change per differing pair.\n    If they are the same, no change is needed for that pair.\n    \n    This greedy approach works because any valid partition into even-length monochromatic substrings\n    implies that within each chunk of 2 characters in the optimal pairing structure, they should ideally\n    be the same. More formally, since every block in the partition has even length and is uniform,\n    we can view the string as composed of blocks of size >= 2. Within any contiguous segment of 2 chars\n    that falls entirely within a uniform block, they must be equal. Since we can choose the partition,\n    the minimal cost is achieved by ensuring s[2i] == s[2i+1] for all i.\n    \n    Args:\n        s: A binary string with even length.\n        \n    Returns:\n        The minimum number of changes required.\n    \"\"\"\n    changes = 0\n    # Iterate through the string in steps of 2\n    for i in range(0, len(s), 2):\n        # Check if the current pair of characters are different\n        if s[i] != s[i + 1]:\n            changes += 1\n    return changes\n```",
