      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to:\n1. Understand that swaps only happen at distance 2 (indices 0↔2, 1↔3)\n2. Generate all permutations reachable from s1 via these swaps\n3. Check if s2 is among them\n\n**Key insight:** Position 0 and 2 are linked, and position 1 and 3 are linked. Swapping a pair twice returns to original (so each pair can be in 2 states), giving at most 2×2 = 4 reachable configurations from any starting string.\n\n```python\ndef can_be_equal(s1, s2):\n    # Generate all strings reachable from s1 using swaps of distance 2\n    reachable = set()\n    queue = [s1]\n    visited = {s1}\n    \n    while queue:\n        current = queue.pop()\n        reachable.add(current)\n        \n        for i in range(4):\n            j = i + 2\n            lst = list(current)\n            lst[i], lst[j] = lst[j], lst[i]\n            new_str = ''.join(lst)\n            if new_str not in visited:\n                visited.add(new_str)\n                queue.append(new_str)\n    \n    return s2 in reachable\n```\n\nAlternatively, the closed-form answer is much cleaner - since each pair (0,2) and (1,3) independently has 2 states:\n\n```python\ndef can_be_equal(s1, s2):\n    return (s1[0] == s2[0] and s1[2] == s2[2]) != (s1[0] == s2[2] and s1[2] == s2[0]) or \\\n           (s1[1] == s2[1] and s1[3] == s2[3]) != (s1[1] == s2[3] and s1[3] == s2[1])\n```\n\n**Cleanest closed-form solution:** Direct set construction:\n\n```python\ndef can_be_equal(s1, s2):\n    reachable = set()\n    for a in (s1[0], s1[2]):\n        for b in (s1[1], s1[3]):\n            for c in (s1[0], s1[2]):\n                for d in (s1[1], s1[3]):\n                    if a == c and b == d and a != s1[0] and b != s1[1]:\n                        continue\n                    reachable.add(a + b + c + d)\n    return s2 in reachable\n```\n\n**Final efficient version** using the observation that each index pair is independent:\n\n```python\ndef can_be_equal(s1, s2):\n    reachable = {\n        s1,\n        s1[2] + s1[1] + s1[0] + s1[3],\n        s1[0] + s1[3] + s1[2] + s1[1],\n        s1[2] + s1[3] + s1[0] + s1[1]\n    }\n    return s2 in reachable\n```",
