    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef find_kth_smallest(coins: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Find the k-th smallest amount that can be made using coins of given denominations.\n    You can use any number of coins of each denomination, but you cannot combine different denominations.\n    This means we are looking for the k-th smallest number that is a multiple of at least one coin value.\n    \n    Args:\n        coins: List of distinct positive integers representing coin denominations.\n        k: The position of the smallest amount to find (1-indexed).\n        \n    Returns:\n        The k-th smallest amount that is a multiple of at least one coin in the list.\n    \"\"\"\n    import math\n    from itertools import combinations\n    \n    def count_up_to(n: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Count how many numbers in range [1, n] are divisible by at least one coin.\n        Uses inclusion-exclusion principle.\n        \"\"\"\n        m = len(coins)\n        total = 0\n        # Iterate through all non-empty subsets of coins\n        for r in range(1, m + 1):\n            for combo in combinations(coins, r):\n                # Compute LCM of the current combination\n                lcm_val = combo[0]\n                for c in combo[1:]:\n                    lcm_val = lcm_val * c // math.gcd(lcm_val, c)\n                \n                # Number of multiples of lcm_val in [1, n]\n                count = n // lcm_val\n                \n                # Inclusion-exclusion: add if odd size, subtract if even size\n                if r % 2 == 1:\n                    total += count\n                else:\n                    total -= count\n        return total\n    \n    # Binary search for the smallest n such that count_up_to(n) >= k\n    low = 1\n    high = k * min(coins)  # Upper bound: worst case, only smallest coin matters\n    \n    while low < high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if count_up_to(mid) < k:\n            low = mid + 