    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef longest_semi_repetitive_substring(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the length of the longest semi-repetitive substring inside s.\n    A string is semi-repetitive if there is at most one consecutive pair of the same digits.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    \n    # We can use a sliding window approach.\n    # Keep track of the indices where consecutive pairs occur.\n    # Let's store the indices i such that s[i] == s[i+1].\n    # These are the \"bad\" spots where we have a repeated pair.\n    \n    # Actually, simpler: maintain a window [left, right].\n    # Count how many consecutive duplicate pairs are in the current window.\n    # If count > 1, shrink from left until count <= 1.\n    \n    max_len = 1\n    left = 0\n    # We need to count the number of adjacent duplicates in s[left:right+1]\n    # An adjacent duplicate is at index i if s[i] == s[i+1], for i in range(left, right).\n    \n    # Let's iterate right from 0 to n-1.\n    # Maintain a variable 'duplicates' which counts the number of indices i in [left, right-1]\n    # such that s[i] == s[i+1].\n    \n    duplicates = 0\n    \n    for right in range(1, n):\n        # Check if the new pair (right-1, right) is a duplicate\n        if s[right - 1] == s[right]:\n            duplicates += 1\n        \n        # While duplicates > 1, shrink the window from the left\n        while duplicates > 1:\n            # Check if the pair starting at 'left' was a duplicate\n            if s[left] == s[left + 1]:\n                duplicates -= 1\n            left += 1\n        \n        # Current valid window is [left, right]\n        current_len = right - left + 1\n        if current_len > max_len:\n            max_len = current_len\n            \n    return max_len\n```",
