      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef maxStrength(nums: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the maximum strength obtainable by selecting k disjoint subarrays.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    # dp[i][j] represents the max strength using j subarrays considering prefix ending at or before index i\n    # However, we need to track whether the j-th subarray is currently open or closed.\n    # Let's define two DP states for each step i (from 0 to n-1) and count of subarrays j (from 1 to k):\n    # dp_open[j]: Max strength using j subarrays, where the j-th subarray is currently open (includes nums[i]).\n    # dp_close[j]: Max strength using j subarrays, where the j-th subarray is closed (ends before i).\n    \n    # Initialize with negative infinity\n    NEG_INF = float('-inf')\n    \n    # dp_close[j] means we have completed j subarrays, and the last one ended before current position.\n    # dp_open[j] means we have started the j-th subarray and it is currently active including current element.\n    \n    # We can optimize space since we only need previous column.\n    # But given constraints n*k <= 10^6, O(n*k) space is acceptable (10^6 integers ~ 8MB).\n    \n    # Let's use 1-based indexing for j (number of subarrays)\n    # dp_close[j]: max strength after completing j subarrays, last subarray ended strictly before current index i.\n    # dp_open[j]: max strength where the j-th subarray is currently being extended to include nums[i].\n    \n    # Base case:\n    # Before processing any numbers, we have 0 subarrays.\n    # dp_close[0] = 0\n    # All other dp values are -inf\n    \n    # Precompute prefix sums for quick subarray sum calculation? \n    # Actually, when extending an open subarray, we just add nums[i].\n    # When starting a new subarray, we take dp_close[j-1] + nums[i].\n    \n    # Coefficients: The j-th subarray (1-indexed) has coefficient C_j = (-1)^(j+1) * (k - j + 1)\n    # Since k is odd:\n    # j=1: coeff = 1 * k = k\n    # j=2: coeff = -1 * (k-1) = -(k-1)\n    # j=3: coeff = 1 * (k-2) = k-2\n    # ...\n    # j=k: coeff = 1 * 1 = 1\n    \n    # Let's compute coefficients\n    coeffs = []\n    for j in range(1, k + 1):\n        sign = 1 if (j % 2 == 1) else -1\n        c = sign * (k - j + 1)\n        coeffs.append(c)\n    \n    # Initialize DP tables\n    # close[j] corresponds to dp_close[j]\n    # open[j] corresponds to dp_open[j]\n    # We only care about j from 1 to k.\n    \n    close = [NEG_INF] * (k + 1)\n    open_ = [NEG_INF] * (k + 1)\n    \n    close[0] = 0\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        val = nums[i]\n        # We need to update based on previous state.\n        # Create temporary copies or iterate carefully.\n        # Since open_[j] depends on open_[j] (previous step) and close[j-1] (previous step).\n        # close[j] depends on close[j] (previous step) and open_[j] (previous step).\n        \n        # To avoid using updated values from current i for other j's in same i, we compute new values.\n        new_close = [NEG_INF] * (k + 1)\n        new_open = [NEG_INF] * (k + 1)\n        \n        for j in range(1, k + 1):\n            c = coeffs[j-1]\n            \n            # Option 1: Start a new j-th subarray at i.\n            # This requires having completed j-1 subarrays before i.\n            # Contribution: c * val\n            if close[j-1] != NEG_INF:\n                start_val = close[j-1] + c * val\n                if start_val > new_open[j]:\n                    new_open[j] = start_val\n            \n            # Option 2: Extend the existing j-th subarray.\n            # This requires the j-th subarray was already open at i-1.\n            # Contribution: c * val\n            if open_[j] != NEG_INF:\n                extend_val = open_[j] + c * val\n                if extend_val > new_open[j]:\n                    new_open[j] = extend_val\n            \n            # Now for close[j]:\n            # Option 3: Close the j-th subarray at i-1.\n            # This means the j-th subarray ended before i. The value is whatever open_[j] was at i-1?\n            # No, close[j] at step i means we have finalized j subarrays, and the last one ended at or before i-1.\n            # Actually, standard definition:\n            # close[j] at end of step i: max strength using j disjoint subarrays within nums[0...i], where the j-th subarray ends at or before i.\n            # open[j] at end of step i: max strength using j disjoint subarrays within nums[0...i], where the j-th subarray ENDS at i.\n            \n            # Let's refine definitions:\n            # close[j]: Max strength with j subarrays selected from nums[0...i], and the j-th subarray has ended at some index <= i.\n            #           Effectively, we are \"free\" to start a new subarray at i+1.\n            # open[j]: Max strength with j subarrays selected from nums[0...i], and the j-th subarray ends EXACTLY at i.\n            \n            # Transitions for i:\n            # For open[j]:\n            #   1. Start new subarray j at i: close[j-1] (at i-1) + c * val\n            #   2. Extend subarray j from i-1: open[j] (at i-1) + c * val\n            \n            # For close[j]:\n            #   1. The j-th subarray ended at i-1 (or earlier): close[j] (at i-1)\n            #      Wait, if close[j] at i-1 means j-th subarray ended <= i-1, then at i, it still ends <= i-1 <= i. So close[j] carries over.\n            #   2. The j-th subarray ends exactly at i: open[j] (at i-1's calculation? No, open[j] at i is calculated above).\n            #      So close[j] at i can also be open[j] at i.\n            \n            # So:\n            # new_open[j] = max(\n            #     close[j-1] + c * val,\n            #     open_[j] + c * val\n            # )\n            \n            # new_close[j] = max(\n            #     close[j],       # Carry forward: j-th subarray ended <= i-1, so definitely <= i\n            #     new_open[j]     # j-th subarray ends exactly at i\n            # )\n            \n            # Note: close[0] is always 0.\n            \n            # Calculate new_open[j]\n            cand_start = close[j-1] + c * val if close[j-1] != NEG_INF else NEG_INF\n            cand_extend = open_[j] + c * val if open_[j] != NEG_INF else NEG_INF\n            new_open[j] = max(cand_start, cand_extend)\n            \n            # Calculate new_close[j]\n            cand_carry = close[j] if close[j] != NEG_INF else NEG_INF\n            cand_end_now = new_open[j] if new_open[j] != NEG_INF else NEG_INF\n            new_close[j] = max(cand_carry, cand_end_now)\n        \n        close = new_close\n        open_ = new_open\n        \n    return close[k]\n```",
