 ascending order.\n    # Let's try to keep a subset of characters whose frequencies are all in some range [x, x+k].\n    # We want to maximize sum of kept frequencies.\n    \n    # Alternative DP or greedy:\n    # Since N is up to 10^5, but unique characters are at most 26.\n    # So we have at most 26 frequencies.\n    # We can iterate over all possible minimum frequencies m in the kept set.\n    # The maximum allowed frequency is m + k.\n    # For each original frequency f:\n    #   if f < m: we cannot keep this character (must delete all) -> keep 0\n    #   if f > m + k: we reduce to m + k -> keep m + k\n    #   if m <= f <= m + k: we keep f -> keep f\n    # Sum these up. Maximize over all valid m.\n    \n    # What are the candidates for m?\n    # m can be 0 (delete everything? no, we can always keep at least one char type with freq reduced appropriately)\n    # Actually m=0 means we delete all characters with freq < 0 (none) and cap others at k.\n    # But if m=0, then freq 0 means we don't include that character.\n    # Candidates for m: all unique frequencies present, and possibly 0.\n    # Also, m could be higher than any freq? No, if m > max_freq, then all chars have f < m, so we delete everything. Sum=0. Not optimal unless word is empty.\n    \n    unique_freqs = sorted(set(freqs))\n    candidates_m = set(unique_freqs)\n    candidates_m.add(0)\n    \n    max_kept = 0\n    \n    for m in candidates_m:\n        current_kept = 0\n        for f in freqs:\n            if f < m:\n                # Must delete all instances of this character\n                current_kept += 0\n            elif f > m + k:\n                # Reduce to m + k\n                current_kept += (m + k)\n            else:\n                # Keep as is\n                current_kept += f\n        if current_kept > max_kept:\n            max_kept = current_kept\n            \n    return len(word) - max_kept\n```",
