      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom itertools import permutations\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    \n    try:\n        H = int(next(iterator))\n        W = int(next(iterator))\n        \n        A = []\n        for r in range(H):\n            row = []\n            for c in range(W):\n                row.append(int(next(iterator)))\n            A.append(row)\n            \n        B = []\n        for r in range(H):\n            row = []\n            for c in range(W):\n                row.append(int(next(iterator)))\n            B.append(row)\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    # Check if multisets of rows in A match multisets of rows in B\n    # And multisets of columns in A match multisets of columns in B?\n    # Actually, we can permute rows and columns independently.\n    # Let's find a permutation of rows P and a permutation of columns Q such that\n    # A[P[i]][Q[j]] == B[i][j] for all i, j.\n    # The cost is (number of swaps to get P) + (number of swaps to get Q).\n    # Number of swaps to get a permutation is N - number of cycles in the permutation.\n    \n    # However, H and W are small (<= 5). We can brute force all permutations of rows and columns.\n    # Total permutations: 5! * 5! = 120 * 120 = 14400. This is very small.\n    \n    def get_min_swaps(perm, n):\n        # Calculate minimum adjacent swaps to reach 'perm' from identity [0, 1, ..., n-1]\n        # This is equivalent to the number of inversions if we were sorting? \n        # No, adjacent swaps to transform identity to perm is the number of inversions of the inverse permutation?\n        # Actually, the minimum number of adjacent swaps to sort a permutation P is the number of inversions in P.\n        # Here we want to transform Identity to Perm. That is the same as transforming Perm to Identity via adjacent swaps.\n        # So it's the number of inversions in Perm.\n        count = 0\n        arr = list(range(n))\n        # We want to reorder arr to match perm.\n        # Let's just count inversions in perm itself relative to sorted order?\n        # Wait. If we have permutation p, the min adjacent swaps to sort it is inversions(p).\n        # Here, we start with rows 0..H-1. We want to end up with rows perm[0], perm[1]...\n        # This is equivalent to applying the inverse permutation to the indices?\n        # Let's use a simpler approach: BFS or just count inversions of the permutation array.\n        # If target arrangement is perm, then element at index i in the new grid comes from old grid at index perm[i].\n        # So the permutation vector P = [p0, p1, ..., pH-1] means new_row_i = old_row_{pi}.\n        # To achieve this from identity [0, 1, ...] using adjacent swaps, the number of swaps is the number of inversions in P.\n        \n        inversions = 0\n        for i in range(len(arr)):\n            for j in range(i + 1, len(arr)):\n                if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                    inversions += 1\n        # Wait, this counts inversions in identity? No.\n        # Let's trace. Target permutation P.\n        # Start: [0, 1, 2]\n        # Target: [2, 0, 1]\n        # Swaps needed: \n        # [0, 1, 2] -> swap(1,2) -> [0, 2, 1] -> swap(0,1) -> [2, 0, 1]. 2 swaps.\n        # Inversions in [2, 0, 1]: (2,0), (2,1). Count = 2. Correct.\n        # So yes, it is the number of inversions in the permutation list P.\n        \n        inv_count = 0\n        for i in range(len(P)):\n            for j in range(i + 1, len(P)):\n                if P[i] > P[j]:\n                    inv_count += 1\n        return inv_count\n\n    # But wait, the problem asks for min operations.\n    # Operations are swapping adjacent rows or adjacent columns.\n    # These commute. Row swaps don't affect column order, col swaps don't affect row order.\n    # So we can optimize rows and columns independently.\n    \n    best_ops = float('inf')\n    found = False\n    \n    # Precompute row permutations and their costs\n    row_perms = list(permutations(range(H)))\n    row_costs = {}\n    for p in row_perms:\n        # p is a tuple representing the target order of original rows.\n        # e.g., if p=(1, 0, 2), it means new row 0 is old row 1, etc.\n        # Cost is inversions in p.\n        inv = 0\n        for i in range(H):\n            for j in range(i + 1, H):\n                if p[i] > p[j]:\n                    inv += 1\n        row_costs[p] = inv\n        \n    # Precompute col permutations and their costs\n    col_perms = list(permutations(range(W)))\n    col_costs = {}\n    for q in col_perms:\n        inv = 0\n        for i in range(W):\n            for j in range(i + 1, W):\n                if q[i] > q[j]:\n                    inv += 1\n        col_costs[q] = inv\n\n    # Iterate over all combinations\n    for rp in row_perms:\n        for cp in col_perms:\n            # Check if applying row perm rp and col perm cp to A yields B\n            # New A'[i][j] = A[rp[i]][cp[j]]\n            match = True\n            for i in range(H):\n                for j in range(W):\n                    if A[rp[i]][cp[j]] != B[i][j]:\n                        match = False\n                        break\n                if not match:\n                    break\n            \n            if match:\n                ops = row_costs[rp] + col_costs[cp]\n                if ops < best_ops:\n                    best_ops = ops\n                found = True\n\n    if found:\n        print(best_ops)\n    else:\n        print(-1)\n\nsolve()\n```",
