 \"BB\"s.\n    # Sequence: AA, BB, AA, BB ...\n    # Length 2*(a+b).\n    # We can insert all c \"AB\"s.\n    # Where?\n    # After any \"BB\" (before an \"AA\" or \"AB\").\n    # Before any \"AA\" (after a \"BB\" or \"AB\").\n    # Essentially, \"AB\"s can be placed in any slot that accepts a block starting with 'A' after a block ending with 'B'.\n    # In the AA-BB chain, every transition is BB->AA.\n    # BB ends B. AA starts A.\n    # We can insert \"AB\" here: BB, AB, AA.\n    # Also \"AB\" ends B, so another \"AB\" can follow: BB, AB, AB, AA.\n    # So we can dump all c \"AB\"s into any BB->AA gap.\n    # Also, can we add to the ends?\n    # Start: Needs block starting with A. \"AA\" or \"AB\".\n    # If we start with \"AB\", it needs no predecessor.\n    # End: Needs block ending with B. \"BB\" or \"AB\".\n    # If we end with \"AB\", it needs no successor.\n    \n    # So if a == b:\n    # We have a chain of a+b blocks.\n    # We can prepend all c \"AB\"s?\n    # AB...AB, AA, BB...\n    # Last AB ends B. First AA starts A. OK.\n    # Can we append all c \"AB\"s?\n    # ...BB, AB...AB\n    # Last BB ends B. First AB starts A. OK.\n    # But we only have z \"AB\"s total.\n    # We can split them?\n    # Actually, since \"AB\" can follow \"BB\" and precede \"AA\", and \"AB\" can follow \"AB\" and precede \"AB\"...\n    # \"AB\" acts as a perfect filler between B-ending and A-starting contexts.\n    # The entire AA-BB chain is surrounded by \"virtual\" B-end before start and A-start after end?\n    # Start of chain is AA (starts A). Preceded by nothing.\n    # End of chain is BB (ends B). Followed by nothing.\n    # We can put all z \"AB\"s at the start: AB* z, then AA...\n    # Or all at the end: ...BB, then AB* z.\n    # Or mix.\n    # Regardless, we can use ALL z \"AB\"s.\n    # Total blocks = x + y + z.\n    # Length = 2 * (x + y + z).\n    \n    # If a != b:\n    # Assume a > b.\n    # We have b \"BB\"s. Each \"BB\" (except maybe the first) needs a preceding \"AA\".\n    # We have a \"AA\"s.\n    # Max \"BB\"s we can support is a + 1 (if we start with BB).\n    # But we only have b \"BB\"s.\n    # Since a > b, we have enough \"AA\"s to support all \"BB\"s, even if we start with BB.\n    # However, \"AA\" is the bottleneck for \"BB\" successors? No, \"BB\" is the bottleneck for \"AA\" predecessors?\n    # Let's look at the chain structure again.\n    # AA, BB, AA, BB ...\n    # If a > b, we have extra \"AA\"s.\n    # We can form b pairs of (AA, BB).\n    # We have a - b extra \"AA\"s.\n    # Can we use them?\n    # We can put one extra \"AA\" at the end: ... BB, AA.\n    # This uses 1 extra \"AA\".\n    # Can we put more?\n    # ... BB, AA, AA -> Invalid.\n    # So we can use at most b + 1 \"AA\"s if we end with AA?\n    # Or b \"AA\"s if we end with BB?\n    # Actually, if we have a > b, we can use all b \"BB\"s.\n    # We can use up to b + 1 \"AA\"s.\n    # Since a >= b + 1 (if a > b and integers), we can use b + 1 \"AA\"s.\n    # The remaining a - (b + 1) \"AA\"s cannot be used in the AA-BB core.\n    # Can we use them with \"AB\"s?\n    # \"AB\" starts with A.\n    # We can have a sequence of \"AB\"s.\n    # AB, AB, ...\n    # This group ends in B.\n    # It can be followed by an \"AA\".\n    # So: [ABs], AA, BB, AA, BB ...\n    # The first block in the core is AA.\n    # Preceding it with \"AB\" is valid.\n    # So we can dump all z \"AB\"s before the first \"AA\".\n    # This doesn't help use extra \"AA\"s.\n    \n    # What if we put \"AB\"s between \"AA\"s?\n    # AA, AB, AA?\n    # AA ends A. AB starts A. \"AAAB\" -> Invalid.\n    # So \"AB\" cannot follow \"AA\".\n    \n    # What if we put \"AB\"s after \"BB\"?\n    # BB, AB, AA.\n    # This is part of the standard flow.\n    \n    # So, if a > b:\n    # We use all b \"BB\"s.\n    # We use min(a, b + 1) \"AA\"s.\n    # Since a > b, min(a, b+1) is b + 1.\n    # So we use b + 1 \"AA\"s.\n    # We use all z \"AB\"s.\n    # Total blocks = b + (b + 1) + z = 2*b +