    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    \n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n        M = int(next(iterator))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    # Adjacency matrix for direct relationships\n    # stronger[i][j] = True means i is stronger than j\n    stronger = [[False] * (N + 1) for _ in range(N + 1)]\n    \n    for _ in range(M):\n        try:\n            u = int(next(iterator))\n            v = int(next(iterator))\n            stronger[u][v] = True\n        except StopIteration:\n            break\n            \n    # Compute transitive closure using Floyd-Warshall algorithm\n    # If A > B and B > C, then A > C\n    for k in range(1, N + 1):\n        for i in range(1, N + 1):\n            for j in range(1, N + 1):\n                if i != j and j != k and i != k:\n                    if stronger[i][k] and stronger[k][j]:\n                        stronger[i][j] = True\n                        \n    # A person X is the strongest if they are stronger than everyone else.\n    # Let's count how many people each person is stronger than.\n    # If a person is stronger than N-1 others, they are a candidate for being the strongest.\n    # However, we need to check if there is exactly one such person.\n    # Note: It's possible for multiple people to be stronger than N-1 others if the graph isn't fully connected in a total order sense locally, \n    # but the problem states \"There is at least one way to determine superiorities...\".\n    # Actually, if person A is stronger than everyone else, and person B is stronger than everyone else,\n    # then A must be stronger than B and B must be stronger than A, which is impossible since they are distinct.\n    # So there can be at most one person who is strictly stronger than all other N-1 people based on the known relations.\n    # Wait, the input only gives partial information. We need to deduce if the strongest person is UNIQUELY determined.\n    # The condition \"uniquely determined\" means that across ALL valid completions of the tournament graph consistent with the given edges,\n    # the strongest person is the same.\n    # Or simpler: Can we identify a person who MUST be the strongest?\n    # A person X is the strongest if X > Y for all Y != X.\n    # From the transitive closure, if stronger[X][Y] is True for all Y != X, then X is a candidate.\n    # Is it possible that no one has stronger[X][Y] True for all Y? Yes, if information is missing.\n    # But the problem asks: \"Can you determine the strongest programmer... If you can, print the person's number. Otherwise... print -1.\"\n    # Sample 2: 1>3, 2>3. Transitive closure doesn't add anything. \n    # 1 is stronger than 3. 1 is not stronger than 2 (unknown).\n    # 2 is stronger than 3. 2 is not stronger than 1 (unknown).\n    # 3 is stronger than nobody.\n    # Possible scenarios:\n    # 1 > 2 > 3 -> Strongest is 1.\n    # 2 > 1 > 3 -> Strongest is 2.\n    # Both are consistent with inputs. So answer is -1.\n    \n    # How to check if the strongest is unique?\n    # The strongest person must be able to reach all other nodes in the dominance graph.\n    # Let's find all nodes that can reach all other nodes in the current transitive closure.\n    # Let Candidates be the set of persons X such that for all Y != X, stronger[X][Y] is True.\n    \n    candidates = []\n    for x in range(1, N + 1):\n        is_stronger_than_all = True\n        for y in range(1, N + 1):\n            if x == y:\n                continue\n            if not stronger[x][y]:\n                is_stronger_than_all = False\n                break\n        if is_stronger_than_all:\n            candidates.append(x)\n            \n    # If there are no candidates, it means we don't have enough info to say anyone is definitely stronger than everyone.\n    # In this case, the strongest person is not uniquely determined?\n    # Actually, if there are no candidates, it implies that for every person X, there is some person Y such that we don't know X > Y.\n    # This could mean the true strongest person is unknown.\n    # However, consider the constraints: \"There is at least one way to determine superiorities...\".\n    # This implies a total ordering exists.\n    # If we have candidates, can we have more than one?\n    # If X is stronger than all Y, and Z is stronger than all W, then X must be stronger than Z and Z stronger than X. Contradiction.\n    # So there can be at most 1 candidate in the transitive closure of the given facts.\n    # Why? Because if X is stronger than all others, then X > Z. If Z is stronger than all others, then Z > X.\n    # Since the relation is antisymmetric (exactly one holds for distinct pairs), X > Z and Z > X cannot both be true.\n    # Thus, if the transitive closure shows X > Z, then Z cannot be stronger than X, so Z cannot be a candidate (since Z must be stronger than X to be a candidate).\n    # Therefore, there is at most 1 candidate.\n    \n    # If there is exactly 1 candidate, is the answer that candidate?\n    # Yes, because that candidate is proven to be stronger than everyone else based on the given information and transitivity.\n    # Since the strongest programmer MUST be stronger than everyone else, and this person X is stronger than everyone else,\n    # X must be the strongest. And since no one else can be stronger than everyone else, X is the unique strongest.\n    \n    # If there are 0 candidates, does it mean -1?\n    # Not necessarily. It might just mean we haven't derived the full order.\n    # But wait, if the strongest person P exists in ANY valid completion, P must be stronger than everyone.\n    # If our transitive closure doesn't show P > Q for some Q, then Q > P is possible in some completion.\n    # If P > Q is NOT derivable, then it's possible that Q > P.\n    # If Q > P, then P is not the strongest.\n    # So if we have 0 candidates, it means for every person X, there is someone Y such that X > Y is not proven.\n    # This allows for scenarios where different people are the strongest.\n    # Example 2: 1>3, 2>3. No one is proven stronger than everyone.\n    # Candidate set is empty. Answer -1. Correct.\n    \n    # Example 1: 1>2, 2>3. Transitive: 1>3.\n    # 1 is stronger than 2 and 3. Candidate: [1].\n    # 2 is stronger than 3, but not 1. Not candidate.\n    # 3 is stronger than none. Not candidate.\n    # Answer 1. Correct.\n    \n    if len(candidates) == 1:\n        print(candidates[0])\n    else:\n        print(-1)\n\nsolve()\n```",
