f i+1 <= L-1, then both are outside or left boundary. Wait.\n    #      The flipped range is [L-1, R-1].\n    #      S[i] is flipped if L-1 <= i <= R-1.\n    #      S[i+1] is flipped if L-1 <= i+1 <= R-1 => L-2 <= i <= R-2.\n    #      \n    #      Case 1: Both flipped. i >= L-1 AND i+1 <= R-1 => i in [L-1, R-2]. D[i] unchanged.\n    #      Case 2: Neither flipped. i < L-1 AND i+1 < L-1 => i+1 <= L-2 => i <= L-3. Or i > R-1 AND i+1 > R-1 => i >= R.\n    #              Actually, if i < L-1, then i+1 <= L-1. \n    #              If i+1 < L-1, then neither is flipped.\n    #              If i+1 == L-1, then S[i] is not flipped, S[i+1] is flipped. So D[i] flips.\n    #      Case 3: Only S[i] flipped. i in [L-1, R-1] and i+1 not in [L-1, R-1].\n    #              i+1 > R-1 => i >= R. But i <= R-1. So i=R-1.\n    #              At i = R-1, S[R-1] is flipped, S[R] is not. D[R-1] flips.\n    #      Case 4: Only S[i+1] flipped. i not in [L-1, R-1] and i+1 in [L-1, R-1].\n    #              i < L-1 and i+1 >= L-1 => i+1 = L-1 => i = L-2.\n    #              At i = L-2, S[L-2] is not flipped, S[L-1] is. D[L-2] flips.\n    \n    #    Summary of D array changes when flipping S[L-1...R-1]:\n    #    - D[i] for i in [L-1, R-2] remains unchanged.\n    #    - D[L-2] flips (if L-2 >= 0).\n    #    - D[R-1] flips (if R-1 < N-1).\n    #    - All other D[i] remain unchanged.\n    \n    #    So, a range flip on S[L...R] (1-indexed) corresponds to:\n    #    - Toggle D[L-2] if L > 1.\n    #    - Toggle D[R-1] if R < N.\n    #    - The \"middle\" part of D doesn't change value, but wait... does the query type 2 depend on D?\n    #      Yes, Type 2 asks if S[L...R] is good. This is equivalent to checking if D[i]==1 for all i in [L-1, R-2].\n    #      Note: If L=R, the range of D is empty, so it's trivially good.\n    \n    #    So we need a data structure that supports:\n    #    1. Point update on D: flip D[k] (0 -> 1, 1 -> 0).\n    #    2. Range query on D: check if all values in D[l...r] are 1.\n    \n    #    We can use a Segment Tree or a Fenwick Tree (Binary Indexed Tree).\n    #    Since we need to check if ALL are 1, we can store the sum of D[i] in the segment tree nodes.\n    #    If sum(l, r) == (r - l + 1), then all are 1.\n    #    Wait, D[i] is 0 or 1. Sum is the count of 1s.\n    #    So we need:\n    #    - Point update: add 1 or subtract 1 to D[k].\n    #    - Range sum query.\n    \n    #    Let's maintain an array D of size N-1.\n    #    Initialize D[i] = S[i] ^ S[i+1].\n    #    Build a Segment Tree / BIT over D.\n    \n    #    BIT supports:\n    #    - update(index, delta): add delta to element at index.\n    #    - query(index): prefix sum up to index.\n    #    - range_sum(l, r) = query(r) - query(l-1).\n    \n    #    Indices for D are 0 to N-2.\n    #    BIT is usually 1-indexed. Let's map D[i] to BIT index i+1.\n    \n    if N == 1:\n        # D is empty.\n        # Any substring of length 1 is good.\n        # Queries will only be type 2 with L=R=1.\n        out_lines = []\n        for _ in range(Q):\n            t = next(iterator)\n            L = int(next(iterator))\n            R = int(next(iterator))\n            if t == '2':\n                out_lines.append(\"Yes\")\n        print('\\n'.join(out_lines))\n        return\n\n    # Initialize D\n    D = [0] * (N - 1)\n    for i in range(N - 1):\n        D[i] = S[i] ^ S[i+1]\n        \n    # BIT Implementation\n    # Size N-1. 1-indexed BIT of size N-1.\n    bit_size = N - 1\n    bit = [0] * (bit_size + 1)\n    \n    def bit_update(idx, val):\n        # idx is 0-based index into D\n        # convert to 1-based\n        i = idx + 1\n        while i <= bit_size:\n            bit[i] += val\n            i += i & (-i)\n            \n    def bit_query(idx):\n        # returns sum from D[0] to D[idx] (inclusive)\n        # idx is 0-based\n        i = idx + 1\n        s = 0\n        while i > 0:\n            s += bit[i]\n            i -= i & (-i)\n        return s\n        \n    def range_sum(l, r):\n        if l > r:\n            return 0\n        res = bit_query(r)\n        if l > 0:\n            res -= bit_query(l - 1)\n        return res\n\n    # Build BIT\n    for i in range(bit_size):\n        if D[i] == 1:\n            bit_update(i, 1)\n            \n    out_lines = []\n    \n    for _ in range(Q):\n        t = next(iterator)\n        L = int(next(iterator))\n        R = int(next(iterator))\n        \n        if t == '1':\n            # Flip S[L-1 ... R-1]\n            # Update D[L-2] if L > 1\n            if L > 1:\n                idx = L - 2\n                # Current value D[idx]\n                # We want to flip it.\n                # If it was 1, it becomes 0 (delta -1)\n                # If it was 0, it becomes 1 (delta +1)\n                # We can just query the current value from BIT? \n                # Or maintain D array separately.\n                # Let's maintain D array to know current value.\n                if D[idx] == 1:\n                    bit_update(idx, -1)\n                    D[idx] = 0\n                else:\n                    bit_update(idx, 1)\n                    D[idx] = 1\n                    \n            # Update D[R-1] if R < N\n            if R < N:\n                idx = R - 1\n                if D[idx] == 1:\n                    bit_update(idx, -1)\n                    D[idx] = 0\n                else:\n                    bit_update(idx, 1)\n                    D[idx] = 1\n                    \n        else:\n            # Query S[L-1 ... R-1]\n            if L == R:\n                out_lines.append(\"Yes\")\n            else:\n                # Check D[L-1 ... R-2]\n                l_d = L - 1\n                r_d = R - 2\n                # Length of this D range is r_d - l_d + 1\n                length = r_d - l_d + 1\n                total_ones = range_sum(l_d, r_d)\n                if total_ones == length:\n                    out_lines.append(\"Yes\")\n                else:\n          