he boundary, causing the bounding box to shrink.\n    # Specifically, if the missing cookie was at (min_r, c) for some c, then the new min_r is min_r + 1 (if there were other cookies in min_r+1).\n    \n    # Let's calculate the area difference.\n    area_bbox = (max_r - min_r + 1) * (max_c - min_c + 1)\n    diff = area_bbox - current_count\n    \n    if diff == 1:\n        # The original rectangle is exactly the bounding box of the current cookies.\n        # Find the '.' inside the bounding box.\n        for r in range(min_r, max_r + 1):\n            for c in range(min_c, max_c + 1):\n                if grid[r][c] == '.':\n                    print(f\"{r+1} {c+1}\")\n                    return\n    else:\n        # The original rectangle is larger.\n        # The missing cookie must be adjacent to the bounding box, extending it.\n        # Since only one cookie is missing, the original rectangle must be formed by expanding the bounding box by 1 in some direction(s)?\n        # Not necessarily by 1. But the area difference tells us how many extra cells are in the original rectangle compared to the current bounding box.\n        # Wait, if the original rectangle is larger, then the cells in the original rectangle but outside the current bounding box MUST be '#'?\n        # No! The only cookie eaten is ONE.\n        # So, all cells in the Original Rectangle are '#' except the eaten one.\n        # Therefore, any cell in the Original Rectangle that is NOT in the Current Bounding Box MUST be '#' (because if it were '.', it would mean another cookie is missing, or it was never a cookie).\n        # But wait, if a cell is outside the Current Bounding Box, it means there are NO '#' there.\n        # So if the Original Rectangle extends beyond the Current Bounding Box, those extended cells must be '#' in the original state.\n        # But they are '.' in the input.\n        # This implies they are the eaten cookie? No, only ONE cookie was eaten.\n        # So if the Original Rectangle is larger than the Current Bounding Box, the cells in the difference region must be the eaten cookie?\n        # But the eaten cookie is just ONE cell.\n        # So the difference region must contain exactly one cell, and that cell is the eaten cookie.\n        # AND all other cells in the Original Rectangle must be '#'.\n        \n        # So, diff must be 1?\n        # Let's check Sample 2.\n        # 3 2\n        # #.\n        # ##\n        # ##\n        # Current #: (0,0), (1,0), (1,1), (2,0), (2,1). Count = 5.\n        # min_r=0, max_r=2, min_c=0, max_c=1.\n        # B_box area = 3*2 = 6.\n        # diff = 6 - 5 = 1.\n        # So diff is 1.\n        \n        # What if the missing cookie is at the corner, e.g., (0,1) in Sample 2.\n        # B_box covers (0,0) to (2,1).\n        # (0,1) is in B_box and is '.'.\n        # So it works.\n        \n        # Is it possible that diff > 1?\n        # Suppose original rect is 3x3 (area 9). One eaten. 8 remain.\n        # If eaten is center (1,1), B_box is still 3x3. Diff = 9-8=1.\n        # If eaten is corner (0,0), remaining '#' are in (0,1)..(2,2).\n        # min_r=0, max_r=2, min_c=1, max_c=2.\n        # B_box is 3x2 (rows 0-2, cols 1-2). Area 6.\n        # Diff = 6 - 8? No, current_count is 8.\n        # Wait, if eaten is (0,0), then row 0 has '#' at col 1,2.\n        # So min_r=0, max_r=2. min_c=1, max_c=2.\n        # B_box area = 3 * 2 = 6.\n        # Current count = 8.\n        # This is impossible because B_box is contained in Original Rect, so B_box area <= Original Area = 9.\n        # But B_box area 6 < 8? No, B_box area is the number of cells in the bounding box.\n        # The current '#' are inside B_box. So current_count <= B_box area.\n        # Here 8 <= 6 is false.\n        # Contradiction.\n        # Why? Because if (0,0) is missing, the '#' at (0,1) and (0,2) exist.\n        # So min_r=0, max_r=2. min_c=1, max_c=2.\n        # The cells in B_box are (0,1),(0,2),(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2)