tes for the left part. They should ideally be <= k. If any is > k, we must reduce it to k (or less, but reducing to k is optimal if we want to minimize distance, unless it's already small). Actually, if nums[i] > k, we pay nums[i]-k to bring it to k. If we bring it below k, we pay more. So target is k.\n    # - The last (n - median_idx) elements (indices median_idx to n-1) are candidates for the right part. They should ideally be >= k. If any is < k, we pay k - nums[i] to bring it to k.\n    # - The element at median_idx is the pivot.\n    \n    # Wait, in Example 1:\n    # Sorted: [2, 5, 5, 6, 8]. median_idx = 2.\n    # Left part indices: 0, 1. Values: 2, 5.\n    # Right part indices: 2, 3, 4. Values: 5, 6, 8.\n    \n    # My previous logic:\n    # Left: 2<=4 (ok), 5>4 -> cost 1.\n    # Right: 5>=4 (ok), 6>=4 (ok), 8>=4 (ok).\n    # Total 1. But median of [2,4,5,6,8] is 5.\n    \n    # The problem is that the element at index 2 in the final array must be k.\n    # In the \"Left\" group, we ensured elements are <= k. In the \"Right\" group, we ensured elements are >= k.\n    # But the element at the boundary (index median_idx) is part of the Right group in my split?\n    # Yes, indices median_idx to n-1.\n    # So nums[median_idx] = 5. It is in the Right group. It is >= 4. So no cost.\n    # But in the final sorted array, if we have [2, 4, 5, 6, 8], the median is 5.\n    # We need the median to be 4.\n    # This implies that the element at index 2 in the final sorted array must be 4.\n    # This requires that there are at least 3 elements <= 4.\n    # Currently, we have 2 (the 2 and the modified 5->4). We need one more.\n    # The next smallest is 5 (at index 2). We must reduce it to 4. Cost 1.\n    # Total cost 1 + 1 = 2.\n    \n    # So the rule is:\n    # - For i from 0 to median_idx: elements must be <= k. If nums[i] > k, cost += nums[i] - k.\n    # - For i from median_idx to n-1: elements must be >= k. If nums[i] < k, cost += k - nums[i].\n    \n    # Let's tes