      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef maxStrength(nums):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the maximum strength of a non-empty group of students.\n    Strength is defined as the product of the scores in the group.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Since the constraints are small (length <= 13), we could use brute force,\n    # but let's think logically.\n    \n    # We want to maximize the product.\n    # Key observations:\n    # 1. Positive numbers always help increase the product (if > 1) or keep it same (if = 1).\n    #    But since we must pick a non-empty group, we need to be careful.\n    # 2. Negative numbers: pairs of negatives make positives. So we should include negative numbers\n    #    in pairs. If there's an odd number of negatives, we might exclude the one with the smallest absolute value\n    #    (i.e., the one closest to zero, which is -1, -2, etc.) to maximize the product.\n    # 3. Zeros: generally bad for product unless all other options yield negative or zero.\n    \n    # Let's sort the array to easily identify negatives, zeros, and positives.\n    nums_sorted = sorted(nums)\n    \n    n = len(nums_sorted)\n    \n    # Count negatives, zeros, and positives\n    negatives = [x for x in nums_sorted if x < 0]\n    zeros = [x for x in nums_sorted if x == 0]\n    positives = [x for x in nums_sorted if x > 0]\n    \n    num_neg = len(negatives)\n    num_zero = len(zeros)\n    num_pos = len(positives)\n    \n    # Case 1: Only one element\n    if n == 1:\n        return nums[0]\n    \n    # If there are no negatives and no positives (only zeros)\n    if num_neg == 0 and num_pos == 0:\n        return 0\n    \n    # Calculate the product of all positive numbers\n    pos_product = 1\n    for p in positives:\n        pos_product *= p\n    \n    # Calculate the product of negative numbers\n    # We want to include an even number of negatives to make them positive.\n    # If num_neg is even, include all.\n    # If num_neg is odd, exclude the negative with the largest value (closest to 0, i.e., least absolute value).\n    # Since negatives are sorted in ascending order (e.g., -5, -4, -3, -2, -1),\n    # the last one (nums_sorted[-1] among negatives) is the largest (closest to 0).\n    \n    neg_product = 1\n    if num_neg % 2 == 0:\n        for neg in negatives:\n            neg_product *= neg\n    else:\n        # Exclude the last negative (largest, closest to 0)\n        for i in range(num_neg - 1):\n            neg_product *= negatives[i]\n    \n    total_product = pos_product * neg_product\n    \n    # Edge case: if total_product is 0, it might be because we had no positives and excluded all negatives?\n    # Let's check specific edge cases.\n    \n    # If there are no positives and only 1 negative and rest zeros?\n    # Example: nums = [-1, 0, 0]\n    # negatives = [-1], positives = [], zeros = [0, 0]\n    # num_neg = 1 (odd), so we exclude the last (and only) negative -> neg_product = 1 (empty product?)\n    # Wait, if we exclude all negatives, neg_product should be 1? No, if we don't pick any negative, the product from negatives is 1.\n    # But we must pick a non-empty group.\n    \n    # Let's reconsider. If num_neg is odd and we exclude one negative, we still have num_neg - 1 negatives.\n    # If num_neg was 1, then after exclusion, we have 0 negatives.\n    # In that case, if there are no positives either, we might be left with only zeros.\n    \n    if num_pos == 0 and num_neg <= 1:\n        # If only 1 negative and no positives, and possibly zeros\n        if num_neg == 1:\n            # The best we can do is pick just that negative if there are no other options that yield better?\n            # But wait, if we have [-1, 0], picking -1 gives -1, picking 0 gives 0. Max is 0.\n            # If we have [-1], answer is -1.\n            # If we have [-1, 0, 0], we can pick [-1] -> -1, or [0] -> 0, or [-1, 0] -> 0. Max is 0.\n            # So if there are zeros, and we can't form a positive product, the max is 0.\n            # Unless there are no zeros, then it's the single negative.\n            if num_zero > 0:\n                return 0\n            else:\n                return negatives[0]\n        else:\n            # num_neg == 0, so only zeros\n            return 0\n            \n    # If the calculated total_product is 0, it might be due to the logic above.\n    # Actually, let's just compute it properly.\n    \n    # Recalculate based on selected elements\n    selected = []\n    selected.extend(positives)\n    if num_neg % 2 == 0:\n        selected.extend(negatives)\n    else:\n        selected.extend(negatives[:-1])\n        \n    if not selected:\n        # This happens if we have no positives and either 0 or 1 negative (and we excluded it)\n        # And possibly zeros.\n        # If we have zeros, return 0.\n        # If we have a single negative left over (because we excluded it? No, if num_neg=1 and odd, we excluded it).\n        # So selected is empty.\n        # Check if there are zeros\n        if num_zero > 0:\n            return 0\n        elif num_neg == 1:\n            # We excluded the only negative. But maybe we should have picked it?\n            # If we pick it, product is negative. If we pick nothing, invalid.\n            # But we can pick a zero instead if available.\n            # If no zeros, and only 1 negative, we must pick that negative.\n            return negatives[0]\n        else:\n            # Should not happen given earlier checks\n            return 0\n    else:\n        result = 1\n        for x in selected:\n            result *= x\n        return result\n```",
