e.\n    # Ideally, we want to convert one piece of weight W[x] into as many pieces as possible on low-weight vertices.\n    \n    # Actually, this looks like a flow or DP problem on the graph structure relative to weights.\n    # Notice that pieces only move from higher weight vertices to lower weight vertices? Not necessarily strictly lower,\n    # but the SUM of weights of destination vertices must be less than source weight.\n    # Since W_i >= 1, if a neighbor has W_y >= W_x, it cannot be part of S unless other neighbors have very small weights?\n    # No, if W_y >= W_x, then including y in S makes sum >= W_y >= W_x, violating strict inequality.\n    # So, a piece can ONLY move to neighbors y with W_y < W_x.\n    # This implies pieces always move from higher weight to strictly lower weight.\n    # Thus, the graph of possible moves is a DAG based on weights.\n    \n    # Let's group vertices by weight.\n    # Max weight is 5000. N is up to 5000.\n    # Pieces on vertices with the minimum weight in the connected component (considering only valid moves) cannot move anywhere.\n    # Let's define a directed graph where an edge exists from u to v if W_v < W_u and u, v are adjacent.\n    # Since W_v < W_w, cycles are impossible.\n    \n    # For a piece on vertex u, it can be replaced by a set of pieces on neighbors v with W_v < W_u such that sum(W_v) < W_u.\n    # To maximize the number of resulting pieces, we should pick neighbors with smallest weights.\n    # Let's sort the neighbors of u by weight.\n    # We want to find the largest subset S of neighbors such that sum(W_v for v in S) < W_u.\n    # Greedy strategy: Pick neighbors with smallest weights first.\n    \n    # Let K_u be the maximum number of pieces we can generate from one piece on u.\n    # Actually, it's not just about the count, because the \"value\" of the new pieces depends on their locations.\n    # Pieces on lower weight vertices can potentially spawn even more pieces.\n    \n    # Let f(u) be the