ls is:\n    # - cr if tr > tc\n    # - cc if tc > tr\n    # - 0 if tr == tc == 0 (initial)\n    # - What if tr == tc != 0? This implies one happened before the other? \n    #   Wait, time indices are unique 1..M. So tr == tc only if both are 0.\n    #   Because each operation gets a unique time stamp.\n    #   So if tr > 0 and tc > 0, they cannot be equal unless they refer to the same operation?\n    #   No, a row op and a col op are different types. They have distinct time stamps.\n    #   So tr == tc is only possible if tr == tc == 0.\n    \n    # So, logic simplifies:\n    # For any row r and col c:\n    # If row_time[r] > col_time[c]: color is row_color[r]\n    # Else: color is col_color[c] (since col_time[c] >= row_time[r], and if equal both 0 -> color 0)\n    \n    # We want to count frequency of each color.\n    #\n    # Let's sum up contributions.\n    #\n    # Contribution from rows:\n    # For each row r, it \"wins\" against all columns c where col_time[c] < row_time[r].\n    # The number of such columns is the count of cols with time < row_time[r].\n    # Let's precompute a frequency array or prefix sum for column times.\n    # Max time is M.\n    #\n    # Let cnt_col_time[t] = number of columns whose last operation was at time t.\n    # Then prefix_cnt_col_time[t] = sum(cnt_col_time[0...t]) = number of cols with time <= t.\n    # Number of cols with time < row_time[r] is prefix_cnt_col_time[row_time[r] - 1].\n    #\n    # Similarly, we can handle columns.\n    # For each col c, it \"wins\" against all rows r where row_time[r] < col_time[c].\n    # Number of such rows is prefix_cnt_row_time[col_time[c] - 1].\n    #\n    # BUT, we must be careful not to double count or miss the \"initial\" cells.\n    # Every cell is either won by its row or its col.\n    # If row_time[r] > col_time[c], row wins.\n    # If col_time[c] > row_time[r], col wins.\n    # If both 0, neither \"won\" via an operation, color 0.\n    #\n    # So:\n    # 1. Count cells won by rows.\n