      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    idx = 0\n    t = int(data[idx])\n    idx += 1\n    results = []\n    for _ in range(t):\n        n = int(data[idx])\n        idx += 1\n        a = []\n        for i in range(n):\n            a.append(int(data[idx]))\n            idx += 1\n        \n        # We need to find the maximum sum of a subarray with alternating parities.\n        # A subarray has alternating parities if for every adjacent pair, one is even and the other is odd.\n        # This means we can break the array into segments where the parity alternates.\n        # Within each such maximal segment, we want to find the maximum subarray sum.\n        # But note: the constraint is on adjacent elements in the subarray having different parity.\n        # So any valid subarray must lie entirely within a \"maximal alternating parity segment\".\n        \n        # Let's identify the maximal contiguous segments where parities alternate.\n        # Then for each such segment, run Kadane's algorithm to find max subarray sum.\n        \n        # First, let's build the segments.\n        # Two adjacent elements a[i], a[i+1] are compatible if (a[i] % 2) != (a[i+1] % 2).\n        # Note: In Python, negative numbers modulo 2 can be tricky. \n        # For parity check, we should use abs(x) % 2 or x & 1.\n        # Actually, (-1) % 2 in Python is 1, which is correct for parity (odd).\n        # (-2) % 2 is 0, which is correct (even).\n        # So x % 2 works fine for checking parity in Python.\n        \n        segments = []\n        if n == 0:\n            results.append(0)\n            continue\n            \n        current_segment = [a[0]]\n        for i in range(1, n):\n            prev_parity = a[i-1] % 2\n            curr_parity = a[i] % 2\n            if prev_parity != curr_parity:\n                current_segment.append(a[i])\n            else:\n                segments.append(current_segment)\n                current_segment = [a[i]]\n        segments.append(current_segment)\n        \n        max_sum = -float('inf')\n        \n        for seg in segments:\n            # Kadane's algorithm on this segment\n            current_max = seg[0]\n            global_max = seg[0]\n            for j in range(1, len(seg)):\n                current_max = max(seg[j], current_max + seg[j])\n                global_max = max(global_max, current_max)\n            if global_max > max_sum:\n                max_sum = global_max\n        \n        results.append(max_sum)\n    \n    for res in results:\n        print(res)\n\nsolve()\n```",
