n).\n        # If a_i is even: cost ? \n        #   If a_i=2 (2^1), need 2^2=4. 2->3 (odd, bad), 2->4 (2^2). Cost 2.\n        #   If a_i=4 (2^2), already has 2s. But if we need MORE, 4->5 (bad), 4->6 (2*3, still 1 factor of 2). 4->8 (2^3). Cost 4.\n        #   This seems expensive.\n        # But if there is ANY odd element, cost is 1.\n        # If all elements are even, then product has many 2s. \n        # If product is not divisible by 2, impossible? No, if all even, product is divisible by 2^n.\n        # So if k=2 and product not div by 2, there MUST be an odd element. Cost 1.\n        \n        # For k=3: need 1 factor of 3.\n        # If any a_i % 3 == 2, cost 1.\n        # Else if any a_i % 3 == 1, cost 2.\n        # (If all a_i % 3 == 0, product div by 3).\n        \n        # For k=5: need 1 factor of 5.\n        # Min cost = min(5 - a_i%5) for all i.\n        \n        # For k=4: need 2 factors of 2.\n        # Option A: One element provides 2 extra factors of 2.\n        # Option B: Two elements provide 1 extra factor of 2 each.\n        \n        # Cost for one element to gain 1 factor of 2:\n        # If a_i is odd: cost 1.\n        # If a_i is even: \n        #   Let v = a_i. We want smallest c>0 such that v+c has more factors of 2 than v.\n        #   Actually, we just need the TOTAL count of 2s in product to increase by 2.\n        \n        # Let's compute min cost to gain 1 factor of 2 from the entire array.\n        # min_cost_1 = min over i of (cost to increase exponent of 2 in a_i by 1 relative to original contribution? No.)\n        # We need delta(total_exponent_2) >= 2.\n        \n        # For each element, what is the cost to increase its exponent of 2 by 1?\n        # If a_i is odd (exp 0): next even is a_i+1 (exp at least 1). Cost 1. Gain 1.\n        # If a_i is 2 (exp 1): next exp 2 is 4. Cost 2. Gain 1.\n        # If a_i is 4 (exp 2): next exp 3 is 8. Cost 4. Gain 1.\n        # If a_i is 6 (exp 1): next exp 2 is ? 6=2*3. 7(0), 8(3). Cost 2. Gain 1.\n        # If a_i is 8 (exp 3): next exp 4 is 16. Cost 8.\n        # If a_i is 10 (exp 1): 10=2*5. 11(0), 12=4*3(exp 2). Cost 2. Gain 1.\n        \n        # So min cost to gain 1 factor of 2 is 1 (if any odd exists).\n        # If all even, min cost is 2 (e.g., 2->4, 6->8, 10->12).\n        \n        # To gain 2 factors of 2:\n        # Case 1: Two separate elements each gain 1.\n        # If there are at least 2 odd elements: cost 1+1=2.\n        # If there is 1 odd element and rest even: cost 1 + min_cost_even_gain_1.\n        # If all even: cost min_cost_even_gain_1 + second_min_cost_even_gain_1.\n        \n        # Case 2: One element gains 2.\n        # Cost for element to gain 2 factors of 2.\n        # Odd: 1->2 (gain 1). 2->3 (loss). 3->4 (gain 2 from 0). Cost 3? \n        #   a_i=1 (exp 0). Target exp 2 (value 4). 1->4 cost 3. Gain 2.\n        #   a_i=3 (exp 0). Target exp 2 (value 4). 3->4 cost 1. Gain 2.\n        # Even: \n        #   a_i=2 (exp 1). Target exp 3 (value 8). 2->8 cost 6. Gain 2.\n        #   a_i=4 (exp 2). Target exp 4 (value 16). Cost 12.\n        #   a_i=6 (exp 1). Target exp 3 (value 8). 6->8 cost 2. Gain 2.\n        #   a_i=8 (exp 3). Target exp 5 (value 32). Cost 24.\n        #   a_i=10 (exp 1). Target exp 3 (value 8). 10->? 11(0), 12(2), 14(1), 16(4). \n        #     10 to 16 cost 6. Gain 3.\n        #     10 to 12 cost 2. Gain 1.\n        # So min cost for one element to gain 2 is 1 (from 3->4) or 2 (from 6->8).\n        \n        # So for k=4:\n        # Ans = min(\n        #   cost_two_separate_gains,\n        #   cost_one_double_gain\n        # )\n        \n        # Let's implement this logic.\n        \n        ans = 0\n        \n        if k == 2:\n            # Need 1 factor of 2.\n            # If any odd, cost 1.\n            if any(x % 2 == 1 for x in a):\n                ans = 1\n            else:\n                # All even. Product divisible by 2^n. Should be div by 2.\n                # But we checked satisfied earlier. If not satisfied, this branch shouldn't happen?\n                # Wait, if all even, product is even. Divisible by 2.\n                # So if k=2 and not satisfied, there MUST be an odd.\n                ans = 1 # Should be covered above.\n                \n        elif k == 3:\n            # Need 1 factor of 3.\n            # Find min cost to get a number divisible by 3.\n            min_c = float('inf')\n            for x in a:\n                rem = x % 3\n                if rem == 0:\n                    continue # Already contributes, but we need MORE?\n                    # If product not div by 3, then NO element is div by 3?\n                    # No, if one is div by 3, product is.\n                    # So if not satisfied, no element is div by 3.\n                if rem == 2:\n                    min_c = min(min_c, 1)\n                elif rem == 1:\n                    min_c = min(min_c, 2)\n            ans = min_c\n            \n        elif k == 5:\n            # Need 1 factor of 5.\n            # No element is div by 5.\n            min_c = float('inf')\n            for x in a:\n                rem = x % 5\n                cost = 5 - rem\n                min_c = min(min_c, cost)\n            ans = min_c\n            \n        elif k == 4:\n            # Need 2 factors of 2.\n            \n            # Calculate cost for each element to gain 1 factor of 2 (delta_exp_2 = 1)\n            # And gain 2 factors of 2 (delta_exp_2 = 2)\n            \n            costs_1 = []\n            costs_2 = []\n            \n            for x in a:\n                # Original exponent of 2 in x\n                def count_2(val):\n                    c = 0\n                    while val > 0 and val % 2 == 0:\n                        c += 1\n                        val //= 2\n                    return c\n                \n                orig_exp = count_2(x)\n                \n                # Find min cost to reach a state where exp >= orig_exp + 1\n                c1 = float('inf')\n                for delta in range(1, 10): # Try up to 9 increments\n                    nx = x + delta\n                    new_exp = count_2(nx)\n                    if new_exp >= orig_exp + 1:\n                        c1 = delta\n                        break\n                \n                # Find min cost to reach a state where exp >= orig_exp + 2\n                c2 = float('inf')\n                for delta in range(1, 10):\n                    nx = x + delta\n                    new_exp = count_2(nx)\n                    if new_exp >= orig_exp + 2:\n                        c2 = delta\n                        break\n                        \n                costs_1.append(c1)\n                costs_2.append(c2)\n                \n            # Option 1: Two separate elements each gain 1\n            costs_1_sorted = sorted(costs_1)\n            opt1 = costs_1_sorted[0] + costs_1_sorted[1] if len(costs_1) >= 2 else float('inf')\n            \n            # Option 2: One element gains 2\n            opt2 = min(costs_2)\n            \n            ans = min(opt1, opt2)\n            \n        results.append(str(ans))\n        \n    print('\\n'.join(results))\n\nsolve()\n```",
