    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_27",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given two integers, n and k.\nAn array of distinct positive integers is called a k-avoiding array if there does not exist any pair of distinct elements that sum to k.\nReturn the minimum possible sum of a k-avoiding array of length n.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: n = 5, k = 4\nOutput: 18\nExplanation: Consider the k-avoiding array [1,2,4,5,6], which has a sum of 18.\nIt can be proven that there is no k-avoiding array with a sum less than 18.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: n = 2, k = 6\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: We can construct the array [1,2], which has a sum of 3.\nIt can be proven that there is no k-avoiding array with a sum less than 3.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= n, k <= 50",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minimumSum(n: int, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the minimum possible sum of a k-avoiding array of length n.\n    A k-avoiding array is an array of distinct positive integers where no two distinct elements sum to k.\n    \"\"\"\n    # We want to pick the smallest n distinct positive integers such that no two sum to k.\n    # Strategy:\n    # Consider pairs (i, k-i). For each pair, we can pick at most one number.\n    # To minimize the sum, we should greedily pick smaller numbers.\n    # Numbers less than k/2 are \"safe\" to pick first because they don't form a sum of k with other small numbers\n    # unless paired with a specific complement. Specifically, if we pick x < k/2, we cannot pick k-x.\n    # If we pick x >= k/2, its complement k-x <= k/2 (if k-x > 0).\n    \n    # Let's iterate through positive integers 1, 2, 3, ... and decide whether to include them.\n    # We maintain a set of included numbers.\n    # For each number i starting from 1:\n    #   Check if including i would violate the condition.\n    #   The condition is violated if there exists some j already in the set such that i + j == k.\n    #   This means j = k - i must already be in the set.\n    #   So, if (k - i) is already in our chosen set, we cannot choose i.\n    #   Otherwise, we can choose i.\n    \n    chosen = []\n    chosen_set = set()\n    \n    i = 1\n    while len(chosen) < n:\n        # If k - i is already in chosen_set, we cannot pick i because i + (k-i) = k\n        if (k - i) in chosen_set:\n            i += 1\n            continue\n        \n        # Pick i\n        chosen.append(i)\n        chosen_set.add(i)\n        i += 1\n        \n    return sum(chosen)\n```",
