      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minSum(nums1: list[int], nums2: list[int]) -> int:\n    sum1 = sum(nums1)\n    zeros1 = nums1.count(0)\n    \n    sum2 = sum(nums2)\n    zeros2 = nums2.count(0)\n    \n    # If there are no zeros in either array, check if sums are already equal\n    if zeros1 == 0 and zeros2 == 0:\n        return sum1 if sum1 == sum2 else -1\n    \n    # If one array has zeros and the other doesn't\n    if zeros1 == 0 and zeros2 > 0:\n        # We need to fill zeros2 with positive integers such that sum2 equals sum1\n        # Minimum possible sum2 is sum2 + zeros2 (each zero becomes at least 1)\n        min_sum2 = sum2 + zeros2\n        if min_sum2 > sum1:\n            return -1\n        else:\n            return sum1\n    \n    if zeros2 == 0 and zeros1 > 0:\n        # We need to fill zeros1 with positive integers such that sum1 equals sum2\n        # Minimum possible sum1 is sum1 + zeros1\n        min_sum1 = sum1 + zeros1\n        if min_sum1 > sum2:\n            return -1\n        else:\n            return sum2\n    \n    # Both arrays have zeros\n    # Minimum possible sum for nums1 is sum1 + zeros1\n    # Minimum possible sum for nums2 is sum2 + zeros2\n    min_sum1 = sum1 + zeros1\n    min_sum2 = sum2 + zeros2\n    \n    # The equal sum must be at least max(min_sum1, min_sum2)\n    # Since both have zeros, we can always increase the sum arbitrarily by adding larger positive integers to the zeros.\n    # So we just need to find the maximum of the two minimums.\n    return max(min_sum1, min_sum2)\n```",
