discarded by any card with higher A (since all such cards have cost >= min_cost > C_y? No wait.)\n    #       Wait. If C_y < min_cost, then for all previous cards x, C_x >= min_cost > C_y.\n    #       So C_x > C_y. Thus no x with A_x > A_y has C_x < C_y. So y remains.\n    #   else:\n    #       C_y >= min_cost. There exists some previous card x with C_x = min_cost <= C_y.\n    #       Since A_x > A_y and C_x <= C_y (and distinct, so C_x < C_y), y can be discarded.\n    #       So y does not remain.\n    #   Update min_cost = min(min_cost, C_y). But we only care about cards that remain? \n    #   Actually, even discarded cards might affect future cards? \n    #   No. The problem says \"repeat the operation until it can no longer be performed\".\n    #   It can be proved that the set of remaining cards is uniquely determined.\n    #   The condition for y to remain is that there is NO x with A_x > A_y and C_x < C_y.\n    #   This condition depends only on the initial set of cards.\n    #   So we just need to check for each card y, whether there exists ANY card x in the original set with A_x > A_y and C_x < C_y.\n    #   If yes, y is discarded. If no, y remains.\n    \n    # So the algorithm:\n    # 1. Sort cards by A descending.\n    # 2. Track the minimum cost seen so far among cards with higher A.\n    # 3. For each card y in this order, if C_y < min_cost_so_far, then y remains.\n    #    Otherwise, y is discarded.\n    # 4. Update min_cost_so_far with C_y.\n    \n    cards.sort(key=lambda x: x[0], reverse=True)\n    \n    remaining_indices = []\n    min_cost = float('inf')\n    \n    for a, c, orig_idx in cards:\n        if c < min_cost:\n            remaining_indices.append(orig_idx)\n        min_cost = min(min_cost, c)\n    \n    remaining_indices.sort()\n    m = len(remaining_indices)\n    \n    print(m)\n    print(' '.join(map(str, remaining_indices)))\n\nsolve()\n```",
