      "generated_answer": "I'll solve this problem by grouping beans by color and finding the minimum deliciousness for each color, then taking the maximum of these minimums.\n\n**Approach:**\n1. Read all beans with their deliciousness and color\n2. Group beans by color\n3. For each color, find the minimum deliciousness\n4. The answer is the maximum of these minimums\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef solve():\n    input = sys.stdin.readline\n    N = int(input())\n    \n    color_to_beans = defaultdict(list)\n    for _ in range(N):\n        A, C = map(int, input().split())\n        color_to_beans[C].append(A)\n    \n    answer = 0\n    for color, beans in color_to_beans.items():\n        min_deliciousness = min(beans)\n        answer = max(answer, min_deliciousness)\n    \n    print(answer)\n\nsolve()\n```\n\nWait, let me reconsider the problem more carefully. \n\nLooking at sample 1:\n- Color 1: deliciousness 100, 40 → minimum is 40\n- Color 5: deliciousness 20, 30 → minimum is 20\n- Answer: max(40, 20) = 40\n\nYes, my interpretation is correct. The problem is straightforward: for each color, since beans of the same color are indistinguishable, the minimum possible deliciousness when picking that color is the minimum among beans of that color. We then maximize this over all colors.\n\nThe code is correct and efficient:\n- Time complexity: O(N) for reading and grouping\n- Space complexity: O(N) for storing the groups\n\nLet me double-check with sample 2:\n- Color 1: 78, 3 → min = 3\n- Color 2: 17, 99, 100 → min = 17\n- Color 3: 68, 10 → min = 10\n- Color 4: 92, 82, 35 → min = 35\n- Answer: max(3, 17, 10, 35) = 35 ✓\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef solve():\n    input = sys.stdin.readline\n    N = int(input())\n    \n    color_to_beans = defaultdict(list)\n    for _ in range(N):\n        A, C = map(int, input().split())\n        color_to_beans[C].append(A)\n    \n    answer = 0\n    for beans in color_to_beans.values():\n        min_deliciousness = min(beans)\n        if min_deliciousness > answer:\n            answer = min_deliciousness\n    \n    print(answer)\n\nsolve()\n```\n\nThe function is ready. It reads N beans, groups them by color, computes the minimum deliciousness per color, and outputs the maximum of these minimums.",
