    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve(n: int) -> int:\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # A string is \"good\" if it can be rearranged to contain \"leet\" as a substring.\n    # This means the string must have at least:\n    # - 1 'l'\n    # - 2 'e's\n    # - 1 't'\n    # The remaining n - 4 characters can be any lowercase English letter (a-z).\n    \n    # Total number of strings of length n with 26 lowercase letters is 26^n.\n    # We need to count how many of these satisfy the condition: count('l') >= 1, count('e') >= 2, count('t') >= 1.\n    \n    # Let's use inclusion-exclusion principle.\n    # Let U be the set of all strings of length n over 26 letters. |U| = 26^n.\n    # Let A be the property that the string does NOT have at least 1 'l' (i.e., has 0 'l's).\n    # Let B be the property that the string does NOT have at least 2 'e's (i.e., has 0 or 1 'e').\n    # Let C be the property that the string does NOT have at least 1 't' (i.e., has 0 't's).\n    \n    # We want to find |U| - |A ∪ B ∪ C|.\n    # By inclusion-exclusion:\n    # |A ∪ B ∪ C| = |A| + |B| + |C| - |A∩B| - |A∩C| - |B∩C| + |A∩B∩C|\n    \n    # Calculate each term:\n    \n    # |A|: Strings with no 'l'. Each character can be chosen from 25 letters (all except 'l').\n    # |A| = 25^n\n    \n    # |C|: Strings with no 't'. Each character can be chosen from 25 letters (all except 't').\n    # |C| = 25^n\n    \n    # |B|: Strings with fewer than 2 'e's (i.e., 0 'e's or 1 'e').\n    # Number of strings with exactly k 'e's: C(n, k) * 1^k * 25^(n-k)\n    # |B| = (strings with 0 'e's) + (strings with 1 'e')\n    #     = C(n, 0) * 25^n + C(n, 1) * 25^(n-1)\n    #     = 25^n + n * 25^(n-1)\n    \n    # |A ∩ C|: Strings with no 'l' AND no 't'. Characters chosen from 24 letters (all except 'l' and 't').\n    # |A ∩ C| = 24^n\n    \n    # |A ∩ B|: Strings with no 'l' AND fewer than 2 'e's.\n    # Characters are chosen from {a,b,c,d,f,g,h,i,j,k,m,n,o,p,q,r,s,u,v,w,x,y,z} which is 25 letters, but 'e' is special.\n    # Actually, the available letters are all except 'l'. So 25 choices per position, but we track 'e'.\n    # Let's think: positions can have 'e' or any of the other 24 non-'l', non-'e' letters.\n    # Wait, no 'l' means alphabet size is 25 (a-z excluding l). Among these 25, one is 'e'.\n    # So we need strings of length n using 25 letters (including 'e'), with fewer than 2 'e's.\n    # |A ∩ B| = (strings with 0 'e's using 24 non-e, non-l letters) + (strings with 1 'e')\n    #         = C(n, 0) * 24^n + C(n, 1) * 24^(n-1) * 1\n    #         = 24^n + n * 24^(n-1)\n    \n    # |C ∩ B|: Strings with no 't' AND fewer than 2 'e's.\n    # Similar to above: no 't' means 25 letters available (a-z excluding t). One of them is 'e'.\n    # |C ∩ B| = 24^n + n * 24^(n-1)\n    \n    # |A ∩ B ∩ C|: Strings with no 'l', no 't', and fewer than 2 'e's.\n    # No 'l' and no 't' means 24 letters available. One of them is 'e'.\n    # |A ∩ B ∩ C| = 23^n + n * 23^(n-1)\n    \n    # Now compute:\n    # Bad = |A| + |B| + |C| - |A∩B| - |A∩C| - |B∩C| + |A∩B∩C|\n    # Good = Total - Bad\n    \n    if n < 4:\n        return 0\n    \n    def pow_mod(base, exp, mod):\n        result = 1\n        base %= mod\n        while exp > 0:\n            if exp % 2 == 1:\n                result = (result * base) % mod\n            exp //= 2\n            base = (base * base) % mod\n        return result\n    \n    def comb_mod(n, k, mod):\n        if k < 0 or k > n:\n            return 0\n        if k == 0 or k == n:\n            return 1\n        if k > n // 2:\n            k = n - k\n        \n        num = 1\n        den = 1\n        for i in range(k):\n            num = (num * (n - i)) % mod\n            den = (den * (i + 1)) % mod\n        \n        # Fermat's little theorem for modular inverse since mod is prime\n        den_inv = pow_mod(den, mod - 2, mod)\n        return (num * den_inv) % mod\n    \n    # Precompute powers\n    # We need 26^n, 25^n, 24^n, 23^n, and also 25^(n-1), 24^(n-1), 23^(n-1)\n    \n    total = pow_mod(26, n, MOD)\n    \n    A = pow_mod(25, n, MOD)\n    C_val = pow_mod(25, n, MOD)\n    \n    # B = 25^n + n * 25^(n-1)\n    B = (pow_mod(25, n, MOD) + n * pow_mod(25, n - 1, MOD)) % MOD\n    \n    # AC = 24^n\n    AC = pow_mod(24, n, MOD)\n    \n    # AB = 24^n + n * 24^(n-1)\n    AB = (pow_mod(24, n, MOD) + n * pow_mod(24, n - 1, MOD)) % MOD\n    \n    # BC = 24^n + n * 24^(n-1)\n    BC = (pow_mod(24, n, MOD) + n * pow_mod(24, n - 1, MOD)) % MOD\n    \n    # ABC = 23^n + n * 23^(n-1)\n    ABC = (pow_mod(23, n, MOD) + n * pow_mod(23, n - 1, MOD)) % MOD\n    \n    bad = (A + B + C_val - AB - AC - BC + ABC) % MOD\n    bad = (bad + MOD) % MOD  # Ensure positive\n    \n    good = (total - bad) % MOD\n    \n    return good\n```",
