    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere are 2N points placed at equal intervals on a circle, numbered 1 to 2N in a clockwise direction starting from a certain point.\nThere are also N chords on the circle, with the i-th chord connecting points A_i and B_i.\nIt is guaranteed that all the values A_1,\\dots,A_N,B_1,\\dots,B_N are distinct.\nDetermine whether there is an intersection between the chords.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\nA_1 B_1\nA_2 B_2\n\\vdots\nA_N B_N\n\nOutput\n\nIf there is an intersection between the chords, print Yes; otherwise, print No.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2\\leq N \\leq 2\\times 10^5\n- 1\\leq A_i,B_i \\leq 2N\n- A_1,\\dots,A_N,B_1,\\dots,B_N are all distinct\n- All input values are integers\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3\n1 3\n4 2\n5 6\n\nSample Output 1\n\nYes\n\n\nAs shown in the figure, chord 1 (the line segment connecting points 1 and 3) and chord 2 (the line segment connecting points 4 and 2) intersect, so print Yes.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3\n6 1\n4 3\n2 5\n\nSample Output 2\n\nNo\n\n\nAs shown in the figure, there is no intersection between the chords, so print No.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n4\n2 4\n3 7\n8 6\n5 1\n\nSample Output 3\n\nYes",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n        \n    chords = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        a = int(next(iterator))\n        b = int(next(iterator))\n        # Normalize chord so that smaller endpoint is first\n        if a > b:\n            a, b = b, a\n        chords.append((a, b))\n    \n    # Sort chords by their starting point\n    chords.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\n    \n    # We need to check if any two chords intersect.\n    # Two chords (a1, b1) and (a2, b2) with a1 < b1 and a2 < b2 intersect if and only if:\n    # a1 < a2 < b1 < b2 OR a2 < a1 < b2 < b1\n    # Since we sorted by start point, for i < j, we have chords[i].start <= chords[j].start.\n    # Since all endpoints are distinct, chords[i].start < chords[j].start.\n    # So intersection happens if chords[i].start < chords[j].start < chords[i].end < chords[j].end.\n    \n    # We can use a sweep-line approach with a Fenwick tree (Binary Indexed Tree) or Segment Tree.\n    # Alternatively, since we just need to detect ANY intersection, we can iterate and maintain a set of active chords.\n    # However, N is up to 2*10^5, so O(N^2) is too slow.\n    \n    # Better approach:\n    # Coordinate compression isn't needed since points are 1..2N.\n    # We can process points from 1 to 2N.\n    # When we encounter the start of a chord, we add its end point to a data structure.\n    # When we encounter the end of a chord, we remove it.\n    # An intersection occurs if, when we are at the start of chord j (which starts after chord i),\n    # chord i is still active (i.e., its end is greater than chord j's start) AND chord i's end is less than chord j's end.\n    # Wait, let's refine.\n    \n    # Let's map each point to what chord it belongs to and whether it's a start or end.\n    # Actually, simpler logic:\n    # Two chords (a, b) and (c, d) with a < c intersect iff a < c < b < d.\n    # If we sort chords by 'a', then for any pair i < j, we have a_i < a_j.\n    # Intersection condition becomes: a_i < a_j < b_i < b_j.\n    \n    # We can check this efficiently.\n    # Iterate through sorted chords. For current chord j with (a_j, b_j), we want to know if there exists an i < j such that:\n    # a_i < a_j < b_i < b_j.\n    # Since a_i < a_j is always true for i < j (sorted), we need:\n    # b_i > a_j AND b_i < b_j.\n    # So among all chords i < j, is there one whose end point b_i is in the range (a_j, b_j)?\n    \n    # We can maintain the set of end points of active chords (chords that have started but not ended).\n    # But \"active\" here means chords with a_i < current position.\n    # As we sweep from left to right (from 1 to 2N):\n    # - When we hit a start point of a chord, we add its end point to a data structure.\n    # - When we hit an end point of a chord, we remove it.\n    # - At any point, if we are at the start of chord j (point a_j), the data structure contains end points of all chords i where a_i < a_j.\n    #   We need to check if any of these end points is less than b_j. If yes, does that mean intersection?\n    #   Condition: a_i < a_j < b_i < b_j.\n    #   So we need b_i in (a_j, b_j).\n    \n    # Let's use a Fenwick Tree (BIT) to count how many end points are in a range.\n    # Points are 1 to 2N.\n    # BIT supports point update and prefix sum.\n    # Range sum [L, R] = query(R) - query(L-1).\n    \n    # Algorithm:\n    # 1. Create events for each chord: (position, type, chord_index).\n    #    Start event: (a_i, 0, i)\n    #    End event: (b_i, 1, i)\n    # 2. Sort events by position.\n    # 3. Initialize BIT of size 2N+1.\n    # 4. Iterate through events:\n    #    - If start event for chord i at pos a_i:\n    #      Check if there is any active chord end point in range (a_i, b_i).\n    #      Active chords are those that have started but not ended.\n    #      Wait, the BIT should store the end points of chords that have STARTED.\n    #      But we must ensure we only count chords that have NOT YET ENDED?\n    #      No, if a chord has ended, its end point was at some pos < a_i (since a_i is current).\n    #      But we are looking for b_i in (a_i, b_j). Since a_i is current, b_i must be > a_i.\n    #      So any chord in the BIT with end point > a_i is still \"active\" in the sense that it hasn't passed its end point yet?\n    #      Actually, if we add end points to BIT when we see the START of the chord, and remove them when we see the END.\n    #      Then at position a_i, the BIT contains end points of all chords that started before a_i and haven't ended yet.\n    #      For such a chord k, we have a_k < a_i. And since it hasn't ended, b_k >= a_i.\n    #      We want to check if b_k < b_i.\n    #      So we check if there is any value in BIT in range (a_i, b_i).\n    #      If yes, then there exists k such that a_k < a_i < b_k < b_i -> Intersection!\n    #    - If end event for chord i at pos b_i:\n    #      Remove b_i from BIT.\n    \n    # Implementation details:\n    # BIT is 1-indexed.\n    # Add: update(pos, +1)\n    # Remove: update(pos, -1)\n    # Query range [L, R]: query(R) - query(L-1)\n    \n    max_val = 2 * N\n    bit = [0] * (max_val + 2)\n    \n    def bit_update(idx, val):\n        while idx <= max_val:\n            bit[idx] += val\n            idx += idx & (-idx)\n            \n    def bit_query(idx):\n        s = 0\n        while idx > 0:\n            s += bit[idx]\n            idx -= idx & (-idx)\n        return s\n        \n    def bit_range_query(l, r):\n        if l > r:\n            return 0\n        return bit_query(r) - bit_query(l - 1)\n        \n    # Create events\n    # Each chord is stored as (a, b)\n    # Events: (coordinate, type)\n    # Type 0: Start, Type 1: End\n    events = []\n    for i in range(N):\n        a, b = chords[i]\n        events.append((a, 0, i))\n        events.append((b, 1, i))\n        \n    # Sort events by coordinate. \n    # If coordinates are equal, process Ends before Starts? \n    # No, endpoints are distinct, so no two events have same coordinate.\n    events.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\n    \n    for coord, typ, idx in events:\n        if typ == 0:\n            # Start of chord idx\n            a, b = chords[idx]\n            # Check for intersection with previously started and not ended chords\n            # Range (a, b) exclusive of a, inclusive of b? No, b_i < b_j.\n            # So we look for end points in range (a, b).\n            # i.e., end_point > a and end_point < b.\n            count = bit_range_query(a + 1, b - 1)\n            if count > 0:\n                print(\"Yes\")\n                return\n            # Add current chord's end point to BIT\n            bit_update(b, 1)\n        else:\n            # End of chord idx\n            a, b = chords[idx]\n            # Remove current chord's end point from BIT\n            bit_update(b, -1)\n            \n    print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
