    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nYou are given two strings: S, which consists of uppercase English letters and has length N, and T, which also consists of uppercase English letters and has length M\\ (\\leq N).\nThere is a string X of length N consisting only of the character #. Determine whether it is possible to make X match S by performing the following operation any number of times:\n\n- Choose M consecutive characters in X and replace them with T.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\nS\nT\n\nOutput\n\nPrint Yes if it is possible to make X match S; print No otherwise.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\leq N \\leq 2\\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq M \\leq \\min(N, 5)\n- S is a string consisting of uppercase English letters with length N.\n- T is a string consisting of uppercase English letters with length M.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n7 3\nABCBABC\nABC\n\nSample Output 1\n\nYes\n\nBelow, let X[l:r] denote the part from the l-th through the r-th character of X.\nYou can make X match S by operating as follows.\n\n- Replace X[3:5] with T. X becomes ##ABC##.\n- Replace X[1:3] with T. X becomes ABCBC##.\n- Replace X[5:7] with T. X becomes ABCBABC.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n7 3\nABBCABC\nABC\n\nSample Output 2\n\nNo\n\nNo matter how you operate, it is impossible to make X match S.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n12 2\nXYXXYXXYYYXY\nXY\n\nSample Output 3\n\nYes",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    N = int(input_data[0])\n    M = int(input_data[1])\n    S = input_data[2]\n    T = input_data[3]\n    \n    # We start with X = \"#\" * N\n    # We want to reach S by replacing M consecutive characters with T.\n    # This is equivalent to checking if we can cover all positions in S that are NOT matching T's potential placements,\n    # but actually, it's better to think in reverse or use dynamic programming / greedy with intervals.\n    \n    # Let's analyze the operation:\n    # We can place T at any position i such that 0 <= i <= N-M.\n    # When we place T at i, X[i:i+M] becomes T.\n    # The goal is to have X == S at the end.\n    # Note that overwriting is allowed. So if we place T at multiple overlapping positions, the last placement on any index determines its value.\n    # However, since we can do operations \"any number of times\" and in any order, this suggests we need to check if S is \"constructible\".\n    \n    # Key Insight:\n    # Consider the positions where S[j] != T[k] for some k. Actually, let's look at mismatches.\n    # If we never touch position j, X[j] remains '#'. So for all j, either X[j] is overwritten by some T placement, or it must be '#' in S? \n    # No, S consists of uppercase letters. So every position in S must be covered by at least one T placement.\n    # Wait, the final string X must equal S. S has no '#'. So every position 0..N-1 must be covered by at least one operation.\n    # Furthermore, for each position j, the character S[j] must match the character in T that was placed there last.\n    # But since we can reorder operations, we can think of this as: Is there a set of intervals [i, i+M) such that their union covers [0, N), and for each position j, \n    # the T-character placed at j (from the interval that covers j and is \"active\") matches S[j]?\n    \n    # Actually, a simpler way to view this:\n    # We can place T at index i if T matches S[i:i+M]? Not necessarily, because later operations might overwrite parts of it.\n    # However, notice that M is very small (<= 5). N is up to 2*10^5.\n    \n    # Let's define a valid placement at index i:\n    # A placement at i is \"compatible\" with S if for all k in 0..M-1, T[k] == S[i+k].\n    # If a placement is compatible, then placing T at i will result in S[i:i+M] being correctly formed, assuming no subsequent operation overwrites these positions incorrectly.\n    # But wait, if we have overlapping compatible placements, can we combine them?\n    # Example: S = ABCABC, T = ABC. M=3.\n    # Placements at 0: ABC matches S[0:3]. Compatible.\n    # Placements at 3: ABC matches S[3:6]. Compatible.\n    # Union covers 0-5. Yes.\n    \n    # What if S = ABABA, T = ABA. M=3.\n    # Pos 0: ABA vs S[0:3]=ABA. Match.\n    # Pos 1: BAB vs S[1:4]=BAB. Match.\n    # Pos 2: ABA vs S[2:5]=ABA. Match.\n    # Can we cover all?\n    # If we pick pos 0, we cover 0,1,2.\n    # If we pick pos 2, we cover 2,3,4.\n    # Union is 0,1,2,3,4. All covered. Yes.\n    \n    # What if S = AAAA, T = AA. M=2.\n    # Pos 0: AA matches S[0:2].\n    # Pos 1: AA matches S[1:3].\n    # Pos 2: AA matches S[2:4].\n    # Cover 0,1 with pos 0. Cover 1,2 with pos 1. Cover 2,3 with pos 2.\n    # Union 0,1,2,3. Yes.\n    \n    # So the problem reduces to:\n    # 1. Identify all indices i where placing T is \"locally consistent\" with S. That is, T == S[i:i+M].\n    # 2. Check if the union of intervals [i, i+M) for all such valid i covers [0, N).\n    \n    # Why is this sufficient?\n    # If T matches S[i:i+M], then placing T at i sets X[i:i+M] to T, which equals S[i:i+M].\n    # If we select a set of such intervals that cover [0, N), we can perform the operations.\n    # Does the order matter?\n    # Suppose we have two overlapping valid intervals I1 and I2.\n    # I1 sets its range to T. I2 sets its range to T.\n    # Since both are valid, T matches S in both ranges.\n    # In the overlap region, both I1 and I2 write T. Since T is the same string, the result is T.\n    # And since both are valid, T matches S in the overlap too.\n    # So regardless of order, the overlapping part will be T, which matches S.\n    # Non-overlapping parts are set by their respective intervals, which also match S.\n    # Therefore, if we have a cover, we can just execute them in any order (e.g., increasing index) and the final string will be S.\n    \n    # So the algorithm is:\n    # 1. Find all start positions i such that S[i:i+M] == T.\n    # 2. These positions generate intervals [i, i+M).\n    # 3. Check if the union of these intervals covers [0, N).\n    \n    # To check coverage efficiently:\n    # Collect all start points i where S[i:i+M] == T.\n    # Sort them.\n    # Iterate and merge intervals or just check coverage linearly.\n    \n    valid_starts = []\n    for i in range(N - M + 1):\n        if S[i:i+M] == T:\n            valid_starts.append(i)\n            \n    if not valid_starts:\n        # If no valid placement exists, we can't cover anything unless N=0? But N>=1.\n        # Also if N < M, loop range is empty, valid_starts empty.\n        # If N < M, we can't place T at all. So impossible.\n        print(\"No\")\n        return\n\n    # Now check if union of [i, i+M) for i in valid_starts covers [0, N).\n    # We can do this greedily.\n    current_end = 0\n    idx = 0\n    n_valid = len(valid_starts)\n    \n    # We need to cover from 0 to N.\n    # At each step, we want to extend current_end as far right as possible using a valid start that begins <= current_end.\n    \n    while current_end < N:\n        # Find the valid_start that is <= current_end and maximizes start + M\n        best_next_end = -1\n        \n        # Since valid_starts is sorted, we can iterate forward.\n        # But we need to consider all valid_starts that start <= current_end.\n        \n        found = False\n        while idx < n_valid and valid_starts[idx] <= current_end:\n            # This interval starts within or before our current coverage gap end.\n            # It extends to valid_starts[idx] + M.\n            next_end = valid_starts[idx] + M\n            if next_end > best_next_end:\n                best_next_end = next_end\n            found = True\n            idx += 1\n            \n        if not found or best_next_end == -1:\n            # Cannot extend further\n            break\n            \n        current_end = best_next_end\n        \n    if current_end >= N:\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
