    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport math\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    x_str = sys.stdin.read().strip()\n    x = int(x_str)\n    # Calculate ceil(x / 10)\n    # Using integer arithmetic: (x + 9) // 10 works for positive numbers\n    # But for negative numbers, we need to be careful.\n    # A robust way using integer arithmetic for ceil(a/b) where b > 0:\n    # ceil(a/b) = (a + b - 1) // b if a >= 0\n    # For general integers, we can use: (a + b - 1) // b doesn't work for negatives directly.\n    # Better approach: use math.ceil with float division might lose precision for large numbers.\n    # Since X can be up to 10^18, float division has enough precision (53 bits significand ~ 15-17 decimal digits), \n    # but 10^18 requires ~60 bits, so float might not be exact.\n    # Let's use integer arithmetic.\n    # ceil(x / 10) is equivalent to:\n    # if x % 10 == 0: x // 10\n    # else: x // 10 + 1   (for positive x)\n    # For negative x:\n    # e.g., x = -13, -13 // 10 = -2 (in Python, floor division), -13 % 10 = 7\n    # We want -1.\n    # Formula: (x + 9) // 10 works for positive?\n    # 27 + 9 = 36, 36 // 10 = 3. Correct.\n    # 40 + 9 = 49, 49 // 10 = 4. Correct.\n    # -13 + 9 = -4, -4 // 10 = -1. Correct.\n    # -20 + 9 = -11, -11 // 10 = -2. Correct.\n    # -27 + 9 = -18, -18 // 10 = -2. \n    # Check: -27/10 = -2.7, ceil is -2. Correct.\n    # So the formula (x + 9) // 10 works for all integers x.\n    \n    result = (x + 9) // 10\n    print(result)\n\nsolve()\n```",
