      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n        \n    rectangles = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        A = int(next(iterator))\n        B = int(next(iterator))\n        C = int(next(iterator))\n        D = int(next(iterator))\n        rectangles.append((A, B, C, D))\n    \n    # Since coordinates are small (0 to 100), we can use a grid approach.\n    # We can discretize the space into unit squares.\n    # Each rectangle covers [A, B] x [C, D].\n    # This corresponds to unit squares [x, x+1] x [y, y+1] for x in A..B-1 and y in C..D-1.\n    \n    # Create a 2D boolean array or set to mark covered unit squares.\n    # Coordinates are integers from 0 to 100.\n    # Max coordinate is 100. So x ranges from 0 to 99 for unit intervals, same for y.\n    \n    covered = [[False] * 100 for _ in range(100)]\n    \n    for (A, B, C, D) in rectangles:\n        for x in range(A, B):\n            for y in range(C, D):\n                if 0 <= x < 100 and 0 <= y < 100:\n                    covered[x][y] = True\n                    \n    count = 0\n    for x in range(100):\n        for y in range(100):\n            if covered[x][y]:\n                count += 1\n                \n    print(count)\n\nsolve()\n```",
