repeats every 2^(p+1).\n    In each block of 2^(p+1), the bit p is 1 for exactly 2^p numbers.\n    \n    So, for a given bit position p, the number of integers in [1, n] that have bit p set is:\n    full_blocks = n // (2^(p+1))\n    remainder = n % (2^(p+1))\n    count_p = full_blocks * 2^p + max(0, remainder - 2^p + 1)\n    \n    Wait, let's verify. For p=0 (LSB), pattern is 0,1,0,1... period 2.\n    In [1, n]:\n    If n=1: count should be 1 (number 1 has bit 0 set).\n    Formula: full_blocks = 1 // 2 = 0, remainder = 1.\n    count_p = 0 * 1 + max(0, 1 - 1 + 1) = 1. Correct.\n    If n=2: numbers 1, 2. Bit 0 set in 1. Count = 1.\n    Formula: full_blocks = 2 // 2 = 1, remainder = 0.\n    count_p = 1 * 1 + max(0, 0 - 1 + 1) = 1 + 0 = 1. Correct.\n    If n=3: numbers 1, 2, 3. Bit 0 set in 1, 3. Count = 2.\n    Formula: full_blocks = 3 // 2 = 1, remainder = 1.\n    count_p = 1 * 1 + max(0, 1 - 1 + 1) = 1 + 1 = 2. Correct.\n    If n=4: numbers 1,2,3,4. Bit 0 set in 1,3. Count = 2.\n    Formula: full_blocks = 4 // 2 = 2, remainder = 0.\n    count_p = 2 * 1 + max(0, 0 - 1 + 1) = 2 + 0 = 2. Correct.\n    \n    So the formula for count of numbers in [1, n] with bit p set is:\n    period = 2^(p+1)\n    half_period = 2^p\n    full_cycles = n // period\n    remaining = n % period\n    count = full_cycles * half_period + max(0, remaining - half_period + 1)\n    \n    The price of n is the sum of contributions from all relevant bit positions.\n    Relevant bit positions are those where the 1-indexed position from right is a multiple of x.\n    1-indexed position i corresponds to 0-indexed position p = i - 1.\n    So we want i % x == 0 => (p + 1) % x == 0 => p % x == x - 1.\n    \n    So for a given x, the bit positions p that contribute are p = x-1, 2x-1, 3x-1, ...\n    We need to sum the counts for all such p up to the maximum bit needed.\n    Since k <= 10^15, and x >= 1, the numbers won't be astronomically large.\n    Max bit position needed: 2^p <= n. If n ~ 10^15, p ~ 50.\n