per function count_total_price(n, x) that computes the sum of prices for numbers 1..n.\n    2. Binary search for the largest n in [0, 10^16] such that count_total_price(n, x) <= k.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    def get_bit_count(n: int, p: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Returns the number of integers in [1, n] that have the p-th bit set (0-indexed from right).\n        \"\"\"\n        if n <= 0:\n            return 0\n        period = 1 << (p + 1)  # 2^(p+1)\n        half_period = 1 << p     # 2^p\n        \n        full_cycles = n // period\n        remaining = n % period\n        \n        count = full_cycles * half_period\n        # In the remaining part, the bit is set for indices [half_period, period - 1] within the cycle.\n        # The values in the remaining part correspond to 0, 1, ..., remaining - 1.\n        # We want to count how many of these have the p-th bit set.\n        # The p-th bit is set when the value v satisfies: v >= half_period and v < period.\n        # But since we are looking at the remainder of n, the values are 0 to remaining-1.\n        # So we count v in [0, remaining-1] such that v >= half_period.\n        # This is max(0, remaining - half_period).\n        # Wait, let's re-verify with previous example.\n        # n=1, p=0. period=2, half=1. full=0, rem=1.\n        # count = 0. max(0, 1-1) = 0. Total 0. But answer should be 1.\n        # Ah, the range is [1, n]. My formula was derived for [0, n-1] or similar?\n        # Let's re-derive carefully for [1, n].\n        \n        # Standard formula for count of set bits at position p in [0, n]:\n        # It's easier to count in [0, n] and subtract the case for 0 (which is always 0).\n        # So count in [1, n] = count in [0, n].\n        \n        # Let's use the formula for [0, n]:\n        # full_cycles = (n + 1) // period\n        # rem = (n + 1) % period\n        # count = full_cycles * half_period + max(0, rem - half_period)\n        \n        # Test n=1, p=0. [0, 1] includes 0, 1.\n        # period=2, half=1.\n        # full_cycles = 2 // 2 = 1.\n        # rem = 2 % 2 = 0.\n        # count = 1*1 + max(0, 0-1) = 1. Correct (only 1 has bit 0 set).\n        \n        # Test n=2, p=0. [0, 2] includes 0, 1, 2.\n        # full_cycles = 3 // 2 = 1.\n        # rem = 3 % 2 = 1.\n        # count = 1*1 + max(0, 1-1) = 1. Correct (only 1 has bit 0 set among 0,1,2? No, 1 is odd. 0 even, 1 odd, 2 even. Yes.)\n        \n        # Test n=3, p=0. [0, 3] includes 0, 1, 2, 3. Odds: 1, 3. Count 2.\n        # full_cycles = 4 // 2 = 2.\n        # rem = 4 % 2 = 0.\n        # count = 2*1 + 0 = 2. Correct.\n        \n        # Test n=4, p=0. [0, 4]. Odds: 1, 3. Count 2.\n        # full_cycles = 5 // 2 = 2.\n        # rem = 5 % 2 = 1.\n        # count = 2*1 + max(0, 1-1) = 2. Correct.\n        \n        # So the formula for count in [0, n] is:\n        # full_cycles = (n + 1) // period\n        # rem = (n + 1) % period\n        # count = full_cycles * half_period + max(0, rem - half_period)\n        \n        count = full_cycles * half_period + max(0, remaining - half_period)\n        return count\n\n    def count_total_price(n: int, x: int) -> int:\n        if n <= 0:\n            return 0\n        \n        total = 0\n        p = x - 1\n        while (1 << p) <= n + 1: # Check if there can be any numbers up to n with this bit set\n            # We can go a bit higher just to be safe, or stop when 2^p > n\n            # Actually, even if 2^p > n, the count is 0. So we can stop when 2^p > n.\n            if (1 << p) > n:\n                break\n                \n            cnt = get_bit_count(n, p)\n            total += cnt\n            \n            p += x\n            \n        return total\n\n    # Binary search\n    low = 0\n    high = 10**16  # Sufficiently large upper bound\n    ans = 0\n    \n    while low <= high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        price_sum = count_total_price(mid, x)\n        if price_sum <= k:\n            ans = mid\n            