red.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: source = \"abcd\", target = \"abce\", original = [\"a\"], changed = [\"e\"], cost = [10000]\nOutput: -1\nExplanation: It is impossible to convert source to target because the value at index 3 cannot be changed from 'd' to 'e'.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= source.length == target.length <= 10^5\nsource, target consist of lowercase English letters.\n1 <= cost.length == original.length == changed.length <= 2000\noriginal[i], changed[i] are lowercase English letters.\n1 <= cost[i] <= 10^6\noriginal[i] != changed[i]",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport heapq\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef minimumCost(source: str, target: str, original: list[str], changed: list[str], cost: list[int]) -> int:\n    # Build adjacency list for the graph\n    # graph[u] = list of (v, weight)\n    graph = defaultdict(list)\n    \n    # We need the minimum cost for each pair (u, v).\n    # Since there might be multiple edges between same u and v, we keep the min.\n    # But Floyd-Warshall or Dijkstra from each node is needed for transitive costs.\n    \n    # First, collect direct edges and keep the minimum cost for each direct edge.\n    edge_cost = defaultdict(lambda: float('inf'))\n    \n    for o, c, w in zip(original, changed, cost):\n        key = (o, c)\n        if w < edge_cost[key]:\n            edge_cost[key] = w\n            \n    # Build the graph with unique edges\n    for (u, v), w in edge_cost.items():\n        graph[u].append((v, w))\n        \n    # We have 26 characters. Let's map them to 0-25.\n    # Run Dijkstra from each character present in source/target or just all 26?\n    # Actually, we only care about paths between characters that appear in source and target.\n    # But since n is up to 10^5, and alphabet is small (26), we can precompute all-pairs shortest paths.\n    \n    # Initialize dist matrix with infinity\n    INF = float('inf')\n    # Map char to index 0-25\n    def char_to_idx(c):\n        return ord(c) - ord('a')\n        \n    # dist[i][j] will store the min cost to convert char i to char j\n    dist = [[INF] * 26 for _ in range(26)]\n    \n    # Self loops have cost 0\n    for i in range(26):\n        dist[i][i] = 0\n        \n    # Fill in direct edges\n    for (u, v), w in edge_cost.items():\n        ui = char_to_idx(u)\n        vi = char_to_idx(v)\n        if w < dist[ui][vi]:\n            dist[ui][vi] = w\n            \n    # Floyd-Warshall Algorithm\n    # Since N=26 is very small, O(N^3) is fine.\n    for k in range(26):\n        for i in range(26):\n            if dist[i][k] == INF:\n                continue\n            for j in range(26):\n                if dist[k][j] == INF:\n                    continue\n                if dist[i][k] + dist[k][j] < dist[i][j]:\n                    dist[i][j] = dist[i][k] + dist[k][j]\n                    \n    total_cost = 0\n    \n    for s_char, t_char in zip(source, target):\n        if s_char == t_char:\n            continue\n            \n        si = char_to_idx(s_char)\n        ti = char_to_idx(t_char)\n        \n        if dist[si][ti] == INF:\n            return -1\n            \n        total_cost += dist[si][ti]\n        \n        # Check for overflow if necessary, but constraints say cost fits in integer.\n        # Max cost per op is 10^6, max length 10^5, so max total ~ 10^11, which fits in 64-bit int.\n        # Python handles large integers automatically.\n        \n    return total_cost\n```",
