      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef paintWalls(cost: list[int], time: list[int]) -> int:\n    n = len(cost)\n    # dp[i][j] represents the minimum cost to paint the first i walls such that\n    # the paid painter has accumulated j units of \"time credit\" (which allows the free painter to work).\n    # However, a more standard DP state for this problem is:\n    # Let dp[j] be the minimum cost to achieve a total time capacity of at least j.\n    # We iterate through each wall. For each wall, we can either:\n    # 1. Have the paid painter paint it: This adds cost[i] to our total cost and time[i] + 1 to our time capacity (since the paid painter is busy for time[i], the free painter can paint time[i] walls, plus the current wall itself is done by paid). Actually, let's rethink.\n    \n    # Alternative DP formulation:\n    # We need to cover all n walls.\n    # When the paid painter paints wall i, it takes time[i] units. During these time[i] units, the free painter can paint time[i] other walls.\n    # So, choosing wall i to be painted by the paid painter effectively \"covers\" 1 + time[i] walls (the wall i itself, plus time[i] others covered by the free painter).\n    # We want to select a subset of walls to be painted by the paid painter such that the total number of walls covered is >= n, and the total cost is minimized.\n    \n    # Let dp[k] be the minimum cost to cover k walls.\n    # Initialize dp[0] = 0, and dp[k] = infinity for k > 0.\n    # The maximum number of walls we need to cover is n.\n    \n    INF = float('inf')\n    dp = [INF] * (n + 1)\n    dp[0] = 0\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        c = cost[i]\n        t = time[i]\n        # This wall, if painted by the paid painter, covers 1 + t walls.\n        # We iterate backwards to avoid using the same item multiple times in the same step (knapsack-like).\n        # The new coverage added is t + 1.\n        add = t + 1\n        for j in range(n, -1, -1):\n            # If we currently have covered j walls, and we use the paid painter on wall i,\n            # the new coverage becomes min(n, j + add).\n            # But actually, we want to update dp[min(n, j + add)] using dp[j].\n            # Wait, standard knapsack: for each item, update dp array.\n            # Let's reverse the loop. For each existing state j, we can transition to a new state.\n            # To avoid overwriting values needed for the same iteration, we go from high to low?\n            # No, if we define dp[j] as min cost to cover AT LEAST j walls, or EXACTLY j?\n            # Let's say dp[j] is min cost to cover exactly j walls? No, because covering more than n is fine.\n            # Let dp[j] be min cost to cover j walls, where j can go up to n. Any coverage beyond n is capped at n.\n            \n            # Let's try: dp[j] = min cost to cover j walls.\n            # Initialize dp[0]=0, others INF.\n            # For each wall i with cost c and time t:\n            #   It can contribute to increasing the number of covered walls by t+1.\n            #   New_dp[j + t + 1] = min(New_dp[j + t + 1], dp[j] + c)\n            #   Also New_dp[j] = min(New_dp[j], dp[j]) (don't pick this wall for paid painter)\n            \n            # Since we want to minimize cost, and we can cap the coverage at n:\n            for j in range(n, -1, -1):\n                if dp[j] == INF:\n                    continue\n                next_j = min(n, j + t + 1)\n                if dp[j] + c < dp[next_j]:\n                    dp[next_j] = dp[j] + c\n                    \n    return dp[n]\n```",
