    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a string word and an integer k.\nA substring s of word is complete if:\n\nEach character in s occurs exactly k times.\nThe difference between two adjacent characters is at most 2. That is, for any two adjacent characters c1 and c2 in s, the absolute difference in their positions in the alphabet is at most 2.\n\nReturn the number of complete substrings of word.\nA substring is a non-empty contiguous sequence of characters in a string.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: word = \"igigee\", k = 2\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: The complete substrings where each character appears exactly twice and the difference between adjacent characters is at most 2 are: igigee, igigee, igigee.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: word = \"aaabbbccc\", k = 3\nOutput: 6\nExplanation: The complete substrings where each character appears exactly three times and the difference between adjacent characters is at most 2 are: aaabbbccc, aaabbbccc, aaabbbccc, aaabbbccc, aaabbbccc, aaabbbccc.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= word.length <= 10^5\nword consists only of lowercase English letters.\n1 <= k <= word.length",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef countCompleteSubstrings(word: str, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of 'complete' substrings in the given word.\n    \n    A substring is complete if:\n    1. Each character in the substring occurs exactly k times.\n    2. The absolute difference between adjacent characters (in alphabet position) is at most 2.\n    \n    Args:\n        word: Input string consisting of lowercase English letters.\n        k: Integer representing the exact frequency required for each character in a complete substring.\n        \n    Returns:\n        The number of complete substrings.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(word)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    \n    # Helper function to check if a substring satisfies the adjacency condition\n    # Since we will iterate through potential valid windows defined by the adjacency constraint,\n    # we first need to identify segments where the adjacency condition holds.\n    \n    # Step 1: Split the word into maximal contiguous segments where the adjacency condition holds.\n    # Adjacency condition: |ord(s[i]) - ord(s[i+1])| <= 2\n    \n    # We can use a sliding window approach on these segments.\n    # Within each segment, all substrings satisfy the adjacency condition.\n    # We then need to count substrings within these segments where each char appears exactly k times.\n    \n    # Let's identify the start indices of such segments.\n    # A segment is a range [l, r] such that for all i in [l, r-1], abs(ord(word[i]) - ord(word[i+1])) <= 2.\n    \n    # Actually, it's easier to just iterate through the string and maintain a current valid segment.\n    # When the adjacency condition breaks, we process the previous segment and start a new one.\n    \n    total_count = 0\n    \n    # Function to count valid substrings within a single segment based on character frequencies\n    def count_in_segment(segment_start, segment_end):\n        # segment is word[segment_start : segment_end + 1]\n        length = segment_end - segment_start + 1\n        if length < k:\n            return 0\n            \n        count = 0\n        # The possible lengths of complete substrings must be multiples of k.\n        # Also, since each unique char appears k times, if there are m unique chars, length = m * k.\n        # The maximum number of unique characters is 26.\n        # So possible lengths are k, 2k, 3k, ..., up to min(26*k, length).\n        \n        # We can iterate over possible number of unique characters m from 1 to 26.\n        # For each m, the substring length L = m * k.\n        # If L > remaining part of segment, break.\n        \n        for m in range(1, 27):\n            L = m * k\n            if L > length:\n                break\n            \n            # Sliding window of size L over the segment\n            freq = {}\n            # Initialize first window\n            for i in range(L):\n                char = word[segment_start + i]\n                freq[char] = freq.get(char, 0) + 1\n            \n            # Check first window\n            is_valid = True\n            for char, cnt in freq.items():\n                if cnt != k:\n                    is_valid = False\n                    break\n            if is_valid:\n                count += 1\n                \n            # Slide the window\n            for i in range(1, length - L + 1):\n                # Remove leftmost char\n                left_char = word[segment_start + i - 1]\n                freq[left_char] -= 1\n                if freq[left_char] == 0:\n                    del freq[left_char]\n                \n                # Add new rightmost char\n                right_char = word[segment_start + i + L - 1]\n                freq[right_char] = freq.get(right_char, 0) + 1\n                \n                # Check validity\n                # Optimization: only check if changes might have affected validity\n                # But since max unique chars is small (<=26), iterating is fast enough.\n                is_valid = True\n                for char, cnt in freq.items():\n                    if cnt != k:\n                        is_valid = False\n                        break\n                if is_valid:\n                    count += 1\n                    \n        return count\n\n    # Identify segments\n    i = 0\n    while i < n:\n        j = i\n        # Extend j as long as adjacency condition holds\n        while j < n - 1 and abs(ord(word[j]) - ord(word[j+1])) <= 2:\n            j += 1\n        \n        # Segment is from i to j\n        if j >= i:\n            total_count += count_in_segment(i, j)\n        \n        # Move to next segment\n        i = j + 1\n        \n    return total_count\n```",
