h `r` in the valid range, we compute:\n    #   min_cost_r = 2*(k-r) + min(\n    #       min_{aliceIndex s.t. nums[aliceIndex]==1} (sum_dist(r-1 closest OTHER ones to aliceIndex)),\n    #       min_{aliceIndex s.t. nums[aliceIndex]==0} (sum_dist(r closest ones to aliceIndex))\n    #   )\n    #   (Handle edge cases: if `r=0`, only creation part exists. If `r>0` and no ones exist, skip.)\n    \n    # How to compute `min_{aliceIndex} sum_dist(m closest ones to aliceIndex)` efficiently?\n    # Let `f(m)` be the minimum sum of distances to pick `m` ones from the array, optimizing `aliceIndex`.\n    # Wait, the `aliceIndex` also determines the starting point.\n    # The sum of distances is calculated relative to `aliceIndex`.\n    \n    # Let's define `dist_sum(i, m)` as the sum of distances of the `m` closest ones to index `i`.\n    # We want `min_i dist_sum(i, m)`.\n    \n    # Key Insight:\n    # The optimal `aliceIndex` for a fixed `m` will likely be near the median of the chosen `m` ones?\n    # Or rather, for a fixed set of `m` ones, the optimal `aliceIndex` is the median of those ones (for L1 distance).\n    # However, we can choose WHICH `m` ones to pick.\n    # To minimize the sum of distances to `aliceIndex`, we should pick `m` ones that are clustered together.\n    # Why? Because spreading them out increases the spread of distances.\n    # So, the problem reduces to: Find a contiguous subarray of `ones` of length `m` such that the sum of distances from the median of these ones to all ones in the subarray is minimized.\n    # Let the chosen ones be `ones[j], ones[j+1], ..., ones[j+m-1]`.\n    # Let `med_idx` be the median index in this subarray.\n    # The optimal `aliceIndex` would be `ones[med_idx]`?\n    # Actually, if `aliceIndex` is one of the ones, it's free to pick up.\n    # If `aliceIndex` is not one of the ones, we just move to it.\n    # But wait, the definition of `sum_dist` above assumes we pay for moving ones TO `aliceIndex`.\n    # If `aliceIndex` coincides with one of the ones, that one costs 0.\n    \n    # Let's verify:\n    # If we pick a block of `m` ones: `ones[j...j+m-1]`.\n    # We stand at `aliceIndex`.\n    # If we stand at `ones[p]` where `j <= p <= j+m-1`, then that one is picked up for free (distance 0).\n    # The cost is sum(|ones[q] - ones[p]| for q in j...j+m-1, q!=p).\n    # If we stand at `aliceIndex` not in the block, cost is sum(|ones[q] - aliceIndex|).\n    # Clearly, standing at one of the ones is better or equal to standing outside, for that specific block.\n    # So, for a fixed block of `m` ones, the best `aliceIndex` is one of the ones in the block (specifically the median minimizes the sum of absolute differences).\n    \n    # Therefore, we can iterate over all contiguous blocks of `ones` of length `m`.\n    # For each block, calculate the cost.\n    # The cost for a block `ones[j...j+m-1]` with `aliceIndex` at `ones[p]` (median) is:\n    # Sum_{q=j}^{j+m-1} |ones[q] - ones[p]|.\n    # Note: If `nums[ones[p]]` is 1, this is consistent.\n    \n    # So, for a fixed `m`:\n    #   min_dist_cost_m = min_{j} ( sum_{q=j}^{j+m-1} |ones[q] - ones[median_index_in_block]| )\n    #   where median_index_in_block is the index in `ones` array corresponding to the median element of the block.\n    \n    # We can compute this for all `m` from 1 to `min(k, total_ones)`.\n    # But `k` is up to 10^5. We can't do O(N*K).\n    # However, we only care about `m` in the range `[max(0, k-maxChanges), min(k, total_ones)]`.\n    # This range might still be large.\n    \n    # Is there a property that allows us to find the global minimum across all `m`?\n    # Total Cost(m) = min_dist_cost_m + 2*(k-m).\n    # We want min_{m} Total Cost(m).\n    \n    # Let's compute `min_dist_cost_m` for all relevant `m`.\n    # `min_dist_cost_m` is the minimum sum of distances to a central point for ANY contiguous subarray of length `m`.\n    \n    # Let `S(j, m)` be the sum of distances from the median of `ones[j:j+m]` to all elements in that slice.\n    # We want `min_j S(j, m)`.\n    \n    # How to compute `S(j, m)` efficiently?\n    # Let `mid = j + m // 2`. The median element is `ones[mid]`.\n    # Sum = sum_{q=j}^{j+m-1} |ones[q] - ones[mid]|.\n    # This can be computed using prefix sums of `ones`.\n    # Let `P[i]` be sum of `ones[0...i-1]`.\n    # Sum of `ones[j...mid-1]` is `P[mid] - P[j]`.\n    # Count is `mid - j`.\n    # Sum of `ones[mid+1...j+m-1]` is `P[j+m] - P[mid+1]`.\n    # Count is `(j+m-1) - (mid+1) + 1 = j + m - mid - 1`.\n    \n    # Left part sum: `ones[mid] * (mid - j) - (P[mid] - P[j])`.\n    # Right part sum: `(P[j+m] - P[mid+1]) - ones[mid] * (j + m - mid - 1)`.\n    # Total = Left + Right.\n    \n    # We can compute `S(j, m)` in O(1) for fixed `m` and `j`.\n    # Iterating all `j` for a fixed `m` takes O(N).\n    # Doing this for all `m` takes O(N*K). Too slow.\n    \n    # However, note that `min_dist_cost_m` is convex-like?\n    # Actually, we can use a sliding window or two pointers?\n    # Or maybe we don't need to check all `m`.\n    \n    # Let's reconsider the range of `m`.\n    # `m` goes from `L = max(0, k - maxChanges)` to `R = min(k, total_ones)`.\n    # If this range is small, we can iterate.\n    # But `maxChanges` can be 0, so `L=k`. Range size 1.\n    # `maxChanges` can be large, so `L=0`. Range size `min(k, total_ones)`.\n    \n    # Is there a way to find the optimal `m` without checking all?\n    # Let `C(m) = min_dist_cost_m + 2*(k-m)`.\n    # `min_dist_cost_m` generally increases with `m` (more ones to cover).\n    # `2*(k-m)` decreases with `m`.\n    # So `C(m)` is likely convex. We can ternary search?\n    # Or just iterate if the range isn't too huge?\n    # Max `N` is 10^5. If we iterate all `m`, and for each `m` iterate all `j`, it's TLE.\n    \n    # We need `min_j S(j, m)` faster.\n    # Notice that as `m` increases by 1, the optimal window shifts.\n    # But the optimal `j` for `m` and `m+1` are related?\n    \n    # Let's try to compute `min_dist_cost_m` for ALL `m` in O(N) total?\n    # This is similar to finding the minimum sum of absolute deviations for all window sizes.\n    \n    # Alternative:\n    # Just iterate `aliceIndex`?\n    # For each `aliceIndex`, we determined the cost is:\n    #   If `nums[ai]==1`: Cost = sum_dist(r-1 closest other ones) + 2*(k-r).\n    #   If `nums[ai]==0`: Cost = sum_dist(r closest ones) + 2*(k-r).\n    # Here `r` is chosen optimally for that `ai`?\n    # No, `r` is constrained by `maxChanges`.\n    # For a fixed `ai`, we can pick any `r` such that `k-r <= maxChanges` and `r <= available_ones`.\n    # We want to minimize `sum_dist(...) + 2*(k-r)`.\n    # `sum_dist(...)` is increasing in `r` (roughly). `2*(k-r)` is decreasing.\n    # So for a fixed `ai`, we can find the optimal `r` by checking the boundary or small range?\n    # Actually, for a fixed `ai`, the \"closest r ones\" are well-defined.\n    # Let `D_ai(r)` be the sum of distances of the `r` closest ones to `ai`.\n    # We want `min_{r in [L, R]} D_ai(r) + 2*(k-r)`.\n    # Since `D_ai(r)` is convex-ish (marginal cost increases as we go further out), and `2*(k-r)` is linear decreasing, the total function is convex.\n    # We can find the minimum by checking the derivative or just iterating if the range is small?\n    # The range of valid `r` for a fixed `ai` is `max(0, k-maxChanges)` to `min(k, available)`.\n    # This range can be large.\n    \n    # However, note that `D_ai(r)` is the sum of the `r` smallest distances.\n    # The marginal cost of adding the `r-th` one is its distance.\n    # We should keep adding ones as long as `distance < 2`.\n    # If `distance > 2`, it's cheaper to create a new one (cost 2).\n    # If `distance == 2`, it doesn't matter.\n    \n    # So, for a fixed `ai`:\n    #   Identify all other ones and their distances to `ai`.\n    #   Sort them by distance.\n    #   Pick ones with distance < 2.\n    #   Pick ones with distance == 2 up to the limit imposed by `maxChanges`?\n    #   Actually, we just need `k` ones total.\n    #   Let `r` be the number of existing ones picked.\n    #   We pick existing ones with smallest distances.\n    #   Stop when:\n    #     1. We have picked `k` ones (if all existing).\n    #     2. The next closest existing one has distance > 2. (Because creating costs 2).\n    #     3. We hit the `maxChanges` limit? No, `maxChanges` limits created ones, not existing ones.\n    #        Created ones = `k - r`.\n    #        Constraint: `k - r <= maxChanges` => `r >= k - maxChanges`.\n    #   So, we MUST pick at least `k - maxChanges` existing ones.\n    #   We SHOULD pick existing ones as long as their distance < 2.\n    #   If distance == 2, it's neutral.\n    #   If distance > 2, we prefer creating.\n    \n    #   So, optimal `r` for `ai`:\n    #     Let `req_min_r = max(0, k - maxChanges)`.\n    #     Let `dists` be sorted distances of available ones to `ai`.\n    #     We must pick at least `req_min_r` ones.\n    #     We should pick all ones with `dist < 2`.\n    #     Let `cnt_strict` be number of ones with `dist < 2`.\n    #     If `cnt_strict >= req_min_r`:\n    #         We can pick `r = cnt_strict` (or more if dist==2?).\n    #         Actually, if we pick one with dist=2, cost is 2. If we create, cost is 2.\n    #         So we can mix.\n    #         Ideally, we pick all with dist < 2.\n    #         Then, if we need more to satisfy `req_min_r`, we pick those with dist=2.\n    #         If we still need more (because `req_min_r` is high), we are forced to pick ones with dist > 2?\n    #         Yes, if `req_min_r > cnt_strict + cnt_eq_2`.\n    #     So, `r` is determined by:\n    #         Start with `r = req_min_r`.\n    #         If `req_min_r` ones include some with dist > 2, we might want to swap?\n    #         No, we MUST pick `req_min_r`. We can pick MORE if it helps?\n    #         Picking an extra one with dist < 2 saves 2 (creation cost) but adds dist. Net save 2-dist > 0. Good.\n    #         Picking an extra one with dist == 2 saves 2, adds 2. Net 0. Neutral.\n    #         Picking an extra one with dist > 2 saves 2, adds dist. Net save 2-dist < 0. Bad.\n    #     So, we should pick all ones with dist < 2.\n    #     We MAY pick ones with dist == 2.\n    #     We MUST pick at least `req_min_r` ones.\n    \n    #   Algorithm for fixed `ai`:\n    #     1. Calculate distances to all available ones.\n    #     2. Sort distances.\n    #     3. Let `req = max(0, k - maxChanges)`.\n    #     4. Let `available = len(dists)`.\n    #     5. If `req > available`, impossible (but constraints guarantee solvability).\n    #     6. We must pick at least `req` ones.\n    #     7. We want to pick all ones with `d < 2`.\n    #     8. Let `cnt_less` be number of ones with `d < 2`.\n    #     9. Let `cnt_eq` be number of ones with `d == 2`.\n    #     10. If `cnt_less >= req`:\n    #           We pick `r = cnt_less`. (We could pick more eq, but it doesn't change cost).\n    #           Cost = sum(distances of these `r` ones) + 2*(k-r).\n    #           Note: If we pick `r` ones, and `r < k`, we create `k-r`.\n    #           Wait, if `cnt_less >= k`, we just pick `k` ones with smallest distances.\n    #           Actually, if `dist < 2`, it's always better to pick than create.\n    #           So we pick all `d < 2`.\n    #           If count of `d < 2` is `>= k`, we pick `k` closest.\n    #           If count of `d < 2` is `< k`, we pick all `d < 2`, and then we need `k - cnt_less` more.\n    #           Next best are `d == 2`.\n    #           We pick min(cnt_eq, k - cnt_less) ones with `d == 2`.\n    #           Remaining needed: `k - cnt_less - cnt_eq_picked`.\n    #           These must be created? Or picked with `d > 2`?\n    #           Creating costs 2. Picking `d > 2` costs `d > 2`. So create.\n    #           BUT we have a constraint: `created <= maxChanges`.\n    #           `created = k - r`.\n    #           So `r >= k - maxChanges`.\n    #           If our greedy selection results in `r < k - maxChanges`, we are forced to pick more existing ones, even if `d > 2`.\n    \n    #   Refined Greedy for fixed `ai`:\n    #     Let `must_pick = max(0, k - maxChanges)`.\n    #     Let `want_pick = k`. (We want to pick up to k, preferring cheap ones).\n    #     Actually, we pick `r` ones.\n    #     Cost = Sum(distances of r closest) + 2*(k-r).\n    #     Subject to: `must_pick <= r <= min(k, available)`.\n    #     And we prefer smaller distances.\n    #     So, we sort distances `d_1 <= d_2 <= ...`.\n    #     We MUST pick at least `must_pick` ones.\n    #     Should we pick more?\n    #     Only if the next distance `d_{r+1} < 2`.\n    #     If `d_{r+1} == 2`, cost is same.\n    #     If `d_{r+1} > 2`, cost increases.\n    \n    #     So:\n    #     1. Pick `r = must_pick`. Calculate cost with these `r` closest ones.\n    #     2. While `r < min(k, available)` and `d_{r+1} < 2`:\n    #          Increment `r`. Recalculate cost?\n    #          Actually, just add `d_{r+1}` to sum, subtract 2 from creation cost.\n    #          Net change: `d_{r+1} - 2`.\n    #          If `d_{r+1} < 2`, net change negative. Good.\n    #     3. Stop when `d_{r+1} >= 2` or `r == k` or `r == available`.\n    \n    #   This gives the optimal cost for `ai`.\n    #   We iterate all `ai`.\n    #   Complexity: O(N^2) if we sort distances for each `ai`.\n    #   We need O(N log N) or O(N).\n    \n    #   Optimization:\n    #   The distances are `|ones[i] - ai|`.\n    #   As `ai` moves, the sorted order of distances changes slowly?\n    #   Not really.\n    \n    #   However, notice that we only care about `d < 2`.\n    #   `d < 2` means `d=0` or `d=1`.\n    #   `d=0`: `ones[i] == ai`.\n    #   `d=1`: `ones[i] == ai-1` or `ai+1`.\n    #   So for each `ai`, only a constant number of ones have distance < 2.\n    #   Most ones have distance >= 2.\n    #   \n    #   For `ai`, the \"want\" phase picks all ones with `d < 2`.\n    #   There are at most 3 such ones (ai-1, ai, ai+1).\n    #   Let `cnt_strict` be the number of ones with `d < 2`.\n    #   Let `sum_strict` be the sum of their distances.\n    #   \n    #   Case 1: `must_pick <= cnt_strict`.\n    #     We pick `r = cnt_strict` (since adding more would mean `d >= 2`, which is not strictly better).\n    #     Actually, if `cnt_strict < k`, we might stop there.\n    #     If `cnt_strict >= k`, we pick `k` closest.\n    #     But since only 3 have `d<2`, and others have `d>=2`, if `k > 3`, we definitely stop at `cnt_strict`?\n    #     No, if `k` is small, we might pick only some.\n    #     \n    #     Let's just calculate the cost for `r = max(must_pick, cnt_strict)`?\n    #     No.\n    #     \n    #     Let `r_opt` be the number of existing ones picked.\n    #     We know `r_opt >= must_pick`.\n    #     We know we should pick all ones with `d < 2`.\n    #     Let `S_<2` be the set of ones with `d < 2`.\n    #     Let `cnt_<2` = |S_<2|.\n    #     Let `sum_<2` = sum of distances in S_<2.\n    #     \n    #     If `must_pick <= cnt_<2`:\n    #         We can pick `r = cnt_<2`.\n    #         Are there ones with `d=2` we should pick?\n    #         Picking a `d=2` one changes cost by `+2 -2 = 0`.\n    #         So cost is same.\n    #         We can just use `r = cnt_<2`.\n    #         Cost = `sum_<2` + 2*(k - cnt_<2).\n    #     If `must_pick > cnt_<2`:\n    #         We MUST pick `must_pick` ones.\n    #         We pick all `cnt_<2` ones (cost `sum_<2`).\n    #         We need `must_pick - cnt_<2` more.\n    #         These must be the next closest ones.\n    #         Since `cnt_<2` ones are done, the next closest are `d=2`, then `d=3`, etc.\n    #         We pick the closest ones until we reach `must_pick`.\n    #         Let `extra = must_pick - cnt_<2`.\n    #         We need sum of smallest `extra` distances from the remaining ones.\n    #         Cost = `sum_<2` + `sum_extra` + 2*(k - must_pick).\n    \n    #   So, for each `ai`, we need:\n    #     1. Sum of distances of all ones with `d < 2`.\n    #     2. Sum of smallest `extra` distances among ones with `d >= 2`.\n    \n    #   Step 1 is easy (check neighbors).\n    #   Step 2: `extra` can be up to `k`.\n    #   But note: if `extra` is large, we are summing many distances.\n    #   However, `must_pick = k - maxChanges`.\n    #   If `maxChanges` is small, `must_pick` is large.\n    #   If `maxChanges` is large, `must_pick` is small (maybe 0).\n    \n    #   If `must_pick <= cnt_<2`, we don't need Step 2.\n    #   If `must_pick > cnt_<2`, we need Step 2.\n    #   `cnt_<2` is at most 3.\n    #   So if `must_pick > 3`, we need Step 2.\n    #   This happens when `k - maxChanges > 3`.\n    \n    #   In this case, we need the sum of the smallest `extra` distances.\n    #   The distances are `|ones[i] - ai|`.\n    #   We need the sum of the `extra` smallest values of `|ones[i] - ai|` for `ones[i]` not in S_<2.\n    \n    #   This is still hard to do for every `ai` in O(1).\n    \n    #   Given the complexity, and N=10^5, O(N log N) is acceptable.\n    #   We can use a Segment Tree or Fenwick Tree?\n    #   Or simply:\n    #   Precompute prefix sums of `ones`.\n    #   For a fixed `ai`, the distances to `ones` are V-shaped.\n    #   The smallest distances are around `ai`.\n    #   We can find the range of `ones` indices that give the smallest distances.\n    #   Specifically, we want the `extra` closest ones.\n    #   We can use binary search to find how far left/right we need to go.\n    #   Then use prefix sums to calculate the sum of distances.\n    \n    #   This would be O(log N) per `ai`. Total O(N log N).\n    \n    #   Implementation details:\n    #   - Precompute `ones` array and prefix sums of `ones`.\n    #   - For each `ai`:\n    #       - Identify S_<2.\n    #       - Calculate `must_pick`.\n    #       - If `must_pick <= len(S_<2)`, cost is simple.\n    #       - Else, `extra = must_pick - len(S_<2)`.\n    #         - Find the `extra` closest ones in `ones` excluding S_<2.\n    #         - Calculate their sum of distances.\n    #         - Add to cost.\n    \n    #   How to find `extra` closest ones efficiently?\n    #   The distances increase as we move away from `ai`.\n    #   We can expand a window around `ai` in `ones` array.\n    #   Use two pointers or binary search to determine the cut-off distance?\n    #   Since we need exactly `extra` items, and they are the closest, they will form a contiguous block in `ones` around `ai` (possibly excluding the immediate neighbors if they were S_<2? No, S_<2 are included in the \"closest\" logic anyway).\n    #   Actually, S_<2 are just a subset.\n    #   We can just take the `must_pick` closest ones overall.\n    #   If `must_pick <= cnt_<2`, we take all S_<2.\n    #   If `must_pick > cnt_<2`, we take all S_<2 and the next `extra` closest.\n    #   The next closest are those with smallest `|ones[i]-ai|`.\n    #   We can find the index in `ones` array corresponding to the `must_pick`-th closest one.\n    #   This index will be within a range `[L, R]` around the insertion point of `ai`.\n    #   We can binary search for the radius `D` such that count of ones with `|ones[i]-ai| <= D` is at least `must_pick`.\n    #   Then sum distances for those.\n    \n    #   This is feasible.\n\n    pass\n\n    # Final Implementation Plan:\n    # 1. Precompute `ones` and `prefix_sums`.\n    # 2. Initialize `global_min = infinity`.\n    # 3. Loop `ai` from 0 to n-1:\n    #    a. Determine `must_pick = max(0, k - maxChanges)`.\n    #    b. If `must_pick == 0`:\n    #       - We can pick 0 existing ones if allowed?\n    #       - Check if we can create all k. Cost 2*k.\n    #       - But we might pick some if close.\n    #       - Actually, if `must_pick == 0`, we are not forced to pick any.\n    #       - We pick ones with `d < 2`.\n    #       - Let `cnt_<2` be count of ones with `d < 2`.\n    #       - We pick `r = min(k, cnt_<2)`.\n    #       - Cost = sum_dist(r) + 2*(k-r).\n    #    c. If `must_pick > 0`:\n    #       - We MUST pick `must_pick` ones.\n    #       - We pick the `must_pick` closest ones.\n    #       - Let `r = must_pick`.\n    #       - Check if we can improve by picking more?\n    #       - Only if next closest has `d < 2`.\n    #       - So, find `r` such that we pick all with `d < 2` and at least `must_pick`.\n    #       - Actually, simpler:\n    #         - Find the `must_pick` closest ones. Sum their distances.\n    #         - Check if there are more ones with `d < 2` beyond these `must_pick`.\n    #         - If yes, pick them too.\n    #         - Continue until `d >= 2` or `r == k`.\n    #    d. Update `global_min`.\n    \n    # To implement \"Find sum of r closest ones to ai\":\n    #   Use binary search on distance `D` to find how many ones are within `D`.\n    #   Then calculate sum.\n\n    import math\n\n    ones = [i for i, x in enumerate(nums) if x == 1]\n    total_ones = len(ones)\n    if total_ones == 0:\n        # Must create all\n        return 2 * k\n\n    # Prefix sums of ones indices for fast range sum\n    # P[i] = sum(ones[0]...ones[i-1])\n    P = [0] * (total_ones + 1)\n    for i in range(total_ones):\n        P[i+1] = P[i] + ones[i]\n\n    def get_sum_dist(ai, count):\n        \"\"\"\n        Returns the sum of distances of the 'count' closest ones to ai.\n        If count > total_ones, returns infinity or handles error.\n        \"\"\"\n        if count <= 0:\n            return 0\n        if count > total_ones:\n            return float('inf')\n        \n        # Binary search for the smallest distance D such that there are at least 'count' ones within distance D.\n        # Low=0, High=n\n        low, high = 0, n\n        while low < high:\n            mid = (low + high) // 2\n            # Count ones in [ai-mid, ai+mid]\n            # Find index in ones of first one >= ai-mid\n            l_idx = bisect_left(ones, ai - mid)\n            # Find index in ones of first one > ai+mid\n            r_idx = bisect_right(ones, ai + mid)\n            cnt = r_idx - l_idx\n            if cnt >= count:\n                high = mid\n            else:\n                low = mid + 1\n        \n        D = low\n        # Now D is the threshold distance.\n        # There might be multiple ones at distance D.\n        # We need exactly 'count' ones.\n        # Count ones with dist < D and dist == D.\n        \n        # Ones with dist < D: range [ai-D+1, ai+D-1]\n        l_idx_lt = bisect_left(ones, ai - D + 1)\n        r_idx_lt = bisect_right(ones, ai + D - 1)\n        cnt_lt = r_idx_lt - l_idx_lt\n        \n        # Ones with dist == D: range [ai-D, ai+D]\n        l_idx_eq = bisect_left(ones, ai - D)\n        r_idx_eq = bisect_right(ones, ai + D)\n        cnt_eq = r_idx_eq - l_idx_eq\n        \n        # We need 'count' ones.\n        # Take all cnt_lt ones.\n        # Take 'count - cnt_lt' ones from the cnt_eq ones.\n        \n        needed_from_eq = count - cnt_lt\n        \n        # Sum of distances for cnt_lt ones:\n        # Sum of |one - ai| for one in ones[l_idx_lt:r_idx_lt]\n        # = Sum(ai - one) for one < ai + Sum(one - ai) for one > ai\n        # Since range is symmetric around ai? No.\n        # But we can split at ai.\n        \n        # Actually, easier:\n        # Sum_{j=l_idx_lt}^{r_idx_lt-1} |ones[j] - ai|\n        # Split into left part (ones[j] <= ai) and right part (ones[j] > ai).\n        \n        # Left part: ones[j] in [ai-D+1, ai]\n        # Right part: ones[j] in (ai, ai+D-1]\n        \n        # We can compute sum of ones in range [l, r] using P.\n        # Sum of distances = ai * count_left - sum_ones_left + sum_ones_right - ai * count_right\n        \n        # Let's find indices for <= ai and > ai within the lt range.\n        # Index of first one > ai\n        idx_ai = bisect_right(ones, ai)\n        \n        # Left lt: indices [l_idx_lt, min(idx_ai, r_idx_lt))\n        l_end = min(idx_ai, r_idx_lt)\n        count_left_lt = l_end -