      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    \n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    # Adjacency list: book i requires books in req[i]\n    # We need to find the set of books required to read book 1.\n    # This is equivalent to finding all ancestors of node 1 in the dependency graph.\n    # The graph edges go from prerequisite -> dependent.\n    # So if book A is required for book B, there is an edge A -> B.\n    # We want to find all nodes that can reach node 1? No.\n    # \"Book i assumes that you have read C_i books... P_{i,j}\"\n    # This means to read book i, you must have read P_{i,1}, ..., P_{i,C_i}.\n    # So P_{i,j} is a prerequisite for i.\n    # Edge direction: P_{i,j} -> i.\n    # To read book 1, we need to read its prerequisites, and their prerequisites, etc.\n    # So we need to find all nodes that can reach node 1 in the graph where edges are Prerequisite -> Dependent.\n    # Alternatively, build a reverse graph: Dependent -> Prerequisite.\n    # Then start BFS/DFS from node 1 in the reverse graph to find all reachable nodes.\n    # These are exactly the books we must read.\n    \n    # Let's build the reverse graph: adj[u] contains v if u is a prerequisite for v (v depends on u).\n    # Actually, simpler: let's just store the dependencies directly.\n    # deps[i] = list of books required to read book i.\n    # We want the set S such that 1 is in S, and for any i in S, all deps[i] are in S.\n    # This is the closure of {1} under the dependency relation.\n    # We can use BFS starting from 1.\n    # Queue: books whose requirements are fully satisfied and which are needed.\n    # But we don't know which ones are needed until we trace back.\n    \n    # Better approach:\n    # Build a graph where edge u -> v means u is required for v.\n    # We want to find all ancestors of 1.\n    # Reverse the graph: edge v -> u means v requires u.\n    # Then find all nodes reachable from 1 in this reversed graph.\n    # These nodes form the set of books we must read.\n    \n    # Let rev_deps[v] = list of u such that v requires u.\n    # Wait, the input gives for each book i, the list of books it requires.\n    # So for book i, we have deps[i] = [P_{i,1}, ..., P_{i,C_i}].\n    # To find all books required for book 1, we can do a DFS/BFS starting from 1.\n    # When we visit book i, we add all P_{i,j} to our queue/stack if not visited.\n    \n    # Collect required books\n    required_books = set()\n    stack = [1]\n    required_books.add(1)\n    \n    # We need to store the dependencies. Since N is up to 2e5, we should read them into a list.\n    # deps[i] will be the list of prerequisites for book i+1 (since books are 1-indexed)\n    \n    deps = [[] for _ in range(N + 1)]\n    \n    for i in range(1, N + 1):\n        try:\n            c_i = int(next(iterator))\n            for _ in range(c_i):\n                p_ij = int(next(iterator))\n                deps[i].append(p_ij)\n        except StopIteration:\n            break\n            \n    # Now traverse to find all required books\n    # Using a stack for DFS\n    stack = [1]\n    visited = set([1])\n    \n    while stack:\n        curr = stack.pop()\n        for prereq in deps[curr]:\n            if prereq not in visited:\n                visited.add(prereq)\n                stack.append(prereq)\n                \n    # Remove book 1 from the output set\n    output_books = visited - {1}\n    \n    # Now we need to print them in a valid reading order.\n    # A valid reading order is a topological sort of the subgraph induced by the required books.\n    # The edges in the subgraph are: for each u in output_books U {1}, and each v in deps[u], if v is in output_books U {1}, then v -> u (v must be read before u).\n    # We need to output the books in output_books in an order such that if v is a prerequisite for u, v comes before u.\n    \n    # Let's build the subgraph of required books including 1.\n    # Nodes: visited\n    # Edges: for each u in visited, for each v in deps[u], if v in visited, add edge v -> u.\n    \n    # Compute in-degrees within the subgraph of required books.\n    # Note: We only care about ordering the books in output_books. Book 1 is the target and doesn't need to be printed, but it affects the ordering of others.\n    # Actually, we can compute a topological sort for all nodes in 'visited', then filter out 1 and print the rest.\n    \n    # Calculate in-degree for each node in the subgraph 'visited'\n    in_degree = {node: 0 for node in visited}\n    adj_sub = {node: [] for node in visited}\n    \n    for u in visited:\n        for v in deps[u]:\n            if v in visited:\n                # Edge v -> u\n                adj_sub[v].append(u)\n                in_degree[u] += 1\n                \n    # Kahn's algorithm for topological sort\n    queue = deque()\n    for node in visited:\n        if in_degree[node] == 0:\n            queue.append(node)\n            \n    topo_order = []\n    while queue:\n        u = queue.popleft()\n        topo_order.append(u)\n        for v in adj_sub[u]:\n            in_degree[v] -= 1\n            if in_degree[v] == 0:\n                queue.append(v)\n                \n    # Filter out book 1\n    result = [str(b) for b in topo_order if b != 1]\n    \n    print(\" \".join(result))\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    solve()\n```",
