s at index 2.\n    # Remove it. String becomes \"AB\".\n    # Output \"AB\".\n    # Stack:\n    # A -> [A]\n    # B -> [A, B]\n    # A -> [A, B, A]\n    # B -> [A, B, A, B]\n    # C -> [A, B, A, B, C] -> Last 3: A, B, C. Match. Pop 3. Stack: [A, B].\n    # Result \"AB\". Correct.\n    \n    # It seems the stack approach works because whenever we form \"ABC\" at the end of the current processed prefix, \n    # it corresponds to a valid removal. Since we process left-to-right, the first time we complete an \"ABC\" \n    # ending at current position i, it is the earliest ending ABC. Does it correspond to the leftmost ABC?\n    # Not necessarily the leftmost starting ABC, but the problem says \"leftmost occurrence\".\n    # Occurrences are defined by their start index.\n    # If we have multiple ABCs, we pick the one with the smallest start index.\n    \n    # Example: \"ABCABC\"\n    # ABC at 0, ABC at 3. Leftmost is at 0.\n    # Remove index 0-2. String becomes \"ABC\".\n    # Remove index 0-2. String becomes \"\".\n    \n    # Stack on \"ABCABC\":\n    # A -> [A]\n    # B -> [A, B]\n    # C -> [A, B, C] -> Match. Pop. Stack [].\n    # A -> [A]\n    # B -> [A, B]\n    # C -> [A, B, C] -> Match. Pop. Stack [].\n    # Result \"\". Correct.\n    \n    # Example: \"ABABC\"\n    # ABC at 2. Only one.\n    # Stack result \"AB\". Correct.\n    \n    # Is it possible that removing a later ABC prevents an earlier ABC from forming?\n    # No, because we are removing characters. Removing characters brings other characters together.\n    # The stack approach essentially simulates the reduction.\n    # Key insight: If a string contains \"ABC\", and we remove the leftmost one, the remaining parts concatenate.\n    # The stack approach processes characters sequentially. When it sees \"ABC\" formed by the last few characters pushed,\n    # it removes them. This is equivalent to removing the rightmost completed \"ABC\" in the current reduced string.\n    # But does removing the rightm