exclusive) has sum = target.\n    # The length is e - s.\n    # In terms of the doubled array, if we look at indices within [0, 2n-1],\n    # a subarray that doesn't wrap more than once (i.e., fits within 2n elements)\n    # can be found by checking pairs (i, j) with 0 <= i < j <= 2n such that\n    # prefix[j] - prefix[i] == target, and the actual length is (j - i) modulo n? \n    # No, that's not quite right because the \"position\" in the infinite array matters.\n    \n    # Correct approach:\n    # We want min(j - i) such that there exist integers k >= 0 where:\n    #   prefix[j] - prefix[i] + k * total_sum == target\n    # where 0 <= i, j <= 2n (we use 2n to cover all possible wrap-around cases for a single cycle),\n    # and the actual start position in the infinite array is i (mod n), end is j (mod n) + k*n.\n    # But wait, the length would be (j - i) + k * n.\n    \n    # So for each pair (i, j) with 0 <= i < j <= 2n, we compute diff = prefix[j] - prefix[i].\n    # If diff > target, skip (since all elements are positive, larger diffs won't help with smaller k).\n    # If diff <= target, we need:\n    #   diff + k * total_sum = target\n    #   => k = (target - diff) / total_sum\n    # This requires (target - diff) >= 0 and (target - diff) % total_sum == 0.\n    # If so, k = (target - diff) // total_sum.\n    # The length of the subarray is (j - i) + k * n.\n    # We want to minimize this length over all valid (i, j, k).\n    \n    # However, iterating over all pairs (i, j) is O(n^2) which is too slow for n=10^5.\n    \n    # Better approach using sliding window on the doubled array for the case when k=0,\n    # and then handling k > 0 separately.\n    \n    # Observation: If total_sum == 0, but nums[i] >= 1, so total_sum >= n >= 1.\n    \n    # Case 1: The optimal subarray does not wrap around more than once in the sense that\n    # it fits within two consecutive copies of nums. This means k=0 or k=1 effectively\n    # when considering the minimal representation. Actually, if we allow the subarray to\n    # span at most 2n elements, we can find all candidates with k=0.\n    \n    # For k >= 1, we have:\n    #   prefix[j] - prefix[i] = target - k * total_sum\n    # Since prefix[j] - prefix[i] >= 0 (all nums positive), we need target - k * total_sum >= 0.\n    # Also, prefix[j] - prefix[i] <= prefix[2n] - prefix[0] = 2 * total_sum.\n    # So target - k * total_sum <= 2 * total_sum => k * total_sum >= target - 2 * total_sum.\n    # => k >= (target - 2 * total_sum) / total_sum = target/total_sum - 2.\n    # And k <= target / total_sum.\n    \n    # So k ranges from max(0, ceil((target - 2*total_sum)/total_sum)) to floor(target/total_sum).\n    # But actually, since we're looking for the minimum length, and length = (j-i) + k*n,\n    # larger k means larger length (since n >= 1). So we should prefer smaller k.\n    \n    # Let's iterate over possible k values. For each k, we need to find if there exists\n    # a pair (i, j) with 0 <= i < j <= 2n such that prefix[j] - prefix[i] = target - k * total_sum.\n    # Let rem = target - k * total_sum. We need rem >= 0.\n    # If rem == 0, then we need prefix[j] == prefix[i], which implies j == i since prefix is strictly increasing\n    # (all nums[i] >= 1). But j > i, so rem cannot be 0 unless... wait, if rem=0, then we need a subarray\n    # of sum 0, which is impossible with positive integers. So rem > 0.\n    \n    # For a fixed rem, we want to find min(j - i) such that prefix[j] - prefix[i] = rem.\n    # We can use a hash map to store the last occurrence of each prefix sum value, or use two pointers.\n    # Since prefix is sorted, we can use two pointers for each rem.\n    \n    # But the number of k values can be up to target / total_sum, which can be up to 10^9 / 1 = 10^9. Too large.\n    \n    # Alternative insight:\n    # The minimum length subarray will have length at most 2n. Why? Because if a subarray spans more than\n    # 2n elements, it contains at least two full copies