      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom math import floor\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    \n    if not input_data:\n        return\n\n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    \n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n        \n        Q = []\n        for _ in range(N):\n            Q.append(int(next(iterator)))\n            \n        A = []\n        for _ in range(N):\n            A.append(int(next(iterator)))\n            \n        B = []\n        for _ in range(N):\n            B.append(int(next(iterator)))\n            \n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    # We want to maximize x + y subject to:\n    # For each i: x * A[i] + y * B[i] <= Q[i]\n    # x >= 0, y >= 0, integers\n    \n    # Since N is small (up to 10), but Q_i is up to 10^6, x+y can be large.\n    # However, the constraints are linear. This is an Integer Linear Programming problem.\n    # But specifically, it's a 2-variable problem.\n    # Let's analyze the constraints.\n    # For each ingredient i, we have: A[i]*x + B[i]*y <= Q[i]\n    \n    # If A[i] == 0 and B[i] == 0, the constraint is 0 <= Q[i], which is always true since Q[i] >= 1.\n    # If A[i] > 0 and B[i] == 0, then x <= Q[i] / A[i].\n    # If A[i] == 0 and B[i] > 0, then y <= Q[i] / B[i].\n    # If A[i] > 0 and B[i] > 0, then y <= (Q[i] - A[i]*x) / B[i].\n    \n    # We can iterate on x. What is the max possible x?\n    # Max x is bounded by min(Q[i] / A[i]) for all i where A[i] > 0.\n    # Similarly for y.\n    # Since Q[i] <= 10^6 and A[i] >= 1 (for some i), max x can be around 10^6.\n    # Iterating x from 0 to 10^6 is feasible (10^6 operations).\n    \n    # Calculate upper bound for x\n    max_x = float('inf')\n    has_A_constraint = False\n    for i in range(N):\n        if A[i] > 0:\n            has_A_constraint = True\n            max_x = min(max_x, floor(Q[i] / A[i]))\n    \n    if max_x == float('inf'):\n        max_x = 0 # Should not happen given constraints \"There is an i such that A_i >= 1\"\n    \n    # Actually, let's just set a safe upper bound.\n    # Max x is at most 10^6 (since min A_i >= 1 and max Q_i = 10^6).\n    limit_x = 10**6 + 7\n    \n    best_total = 0\n    \n    # Iterate over possible values of x\n    # Optimization: We can calculate the max y for a given x.\n    # For a fixed x, y must satisfy:\n    # B[i]*y <= Q[i] - A[i]*x  for all i\n    # So y <= floor((Q[i] - A[i]*x) / B[i]) for all i where B[i] > 0.\n    # If B[i] == 0, the constraint is 0 <= Q[i] - A[i]*x. \n    #   If Q[i] - A[i]*x < 0, then this x is invalid.\n    \n    for x in range(limit_x + 1):\n        # Check if x is valid based on B[i]=0 cases\n        valid_x = True\n        for i in range(N):\n            if B[i] == 0:\n                if A[i] * x > Q[i]:\n                    valid_x = False\n                    break\n        \n        if not valid_x:\n            # Since A[i]>=1 for some i, as x increases, eventually it will fail.\n            # But wait, if A[i]=0 for some i, it doesn't constrain x.\n            # If all A[i]=0, then x can be anything? No, constraint says \"There is an i such that A_i >= 1\".\n            # So there is at least one i with A[i] >= 1.\n            # For that i, if B[i] == 0, then x <= Q[i]/A[i].\n            # If B[i] > 0, then x is constrained by both A and B.\n            # Once x is too large, it becomes invalid.\n            # We can break early if x exceeds the absolute maximum possible x.\n            # Let's compute absolute max x again properly.\n            abs_max_x = 10**6 + 1\n            for i in range(N):\n                if A[i] > 0:\n                    abs_max_x = min(abs_max_x, Q[i] // A[i])\n            if x > abs_max_x:\n                break\n            continue\n            \n        # Calculate max y for this x\n        max_y_for_x = float('inf')\n        possible_y_exists = True\n        \n        for i in range(N):\n            remaining = Q[i] - A[i] * x\n            if remaining < 0:\n                possible_y_exists = False\n                break\n            \n            if B[i] > 0:\n                max_y_for_x = min(max_y_for_x, remaining // B[i])\n            else:\n                # B[i] == 0, constraint is 0*y <= remaining => 0 <= remaining.\n                # Already checked remaining >= 0 above.\n                pass\n        \n        if not possible_y_exists:\n            continue\n            \n        if max_y_for_x == float('inf'):\n            # This means for all i, B[i] == 0.\n            # Then y can be arbitrarily large?\n            # But constraint says \"There is an i such that B_i >= 1\".\n            # So this case shouldn't happen if our iteration covers all constraints.\n            # Wait, if max_y_for_x is inf, it means no B[i] > 0 constrained it.\n            # But there is at least one i with B[i] >= 1.\n            # So max_y_for_x will be finite.\n            max_y_for_x = 0 \n            \n        y = int(max_y_for_x)\n        current_total = x + y\n        if current_total > best_total:\n            best_total = current_total\n            \n    print(best_total)\n\nsolve()\n```",
