matches Sample 1.\n    \n    # Sample 2: N=5, T=1, M=2.\n    # Only 1 team. All players in same team. But 1 and 3 incompatible. Impossible.\n    # Labelled assignments: 1^5 = 1.\n    # Invalid because 1 and 3 are in same team.\n    # Count = 0. Matches Sample 2.\n    \n    # Sample 3: N=6, T=4, M=0.\n    # No incompatibilities.\n    # We need the number of ways to partition 6 elements into exactly 4 non-empty indistinguishable subsets.\n    # This is the Stirling numbers of the second kind S(6, 4).\n    # S(6,4) = 65. Matches Sample 3.\n    \n    # So the problem asks for the number of partitions of N items into exactly T non-empty indistinguishable subsets, subject to constraints.\n    # Since N is small (<= 10), we can iterate over all possible assignments of players to T labelled teams, check validity, and then handle the indistinguishability.\n    \n    # However, handling indistinguishability by dividing by T! is only correct if every valid assignment results in T non-empty teams AND no symmetry within the assignment itself (which is true for labelled teams if they are distinct).\n    # Actually, if we count the number of valid surjective functions f: Players -> {1..T} such that no incompatible pair maps to the same value, let this count be C.\n    # Then the number of partitions is C / T!.\n    # Why? Because each partition into T non-empty sets corresponds to exactly T! labelled assignments (permuting the team labels).\n    # This holds as long as T <= N and we require exactly T non-empty teams.\n    \n    # Algorithm:\n    # 1. Iterate through all T^N assignments.\n    # 2. For each assignment, check:\n    #    a. Is it surjective? (Every team 1..T has at least one player).\n    #    b. Are all incompatibility constraints satisfied? (For each (u,v) in incompatibilities, team[u] != team[v]).\n    # 3. Count the number of such valid assignments.\n    # 4. Result = Count / T!.\n    \n    # Given N <= 10, T <= 10. T^N can be up to 10^10 which is too big.\n    # Wait, N <= 10. 10^10 is 10 billion. That's too slow for Python in typical time limits (usually 2 seconds).\n    # We need a better approach.\n    \n    # Alternative approach:\n    # Since N is very small (up to 10), we can use recursion/backtracking or dynamic programming with bitmask?\n    # Or simply generate partitions directly.\n    # The number of partitions of a set of size N is the Bell number B_N.\n    # B_10 = 115975. This is small!\n    # We can generate all partitions of N players, and for each partition, check if the number of blocks is T and if it satisfies the constraints.\n    \n    # How to generate all partitions of a set of N elements?\n    # We can use a recursive method.\n    # Represent a partition as a list of lists, or more efficiently, an array `assign` where `assign[i]` is the block index of player i.\n    # To avoid duplicates due to block ordering, we can enforce that the first occurrence of block k appears before the first occurrence of block k+1.\n    # Standard algorithm:\n    # Place player 0 in block 0.\n    # For player i (from 1 to N-1):\n    #   Option 1: Put player i in a new block (the next available block index).\n    #   Option 2: Put player i in one of the existing non-empty blocks.\n    \n    # Let's implement this.\n    \n    partitions_count = 0\n    \n    # We will build the partition incrementally.\n    # state: a list `partition` where partition[k] is a list of players in block k.\n    # But checking constraints is easier if we have an array `team_assignment` where `team_assignment[player] = block_index`.\n    \n    # Let's use backtracking to generate canonical partitions.\n    # `assignment` array of size N. assignment[i] is the block ID for player i.\n    # Constraint for canonical form: assignment[0] = 0.\n    # For i > 0, assignment[i] can be any integer from 0 to max(assignment[:i]) + 1.\n    # Specifically, assignment[i] <= max(assignment[0...i-1]) + 1.\n    # And if assignment[i] == max(assignment[0...i-1]) + 1, it creates a new block.\n    \n    # We also need to track the number of blocks used.\n    \n    assignment = [0] * N\n    # assignment[0] is fixed to 0\n    \n    # Precompute incompatibility checks for speed?\n    # For a given assignment, check all M pairs.\n    \n    # Backtracking function\n    # idx: current player index to assign (from 1 to N-1)\n    # max_block: the maximum block index used so far\n    \n    def backtrack(idx, max_block):\n        nonlocal partitions_count\n        \n        if idx == N:\n            # Check if number of blocks is exactly T\n            # Number of blocks is max_block + 1\n            if max_block + 1 == T:\n                # Check constraints\n                valid = True\n                for u, v in incompatibilities:\n                    if assignment[u] == assignment[v]:\n                        valid = False\n                        break\n                if valid:\n                    partitions_count += 1\n            return\n\n        # Determine the range of possible block indices for player `idx`\n        # Can be 0, 1, ..., max_block, or max_block + 1\n        \n        # Try existing blocks\n        for b in range(max_block + 1):\n            assignment[idx] = b\n            backtrack(idx + 1, max_block)\n            \n        # Try new block\n        assignment[idx] = max_block + 1\n        backtrack(idx + 1, max_block + 1)\n\n    # Start backtracking\n    # Player 0 is assigned to block 0.\n    if N == 0:\n        # Edge case N=0? Constraints say 1<=T<=N. So N>=1.\n        pass\n    else:\n        assignment[0] = 0\n        if N > 1:\n            backtrack(1, 0)\n        else:\n            # N=1\n            if T == 1:\n                # Check constraints (none likely for N=1)\n                valid = True\n                for u, v in incompatibilities:\n                    if assignment[u] == assignment[v]:\n                        valid = False\n                        break\n                if valid:\n     