o cost1 operations instead of one cost2.\n    # In that case, we just use cost1 for everything. Cost = D * cost1.\n    \n    # If cost2 < 2 * cost1, then we prefer to use cost2 as much as possible.\n    # How many pairs can we form?\n    # Let cnt be the number of non-zero differences.\n    # We can pair up increments from different elements.\n    # The maximum number of cost2 operations we can use is limited by floor(D / 2) AND the ability to pair distinct indices.\n    \n    # Actually, a simpler way:\n    # Let's consider the total number of increments needed D.\n    # We can use cost2 to cover 2 increments at once, provided they come from different elements.\n    # If we have enough \"spread\" across elements, we can pair almost everything.\n    \n    # Let M = max(d_i). If M <= D - M, then we can pair up all increments such that no single element is used more than its difference allows? \n    # Wait, this is like matching. The condition for being able to cover all D increments using only cost2 (if D is even) or cost2 + one cost1 (if D is odd) is that the maximum difference doesn't exceed the sum of the rest.\n    # Specifically, if max(d_i) <= sum(others), then we can pair up all increments except possibly one if D is odd.\n    # If max(d_i) > sum(others), then the excess must be covered by cost1.\n    \n    # Let's formalize:\n    # Let S = D.\n    # Let mx = max(d_i).\n    # If mx > S - mx, i.e., mx > S/2, then we have \"excess\" increments on one element.\n    # The number of increments that MUST be done via cost1 (single) is 2*mx - S.\n    # Why? Because the other elements can contribute at most S - mx increments. These can be paired with mx's increments.\n    # After pairing (S - mx) increments from mx with all others, we have mx - (S - mx) = 2*mx - S left on the max element.\n    # These remaining increments on the max element must be done individually (cost1), because there are no other elements with positive differences left to pair with.\n    # So, cost = (2*mx - S) * cost1 + (S - mx) * cost2? No wait.\n    # The (S - mx) pairs can be done with cost2. Each pair costs cost2.\n    # The remaining (2*mx - S) singles cost cost1 each.\n    # Total cost = (S - mx) * cost2 + (2*mx - S) * cost1.\n    \n    # If mx <= S - mx, i.e., mx <= S/2, then we can pair up all increments (or all but one if S is odd).\n    # Number of pairs = S // 2.\n    # If S is even, all can be paired. Cost = (S // 2) * cost2.\n    # If S is odd, one single remains. Cost = (S // 2) * cost2 + cost1.\n    \n    # So for a fixed T, the cost is:\n    # S = sum(T - nums[i]) for all i\n    # mx = max(T - nums[i]) = T - min(nums[i])? No, mx = T - min_element_in_nums? No.\n    # d_i = T - nums[i]. So mx_d = T - min(nums). And S = n*T - sum(nums).\n    \n    # Let's verify.\n    # If mx_d <= S/2:\n    #   cost = (S // 2) * cost2 + (S % 2) * cost1\n    # Else:\n    #   cost = (S - mx_d) * cost2 + (2*mx_d - S) * cost1\n    \n    # Now, how to choose T?\n    # T >= max(nums). Let T = max(nums) + k, where k >= 0.\n    # As T increases, S increases. Does the cost increase monotonically?\n    # Not necessarily, because the formula changes based on whether mx_d <= S/2.\n    # However, note that if we increase T by 1, every d_i increases by 1.\n    # S increases by n.\n    # mx_d increases by 1.\n    \n    # Let's analyze the cost function C(T).\n    # Case 1: mx_d <= S/2.\n    # C(T) = (S//2)*cost2 + (S%2)*cost1.\n    # This is roughly (S/2)*cost2. Since S increases with T, C(T) increases.\n    \n    # Case 2: mx_d > S/2.\n    # C(T) = (S - mx_d)*cost2 + (2*mx_d - S)*cost1\n    #      = S*cost2 - mx_d*cost2 + 2*mx_d*cost1 - S*cost1\n    #      = S*(cost2 - cost1) + mx_d*(2*cost1 - cost2)\n    \n    # Recall S = n*T - sum_nums.\n    # mx_d = T - min_nums.\n    # C(T) = (n*T - sum_nums)*(cost2 - cost1) + (T - min_nums)*(2*cost1 - cost2)\n    #      = T * [ n*(cost2 - cost1) + (2*cost1 - cost2) ] - sum_nums*(cost2 - cost1) - min_nums*(2*cost1 - cost2)\n    \n    # 