eeps us in the same regime or switches regimes.\n    \n    # Let's just evaluate T = max(nums) and see if we should go higher.\n    # Actually, since the constraints are large, we can't iterate T.\n    # But notice that if we are in the balanced regime, cost increases with T.\n    # If we are in the excess regime, cost increases with T if (n-1)c2 - (n-2)c1 > 0.\n    # If (n-1)c2 - (n-2)c1 < 0, cost decreases with T in the excess regime.\n    # So if we are in the excess regime and the slope is negative, we should increase T until we hit the balanced regime or infinity?\n    # But eventually, for large T, S ~ n*T, mx_d ~ T.\n    # mx_d <= S/2 becomes T <= n*T/2 => 1 <= n/2 => n >= 2.\n    # For n >= 2, eventually we enter the balanced regime.\n    # So if the slope is negative, we should increase T until we hit the boundary of the balanced regime.\n    \n    # Boundary: mx_d = S/2.\n    # T - min_nums = (n*T - sum_nums) / 2\n    # 2T - 2*min_nums = n*T - sum_nums\n    # sum_nums - 2*min_nums = (n-2)*T\n    # T = (sum_nums - 2*min_nums) / (n-2)\n    \n    # If n=2, the equation is sum_nums - 2*min_nums = 0*T = 0.\n    # If sum_nums == 2*min_nums, then any T satisfies?\n    # For n=2, S = 2T - sum_nums. mx_d = T - min_nums.\n    # mx_d <= S/2 <=> T - min <= T - sum/2 <=> min >= sum/2 <=> 2*min >= sum.\n    # Since sum = min + max, 2*min >= min + max => min >= max.\n    # Since min <= max, this implies min = max.\n    # If nums has distinct elements, min < max, so mx_d > S/2 always for n=2.\n    # So for n=2, we are always in the excess case.\n    # Slope = (2-1)c2 - (2-2)c1 = c2.\n    # If c2 > 0, cost increases with T. So T=max is optimal.\n    \n    # For n > 2:\n    # If we are in excess case and slope < 0, we increase T to the boundary.\n    # T_boundary = ceil((sum_nums - 2*min_nums) / (n-2)).\n    # We should evaluate cost at T = max_nums and T = T_boundary (and maybe T_boundary + 1 to check transition).\n    \n    # Algorithm:\n    # 1. Calculate cost at T = max(nums).\n    # 2. If n > 2 and cost2 < cost1 * (n-2)/(n-1) [i.e., slope < 0 in excess case]:\n    #    Calculate T_boundary = (sum_nums - 2*min_nums) / (n-2).\n    #    Check T candidates around T_boundary.\n    #    Actually, we only care about T >= max(nums).\n    #    If T_boundary < max(nums), then we never reach the balanced regime starting from max(nums)?\n    #    Wait, if T_boundary < max(nums), it means at T=max(nums), we are already past the boundary?\n    #    Let's check.\n    #    At T=max, mx_d = max - min. S = n*max - sum.\n    #    Condition for balanced: max - min <= (n*max - sum)/2.\n    #    2*max - 2*min <= n*max - sum\n    #    sum - 2*min <= (n-2)*max\n    #    max >= (sum - 2*min) / (n-2)\n    #    So if max >= T_boundary, we are in balanced or beyond.\n    #    If max < T_boundary, we are in excess, and increasing T brings us closer to balanced.\n    #    So if we are in excess and slope < 0, we want to increase T to T_boundary.\n    \n    # So, candidates for T:\n    # - T1 = max(nums)\n    # - If T1 is in excess regime and n > 2 and slope < 0:\n    #   T2 = ceil(T_boundary)\n    #   Also check T2 + 1 to handle rounding/transition.\n    \n    # Let's refine.\n    # Compute cost for a given T.\n    # Determine if T is in excess or balanced.\n    # If balanced, cost is monotonic increasing.\n    # If excess, cost is linear in T with slope (n-1)c2 - (n-2)c1.\n    \n    # So:\n    # Start with T = max(nums).\n    # Calculate cost.\n    # Check regime.\n    # If balanced, return cost.\n    # If excess:\n    #   Calculate slope.\n    #   If slope >= 0, return cost (since increasing T will increase or keep cost same).\n    #   If slope < 0, we want to increase T to the boundary.\n    #   T_boundary = (sum_nums - 2*min_nums) / (n-2).\n    #   We need T >= max(nums).\n    #   If T_boundary <= max(nums), then we are already past the boundary?\n    #   No, if T_boundary <= max(nums), then at T=max(nums), we should be balanced or in excess with positive slope?\n    #   Let's re-evaluate the inequality.\n    #   Excess if T < T_boundary.\n    #   Balanced if T >= T_boundary.\n    #   So if T_boundary <= max(nums), then max(nums) is in the balanced region (or exactly at boundary).\n    #   In that case, we don't need to go further.\n    #   If T_boundary > max(nums), then max(nums) is in excess, and we should move to T = floor(T_boundary) or ceil(T_boundary).\n    \n    # So:\n    # T_candidates = [max(nums)]\n    # If n > 2:\n    #   T_bound = (sum_nums - 2 * min_nums) / (n - 2)\n    #   If T_bound > max(nums):\n    #       T_candidates.append(math.floor(T_bound))\n    #       T_candidates.append(math.ceil(T_bound))\n    \n    # For each T in T_candidates, if T < max(nums), skip.\n    # Compute cost for each valid T.\n    # Return min cost modulo 10^9+7.\n    \n    import math\n    \n    n = len(nums)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return 0\n        \n    min_val = min(nums)\n    max_val = max(nums)\n    sum_nums = sum(nums)\n    \n    def get_cost(T):\n        # Calculate S and mx_d\n        # S = sum(T - num for num in nums)\n        # mx_d = T - min_val\n        S = n * T - sum_nums\n        mx_d = T - min_val\n        \n        if mx_d > S - mx_d:\n            # Excess case\n            # Cost = (S - mx_d) * cost2 + (2*mx_d - S) * cost1\n            # Be careful with large numbers\n            c = (S - mx_d) * cost2 + (2 * mx_d - S) * cost1\n        else:\n            # Balanced case\n            # Cost = (S // 2) * cost2 + (S % 2) * cost1\n            c = (S // 2) * cost2 + (S % 2) * cost1\n            \n        return c\n    \n    candidates = set()\n    candidates.add(max_val)\n    \n    if n > 2:\n        # T_boundary = (sum_nums - 2*min_val) / (n-2)\n        # We want to check integers around this value if it's greater than max_val\n        numerator = sum_nums - 2 * min_val\n        denominator = n - 2\n        \n        if numerator > 0: # T_boundary positive\n            # Use float division for comparison\n            t_bound_float = numerator / denominator\n            if t_bound_float > max_val:\n                t_floor = math.floor(t_bound_float)\n                t_ceil = math.ceil(t_bound_float)\n                # Ensure we only consider T >= max_val\n                if t_floor >= max_val:\n                    candidates.add(t_floor)\n                if t_ceil >= max_val:\n                    candidates.add(t_ceil)\n                    \n    min_c = float('inf')\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    for T in candidates:\n        if T < max_val:\n            continue\n        c = get_cost(T)\n        if c < min_c:\n            min_c = c\n            \n    return min_c % MOD\n```",
