    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef canSplitArray(nums: list[int], m: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if it is possible to split the array into n non-empty arrays\n    by recursively splitting subarrays of length >= 2, where each resulting\n    subarray must either have length 1 or sum >= m.\n    \n    Args:\n        nums: List of integers representing the initial array.\n        m: Integer threshold for the sum of subarrays.\n        \n    Returns:\n        True if the array can be fully split into n single-element arrays, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    # If there's only one element, it's already \"split\" into n=1 array of size 1.\n    if n <= 1:\n        return True\n    \n    # Helper function to check if a subarray nums[i:j] (inclusive i, exclusive j) can be fully split.\n    # We use memoization to avoid recomputing states.\n    from functools import lru_cache\n    \n    # Precompute prefix sums for O(1) range sum queries\n    prefix_sum = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for idx in range(n):\n        prefix_sum[idx + 1] = prefix_sum[idx] + nums[idx]\n        \n    def range_sum(i, j):\n        # Sum of nums[i] to nums[j-1]\n        return prefix_sum[j] - prefix_sum[i]\n    \n    @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n    def can_split(i, j):\n        \"\"\"\n        Can the subarray nums[i:j] be split down to all single elements?\n        Returns True if yes, False otherwise.\n        \"\"\"\n        length = j - i\n        \n        # Base case: if length is 1, it's already a single element, so it's valid.\n        if length == 1:\n            return True\n        \n        # Try every possible split point k between i and j-1\n        # Split into nums[i:k] and nums[k:j]\n        for k in range(i + 1, j):\n            left_sum = range_sum(i, k)\n            right_sum = range_sum(k, j)\n            \n            # Check if the split is valid according to the rules:\n            # Each resulting subarray must have length 1 OR sum >= m.\n            left_valid = (k - i == 1) or (left_sum >= m)\n            right_valid = (j - k == 1) or (right_sum >= m)\n            \n            if left_valid and right_valid:\n                # If this split is valid, then we need to check if both sides can be further split completely.\n                if can_split(i, k) and can_split(k, j):\n                    return True\n                    \n        return False\n\n    return can_split(0, n)\n```",
