      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to efficiently answer Q queries asking for the count of consecutive equal character pairs within a substring of S.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nThe count of \"twin\" pairs `(S_p, S_{p+1})` with `S_p = S_{p+1}` in a range `[l, r]` can be reduced to a simple **prefix sum** problem.\n\nIf I precompute a binary array `A` where:\n- `A[i] = 1` if `S[i] == S[i+1]` (the pair starting at position `i` matches)\n- `A[i] = 0` otherwise\n\nThen the number of matches in `[l, r]` equals `prefix[r-1] - prefix[l-1]`, because:\n- Pairs to check start at positions `l, l+1, ..., r-1`\n- The prefix sum up to index `r-1` counts all matches at positions `1` through `r-1`\n- The prefix sum up to index `l-1` counts matches at positions `1` through `l-1`\n- Their difference gives matches at positions `l` through `r-1`\n\n## Algorithm\n\n1. Read N, Q, and string S\n2. Build `pref[0..N-1]` where `pref[i] = number of twin pairs with starting position ≤ i`\n   - Use `pref[0] = 0`, then `pref[i] = pref[i-1] + (1 if S[i-1]==S[i] else 0)`\n3. For each query `(l, r)`:\n   - If `l == r`: answer is 0 (no pairs in single character)\n   - Otherwise: answer is `pref[r-1] - pref[l-1]`\n\nThis runs in **O(N + Q)** time, perfect for the 3×10⁵ constraints.\n\n## Code\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    idx = 0\n    N = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n    Q = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n    S = data[idx]; idx += 1\n    \n    # Build prefix sum: pref[i] = number of twin pairs starting at position <= i\n    # Position i means pair (S[i], S[i+1])\n    pref = [0] * N\n    for i in range(1, N):\n        pref[i] = pref[i-1] + (1 if S[i-1] == S[i] else 0)\n    \n    out = []\n    for _ in range(Q):\n        l = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n        r = int(data[idx]); idx += 1\n        if l == r:\n            out.append(\"0\")\n        else:\n            out.append(str(pref[r-1] - pref[l-1]))\n    \n    sys.stdout.write(\"\\n\".join(out))\n\nsolve()\n```\n\n## Verification with Sample\n\n**Sample 1:** `S = \"mississippi\"`, build `pref`:\n- `pref[0] = 0`\n- `pref[1] = 0` (mi)\n- `pref[2] = 1` (ss ✓)\n- `pref[3] = 1` (si)\n- `pref[4] = 1` (is)\n- `pref[5] = 1` (ss ✓)\n- `pref[6] = 2` (si)\n- `pref[7] = 2` (is)\n- `pref[8] = 2` (ss ✓)\n- `pref[9] = 3` (si)\n- `pref[10] = 3` (ip)\n\nQueries:\n- `(3,9)`: `pref[8] - pref[2] = 2 - 1 = 2` ✓\n- `(4,10)`: `pref[9] - pref[3] = 3 - 1 = 2` ✓\n- `(4,6)`: `pref[5] - pref[3] = 1 - 1 = 0` ✓\n- `(7,7)`: `l==r`, returns 0 ✓\n\n**Sample 2:** `S = \"aaaaa\"` → `pref = [0,1,2,3,4]`, query `(1,5)`: `pref[4] - pref[0] = 4` ✓",
