      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef getGoodIndices(variables: list[list[int]], target: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a list of indices i such that ((a_i^b_i % 10)^c_i) % m_i == target.\n    \"\"\"\n    good_indices = []\n    for i, var in enumerate(variables):\n        a, b, c, m = var\n        \n        # Calculate (a^b) % 10 efficiently\n        # Since we only care about the last digit, we can use modular exponentiation with mod 10\n        # However, Python's pow(a, b, 10) handles this efficiently.\n        base = pow(a, b, 10)\n        \n        # Calculate (base^c) % m\n        result = pow(base, c, m)\n        \n        if result == target:\n            good_indices.append(i)\n            \n    return good_indices\n```",
