    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_441",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nYou are given a permutation P = (P_1, P_2, \\dots, P_N) of (1, 2, \\dots, N).\nA length-K sequence of indices (i_1, i_2, \\dots, i_K) is called a good index sequence if it satisfies both of the following conditions:\n\n- 1 \\leq i_1 < i_2 < \\dots < i_K \\leq N.\n- The subsequence (P_{{i_1}}, P_{{i_2}}, \\dots, P_{{i_K}}) can be obtained by rearranging some consecutive K integers.\nFormally, there exists an integer a such that \\lbrace P_{{i_1}},P_{{i_2}},\\dots,P_{{i_K}} \\rbrace = \\lbrace a,a+1,\\dots,a+K-1 \\rbrace.\n\nFind the minimum value of i_K - i_1 among all good index sequences. It can be shown that at least one good index sequence exists under the constraints of this problem.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN K\nP_1 P_2 \\dots P_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the minimum value of i_K - i_1 among all good index sequences.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\leq K \\leq N \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq P_i \\leq N\n- P_i \\neq P_j if i \\neq j.\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n4 2\n2 3 1 4\n\nSample Output 1\n\n1\n\nThe good index sequences are (1,2),(1,3),(2,4). For example, (i_1, i_2) = (1,3) is a good index sequence because 1 \\leq i_1 < i_2 \\leq N and (P_{{i_1}}, P_{{i_2}}) = (2,1) is a rearrangement of two consecutive integers 1, 2.\nAmong these good index sequences, the smallest value of i_K - i_1 is for (1,2), which is 2-1=1.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n4 1\n2 3 1 4\n\nSample Output 2\n\n0\n\ni_K - i_1 = i_1 - i_1 = 0 in all good index sequences.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n10 5\n10 1 6 8 7 2 5 9 3 4\n\nSample Output 3\n\n5",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    \n    if not input_data:\n        return\n\n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    \n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n        K = int(next(iterator))\n        P = [int(next(iterator)) for _ in range(N)]\n    except StopIteration:\n        return\n\n    # If K == 1, the minimum difference is always 0\n    if K == 1:\n        print(0)\n        return\n\n    # We need to find min(i_K - i_1) such that P[i_1...i_K] contains a set of K consecutive integers.\n    # Let the set of values be {a, a+1, ..., a+K-1}.\n    # This is equivalent to finding a window of indices [L, R] such that the number of distinct values \n    # in P[L...R] that belong to some consecutive range of size K is exactly K, and we want to minimize R-L.\n    # Actually, the condition is simpler: The subsequence defined by indices i_1 < ... < i_K \n    # must contain exactly the values {a, ..., a+K-1} for some a.\n    # Note: The indices don't have to be contiguous in the original array, but the VALUES must form a consecutive block.\n    # However, since we want to minimize i_K - i_1, we are looking for the smallest span of indices \n    # that contains a specific set of K consecutive values.\n    \n    # Let's rephrase: For a fixed set of values V = {a, a+1, ..., a+K-1}, let pos[v] be the index (1-based) of value v in P.\n    # The indices involved would be {pos[a], pos[a+1], ..., pos[a+K-1]}.\n    # Let min_idx = min(pos[a], ..., pos[a+K-1]) and max_idx = max(pos[a], ..., pos[a+K-1]).\n    # Then i_1 = min_idx and i_K = max_idx is a valid good index sequence?\n    # Wait, the definition says \"subsequence ... can be obtained by rearranging\".\n    # So if we pick the indices corresponding to the values a, a+1, ..., a+K-1, they form a valid good index sequence.\n    # The cost is max_pos - min_pos.\n    # Is it possible to pick a subset of indices that gives a smaller max-min?\n    # No, because we MUST include all K values. So the set of indices is fixed for a given 'a'.\n    # Thus, for each a from 1 to N-K+1, we calculate max(pos[a...a+K-1]) - min(pos[a...a+K-1]).\n    # We want the minimum over all a.\n    \n    # Constraints: N up to 2*10^5. O(N*K) is too slow.\n    # We need to efficiently compute min and max of pos[a...a+K-1] as a slides from 1 to N-K+1.\n    # This is a sliding window minimum/maximum problem on the array `pos`.\n    \n    # Step 1: Build pos array. pos[v] = index of value v in P (1-based).\n    pos = [0] * (N + 1)\n    for idx, val in enumerate(P):\n        pos[val] = idx + 1  # 1-based index\n        \n    # We need min and max of pos[a] for a in [x, x+K-1] for each x from 1 to N-K+1.\n    # Let A[x] = pos[x]. We want min(A[x...x+K-1]) and max(A[x...x+K-1]).\n    \n    # Sliding window minimum and maximum can be done with monotonic queues (deques) in O(N).\n    \n    def sliding_window_min_max(arr, k):\n        n = len(arr)\n        # arr is 1-indexed conceptually for values 1..N, but let's use 0-indexed list of size N+1, ignoring index 0.\n        # We care about indices 1 to N.\n        \n        min_vals = []\n        max_vals = []\n        \n        dq_min = deque() # Stores indices into arr\n        dq_max = deque()\n        \n        # Process first window [1, K]\n        for i in range(1, K + 1):\n            # Maintain min deque: increasing order\n            while dq_min and arr[dq_min[-1]] >= arr[i]:\n                dq_min.pop()\n            dq_min.append(i)\n            \n            # Maintain max deque: decreasing order\n            while dq_max and arr[dq_max[-1]] <= arr[i]:\n                dq_max.pop()\n            dq_max.append(i)\n            \n        # First window result\n        min_vals.append(arr[dq_min[0]])\n        max_vals.append(arr[dq_max[0]])\n        \n        # Slide window\n        for i in range(K + 1, N + 1):\n            # Remove elements out of window [i-K+1, i]\n            if dq_min[0] == i - K:\n                dq_min.popleft()\n            if dq_max[0] == i - K:\n                dq_max.popleft()\n                \n            # Add new element i\n            while dq_min and arr[dq_min[-1]] >= arr[i]:\n                dq_min.pop()\n            dq_min.append(i)\n            \n            while dq_max and arr[dq_max[-1]] <= arr[i]:\n                dq_max.pop()\n            dq_max.append(i)\n            \n            min_vals.append(arr[dq_min[0]])\n            max_vals.append(arr[dq_max[0]])\n            \n        return min_vals, max_vals\n\n    # A corresponds to pos[1], pos[2], ..., pos[N]\n    # We ignore index 0 of pos since values are 1..N\n    A = pos[1:] # This is pos[1], pos[2], ..., pos[N]\n    \n    mins, maxs = sliding_window_min_max(pos, K)\n    \n    # mins[j] corresponds to window starting at value j+1, ending at j+K\n    # Window for a=1 is values 1..K -> mins[0]\n    # Window for a=N-K+1 is values N-K+1..N -> mins[N-K]\n    \n    ans = float('inf')\n    for j in range(len(mins)):\n        current_min = mins[j]\n        current_max = maxs[j]\n        diff = current_max - current_min\n        if diff < ans:\n            ans = diff\n            \n    print(ans)\n\nsolve()\n```",
