s not changed).\n\nThus, you should print cszapqbr, the final S.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n2 1\naa\n1 1\n\nSample Output 2\n\naa",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    N = int(next(iterator))\n    M = int(next(iterator))\n    S_str = next(iterator)\n    \n    # Read C list\n    C = []\n    for _ in range(N):\n        C.append(int(next(iterator)))\n    \n    # S is 0-indexed in Python, but C is 1-indexed colors.\n    # Let's convert S to a list of characters for mutability.\n    s_list = list(S_str)\n    \n    # We need to process colors 1 to M in order.\n    # For each color i, we find the indices where C[j] == i.\n    # Then we perform a right circular shift on those positions in s_list.\n    \n    # Pre-group indices by color to avoid scanning C every time.\n    # Colors are 1..M.\n    color_indices = [[] for _ in range(M + 1)]\n    for idx, color in enumerate(C):\n        color_indices[color].append(idx)\n        \n    # Process each color from 1 to M\n    for color in range(1, M + 1):\n        indices = color_indices[color]\n        k = len(indices)\n        if k <= 1:\n            continue\n        \n        # Extract the characters at these indices\n        chars = [s_list[i] for i in indices]\n        \n        # Right circular shift by 1:\n        # New char at indices[0] is old char at indices[-1]\n        # New char at indices[1] is old char at indices[0]\n        # ...\n        # New char at indices[j] is old char at indices[j-1]\n        \n        # So, shifted_chars[0] = chars[-1], shifted_chars[1] = chars[0], etc.\n        shifted_chars = [chars[-1]] + chars[:-1]\n        \n        # Put them back\n        for j, idx in enumerate(indices):\n            s_list[idx] = shifted_chars[j]\n            \n    print(\"\".join(s_list))\n\nsolve()\n```",
