    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_34",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed integer array nums, and you are allowed to traverse between its indices. You can traverse between index i and index j, i != j, if and only if gcd(nums[i], nums[j]) > 1, where gcd is the greatest common divisor.\nYour task is to determine if for every pair of indices i and j in nums, where i < j, there exists a sequence of traversals that can take us from i to j.\nReturn true if it is possible to traverse between all such pairs of indices, or false otherwise.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [2,3,6]\nOutput: true\nExplanation: In this example, there are 3 possible pairs of indices: (0, 1), (0, 2), and (1, 2).\nTo go from index 0 to index 1, we can use the sequence of traversals 0 -> 2 -> 1, where we move from index 0 to index 2 because gcd(nums[0], nums[2]) = gcd(2, 6) = 2 > 1, and then move from index 2 to index 1 because gcd(nums[2], nums[1]) = gcd(6, 3) = 3 > 1.\nTo go from index 0 to index 2, we can just go directly because gcd(nums[0], nums[2]) = gcd(2, 6) = 2 > 1. Likewise, to go from index 1 to index 2, we can just go directly because gcd(nums[1], nums[2]) = gcd(3, 6) = 3 > 1.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [3,9,5]\nOutput: false\nExplanation: No sequence of traversals can take us from index 0 to index 2 in this example. So, we return false.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [4,3,12,8]\nOutput: true\nExplanation: There are 6 possible pairs of indices to traverse between: (0, 1), (0, 2), (0, 3), (1, 2), (1, 3), and (2, 3). A valid sequence of traversals exists for each pair, so we return true.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 10^5\n1 <= nums[i] <= 10^5",
    "prediction": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-0528-Qwen3-8B",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\nTo solve this problem, we need to determine if all indices in a given array can be traversed between each other under the condition that two indices can be connected if the greatest common divisor (gcd) of their corresponding values is greater than 1. The solution involves checking if the entire graph formed by these indices is connected.\n\n### Approach\n1. **Initial Checks**: \n   - If the array length is 1, return `True` since a single element trivially satisfies the condition.\n   - If the array contains any 1s and the length is greater than 1, return `False` because 1 cannot be connected to any other number (gcd(1, any number) = 1).\n\n2. **Prime Factorization**:\n   - Precompute the smallest prime factors (SPF) up to the maximum value in the array using the Sieve of Eratosthenes. This helps in efficiently factorizing each number in the array.\n\n3. **Union-Find Data Structure**:\n   - Use the union-find (disjoint set union) data structure to manage connectivity between indices. For each number in the array, factorize it and use its prime factors to union all indices sharing the same prime factor.\n\n4. **Connectivity Check**:\n   - After processing all prime factors, check if all indices belong to the same connected component in the union-find structure. If they do, return `True`; otherwise, return `False`.\n\n### Solution Code\n```python\ndef canTraverseBetweenAllIndices(nums):\n    n = len(nums)\n    if n == 1:\n        return True\n\n    if 1 in nums:\n        return False\n\n    max_val = max(nums)\n    spf = list(range(max_val + 1))\n    if max_val >= 2:\n        for i in range(2, int(max_val**0.5) + 1):\n            if spf[i] == i:\n                for j in range(i * i, max_val + 1, i):\n                    if spf[j] == j:\n                        spf[j] = i\n\n    factors_map = {}\n    for i in range(n):\n        x = nums[i]\n        factors = set()\n        temp = x\n        while temp > 1:\n            p = spf[temp]\n            factors.add(p)\n            while temp % p == 0:\n                temp //= p\n        for p in factors:\n            if p not in factors_map:\n                factors_map[p] = []\n            factors_map[p].append(i)\n\n    parent = list(range(n))\n    rank = [0] * n\n\n    def find(x):\n        if parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = find(parent[x])\n        return parent[x]\n\n    def union(x, y):\n        rx = find(x)\n        ry = find(y)\n        if rx == ry:\n            return\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\n            parent[rx] = ry\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n        else:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n            rank[rx] += 1\n\n    for p, indices in factors_map.items():\n        if len(indices) < 2:\n            continue\n        base = indices[0]\n        for idx in indices[1:]:\n            union(base, idx)\n\n    roots = set()\n    for i in range(n):\n        roots.add(find(i))\n    return len(roots) == 1\n```\n\n### Explanation\n1. **Initial Checks**: The function first checks if the array length is 1 (trivially connected) or if there are any 1s (which cannot connect to any other indices if the array length is greater than 1).\n\n2. **Prime Factorization**: The smallest prime factors (SPF) for numbers up to the maximum value in the array are precomputed using the Sieve of Eratosthenes. This allows efficient factorization of each number in the array.\n\n3. **Union-Find Setup**: The union-find data structure is initialized to keep track of connected components. Each number in the array is factorized, and indices sharing common prime factors are unioned.\n\n4. **Connectivity Check**: After processing all prime factors, the function checks if all indices belong to a single connected component. If they do, the graph is connected, and the function returns `True`; otherwise, it returns `False`.\n\nThis approach efficiently checks the connectivity of the graph formed by the indices using prime factorization and union-find, ensuring optimal performance even for large input sizes.",
