i as the unique maximum?\n    # But there can be duplicate maximums.\n    \n    # Standard trick for \"count subarrays where max is X\":\n    # Use Next Greater Element to define the range where each element is the maximum.\n    # For duplicates, we need to be careful not to double count.\n    # Usually, we define the range for element i as (prev_greater_or_equal[i] + 1, next_greater[i]) or similar.\n    # Let's define:\n    # left_limit[i]: the index of the nearest element to the left that is >= nums[i]. If none, -1.\n    # right_limit[i]: the index of the nearest element to the right that is > nums[i]. If none, n.\n    # Then nums[i] is the unique maximum in nums[left_limit[i]+1 ... right_limit[i]-1]? \n    # No, if there are duplicates, we need a consistent tie-breaking rule.\n    # Common approach: \n    # Prev Greater or Equal (PGE): previous index j < i such that nums[j] >= nums[i].\n    # Next Greater (NGE): next index k > i such that nums[k] > nums[i].\n    # Then the range where i is considered the \"representative\" maximum is (PGE_index + 1, NGE_index - 1).\n    # In this range, nums[i] is the maximum, and if there are other elements equal to nums[i], they are handled by their own representative ranges or excluded.\n    # Specifically, with PGE (>=) and NGE (>), each subarray's maximum is counted exactly once, attributed to the leftmost occurrence of the maximum value? Or rightmost?\n    # Let's verify:\n    # Subarray [3, 3, 3].\n    # Index 0: PGE=-1, NGE=3 (none). Range (0, 2). Max is 3.\n    # Index 1: PGE=0 (nums[0]>=nums[1]), NGE=3. Range (1, 2).\n    # Index 2: PGE=1, NGE=3. Range (2, 2).\n    \n    # Actually, the standard \"contribution technique\" counts for each i, the number of subarrays where nums[i] is the maximum.\n    # With the definition:\n    # L[i] = index of previous element >= nums[i]. (If none, -1)\n    # R[i] = index of next element > nums[i]. (If none, n)\n    # Then the number of subarrays where nums[i] is the maximum (and it's t