or all i: Previous Greater or Equal element index.\n    # 2. Compute R[i] for all i: Next Greater element index.\n    # 3. For each i, we need to quickly query how many indices j in (L[i], R[i]) have nums[j] == nums[i] and j <= i, and similarly j >= i.\n    #    Actually, since we iterate i, we can just pre-process the positions of each value.\n    \n    # Optimization:\n    # Group indices by value.\n    # For each value v, let pos[v] be a sorted list of indices where nums[index] == v.\n    # For each i, let v = nums[i].\n    # We need to count p in pos[v] such that L[i] < p <= i.\n    # And q in pos[v] such that i <= q < R[i].\n    # Since pos[v] is sorted, we can use binary search (bisect).\n    \n    # Steps:\n    # 1. Compute L array using a monotonic stack (decreasing stack, but handling >= for L).\n    #    To get previous >=, we maintain a stack of indices with decreasing values?\n    #    When processing i, pop elements from stack that are < nums[i]. The top is the previous >=.\n    #    Wait, if we want previous >=, we keep elements in stack that are >= current?\n    #    Standard PGE (Previous Greater or Equal):\n    #    Stack stores indices. For new element x, pop while stack top value < x. The remaining top is >= x.\n    #    If stack empty, L[i] = -1.\n    # 2. Compute R array using monotonic stack (Next Greater).\n    #    Standard NGE (Next Greater Element):\n    #    Stack stores indices. Traverse from right to left or left to right.\n    #    For new element x, pop while stack top value <= x. The remaining top is > x.\n    #    If stack empty, R[i] = n.\n    # 3. Build a dictionary mapping value to sorted list of indices.\n    # 4. For each i, use bisect to find the count of indices in pos[nums[i]] within (L[i], i] and [i, R[i]).\n    \n    # Time Complexity: O(N) for stacks, O(N log N) for binary searches (or O(N) if we optimize, but log N is fine for 10^5).\n    \n    # Let's implement.\n    \n    # Step 1: Compute L\n    L = [-1] * n\n    stack = []  # stores indices\n    for i in range(n):\n        while stack and nums[stack[-1]] < nums[i]:\n            stack.pop()\n        if stack:\n            L[i] = stack[-1]\n        else:\n            L[i] = -1\n        stack.append(i)\n        \n    # Step 2: Compute R\n    R = [n] * n\n    stack = []\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        while stack and nums[stack[-1]] <= nums[i]:\n            stack.pop()\n        if stack:\n            R[i] = stack[-1]\n        else:\n            R[i] = n\n        stack.append(i)\n        \n    # Step 3: Group indices by value\n    from collections import defaultdict\n    import bisect\n    \n    pos = defaultdict(list)\n    for i in range(n):\n        pos[nums[i]].append(i)\n        \n    total_count = 0\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        v = nums[i]\n        p_list = pos[v]\n        \n        # Count indices in p_list such that L[i] < idx <= i\n        # We want the number of elements in p_list that are in range (L[i], i]\n        # bisect_right gives insertion point after all elements <= i\n        # bisect_left gives insertion point before all elements > L[i]\n        \n        # Number of elements <= i\n        right_idx = bisect.bisect_right(p_list, i)\n        # Number of elements <= L[i]\n        left_idx = bisect.bisect_right(p_list, L[i])\n        \n        count_left = right_idx - left_idx\n        \n        # Count indices in p_list such that i <= idx < R[i]\n        # Number of elements < R[i]\n        right_bound_idx = bisect.bisect_left(p_list, R[i])\n        # Number of elements < i\n        left_bound_idx = bisect.bisect_left(p_list, i)\n        \n        count_right = right_bound_idx - left_bound_idx\n        \n        total_count += count_left * count_right\n        \n    return total_count\n```",
