    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nimport bisect\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input from stdin\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    iterator = iter(input_data)\n    \n    try:\n        N = int(next(iterator))\n        Q = int(next(iterator))\n        \n        R = []\n        for _ in range(N):\n            R.append(int(next(iterator)))\n        \n        # Sort R to easily pick the sleighs requiring the fewest reindeer first\n        R.sort()\n        \n        # Precompute prefix sums of sorted R\n        # prefix_sum[k] = sum of the k smallest R_i values\n        # prefix_sum[0] = 0\n        prefix_sum = [0] * (N + 1)\n        for i in range(N):\n            prefix_sum[i+1] = prefix_sum[i] + R[i]\n            \n        # For each query X, find the maximum k such that prefix_sum[k] <= X\n        # Since prefix_sum is non-decreasing, we can use binary search\n        \n        results = []\n        for _ in range(Q):\n            X = int(next(iterator))\n            # We want the largest index k in [0, N] such that prefix_sum[k] <= X\n            # bisect_right returns the insertion point after any existing entries of X\n            # So bisect_right(prefix_sum, X) gives us the index where X would be inserted to keep order\n            # All elements to the left are <= X. But wait, bisect_right on prefix_sum:\n            # prefix_sum is [p0, p1, ..., pN]\n            # If we do bisect_right(prefix_sum, X), it returns i such that all prefix_sum[:i] <= X and prefix_sum[i:] > X\n            # Actually, bisect_right returns the first index where elements to the right are > X? No.\n            # bisect_right(a, x) returns an insertion point i such that all e in a[:i] have e <= x and all e in a[i:] have e > x.\n            # Wait, no. bisect_right ensures that if you insert x at i, the list remains sorted.\n            # So a[i-1] <= x and a[i] > x (if i < len).\n            # So the number of sleighs we can pull is i-1 if a[i] > x? Let's check.\n            # If prefix_sum[k] <= X, then we can pull k sleighs.\n            # We want max k such that prefix_sum[k] <= X.\n            # bisect_right(prefix_sum, X) returns k+1 if prefix_sum[k] == X and prefix_sum[k+1] > X?\n            # Let's test: prefix_sum = [0, 1, 3, 6], X=2. bisect_right([0,1,3,6], 2) -> 2. \n            # prefix_sum[1]=1 <= 2, prefix_sum[2]=3 > 2. So max k is 1. Correct.\n            # X=3. bisect_right([0,1,3,6], 3) -> 3. prefix_sum[2]=3 <= 3. Max k is 2. Correct.\n            # X=0. bisect_right([0,1,3,6], 0) -> 1. prefix_sum[0]=0 <= 0. Max k is 0. Correct.\n            # X=7. bisect_right([0,1,3,6], 7) -> 4. prefix_sum[3]=6 <= 7. Max k is 3. Correct.\n            \n            idx = bisect.bisect_right(prefix_sum, X)\n            # The result is idx - 1 because prefix_sum[idx-1] <= X and prefix_sum[idx] > X (or idx=N+1 which is out of bounds but handled)\n            # Actually, prefix_sum has length N+1. Indices 0..N.\n            # bisect_right returns value in [0, N+1].\n            # If it returns 0, that means X < prefix_sum[0]=0, which is impossible since X>=1 and R_i>=1 so prefix_sum[1]>=1.\n            # So idx >= 1 always.\n            # The answer is idx - 1.\n            results.append(str(idx - 1))\n        \n        print('\\n'.join(results))\n        \n    except StopIteration:\n        pass\n\nsolve()\n```",
