    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_142",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed array of positive integers nums and a positive integer limit.\nIn one operation, you can choose any two indices i and j and swap nums[i] and nums[j] if |nums[i] - nums[j]| <= limit.\nReturn the lexicographically smallest array that can be obtained by performing the operation any number of times.\nAn array a is lexicographically smaller than an array b if in the first position where a and b differ, array a has an element that is less than the corresponding element in b. For example, the array [2,10,3] is lexicographically smaller than the array [10,2,3] because they differ at index 0 and 2 < 10.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,5,3,9,8], limit = 2\nOutput: [1,3,5,8,9]\nExplanation: Apply the operation 2 times:\n- Swap nums[1] with nums[2]. The array becomes [1,3,5,9,8]\n- Swap nums[3] with nums[4]. The array becomes [1,3,5,8,9]\nWe cannot obtain a lexicographically smaller array by applying any more operations.\nNote that it may be possible to get the same result by doing different operations.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [1,7,6,18,2,1], limit = 3\nOutput: [1,6,7,18,1,2]\nExplanation: Apply the operation 3 times:\n- Swap nums[1] with nums[2]. The array becomes [1,6,7,18,2,1]\n- Swap nums[0] with nums[4]. The array becomes [2,6,7,18,1,1]\n- Swap nums[0] with nums[5]. The array becomes [1,6,7,18,1,2]\nWe cannot obtain a lexicographically smaller array by applying any more operations.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [1,7,28,19,10], limit = 3\nOutput: [1,7,28,19,10]\nExplanation: [1,7,28,19,10] is the lexicographically smallest array we can obtain because we cannot apply the operation on any two indices.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 10^5\n1 <= nums[i] <= 10^9\n1 <= limit <= 10^9",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef smallestArray(nums: list[int], limit: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the lexicographically smallest array obtainable by swapping elements\n    if their absolute difference is <= limit.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    # We need to group indices that can reach each other via swaps.\n    # Two numbers can be swapped if their difference <= limit.\n    # This forms connected components based on values.\n    # However, direct union-find on values might be tricky due to large value range.\n    # Instead, sort the array along with original indices.\n    \n    # Create pairs of (value, original_index)\n    indexed_nums = [(num, i) for i, num in enumerate(nums)]\n    indexed_nums.sort()\n    \n    # Now, iterate through the sorted values.\n    # Elements that are within 'limit' of each other in the sorted sequence\n    # can potentially be connected. But connectivity is transitive.\n    # So, we can use a Union-Find structure on the indices? Or rather,\n    # identify groups of indices whose values form a connected component\n    # in the \"swap graph\".\n    \n    # Actually, a simpler view:\n    # Consider the sorted version of nums. Let's call it sorted_nums.\n    # We want to assign the smallest available numbers to the earliest possible positions,\n    # but only among positions that are in the same connected component.\n    \n    # How to find connected components?\n    # Two indices i and j are in the same component if there is a path of swaps.\n    # A swap is allowed between x and y if |x-y| <= limit.\n    # In the sorted array of values, if we have a sequence of values v1, v2, ..., vk\n    # such that vi+1 - vi <= limit for all i, then all these values are in the same component?\n    # Not exactly. It's about the original indices.\n    \n    # Correct approach:\n    # 1. Sort the values while keeping track of original indices.\n    # 2. Iterate through the sorted values. Group consecutive values that are within 'limit'.\n    #    These groups form connected components of values.\n    # 3. For each component, the set of original indices involved can have their values permuted arbitrarily.\n    #    To get the lexicographically smallest result, we should assign the smallest values in the component\n    #    to the smallest original indices in the component.\n    \n    # Step 1: Sort\n    indexed_nums.sort()\n    \n    # Step 2: Identify components\n    # We'll traverse the sorted list and group indices.\n    # A new component starts when the current value minus the previous value > limit.\n    \n    components = []  # List of lists of original indices\n    if n == 0:\n        return []\n        \n    current_component = [indexed_nums[0][1]]\n    current_values = [indexed_nums[0][0]]\n    \n    for i in range(1, n):\n        val, idx = indexed_nums[i]\n        prev_val = indexed_nums[i-1][0]\n        if val - prev_val <= limit:\n            current_component.append(idx)\n            current_values.append(val)\n        else:\n            # Finalize current component\n            components.append((current_component, current_values))\n            current_component = [idx]\n            current_values = [val]\n            \n    # Don't forget the last component\n    components.append((current_component, current_values))\n    \n    # Step 3: For each component, sort the indices and assign sorted values to them\n    result = [0] * n\n    \n    for comp_indices, comp_values in components:\n        # Sort the original indices to know which position gets which value\n        comp_indices_sorted = sorted(comp_indices)\n        # The values are already sorted because indexed_nums was sorted\n        comp_values_sorted = sorted(comp_values)\n        \n        for pos, val in zip(comp_indices_sorted, comp_values_sorted):\n            result[pos] = val\n            \n    return result\n```",
