the target set might distinguish between different non-zero residues.\n    # But the transition is symmetric for all non-zero residues relative to 0?\n    # Let's check:\n    # A_0(0) = 1, A_0(r) = 0 for r != 0.\n    # A_1(0) = T_0 - A_0(0) = 1 - 1 = 0.\n    # A_1(r) for r != 0: A_1(r) = T_0 - A_0(r) = 1 - 0 = 1.\n    # So after 1 step, all r != 0 have count 1, r=0 has count 0.\n    \n    # A_2(0) = T_1 - A_1(0) = (n-1) - 0 = n-1.\n    # A_2(r) for r != 0: A_2(r) = T_1 - A_1(r) = (n-1) - 1 = n-2.\n    \n    # Generally, let a_k = A_k(0) and b_k = A_k(r) for r != 0.\n    # Note: all r != 0 are symmetric.\n    # a_k = T_{k-1} - a_{k-1}\n    # b_k = T_{k-1} - b_{k-1}\n    # Where T_{k-1} = (n-1)^{k-1}.\n    \n    # We want sum_{r in target_set} A_k(r).\n    # target_set contains 0? If 0 is in matches, i.e., s == t.\n    # Let c0 = 1 if 0 in target_set else 0.\n    # Let c_nonzero = number of elements in target_set that are != 0.\n    # Result = c0 * a_k + c_nonzero * b_k.\n    \n    # Compute a_k and b_k using recurrence.\n    # a_k = (n-1)^{k-1} - a_{k-1}\n    # b_k = (n-1)^{k-1} - b_{k-1}\n    \n    # Base case: k=0\n    # a_0 = 1, b_0 = 0.\n    \n    # We can solve this linear recurrence.\n    # a_k + a_{k-1} = (n-1)^{k-1}\n    # b_k + b_{k-1} = (n-1)^{k-1}\n    \n    # For a_k:\n    # a_k = (-1)^k a_0 + sum_{j=1}^{k} (-1)^{k-j} (n-1)^{j-1}\n    # a_0 = 1.\n    # Sum part: let S = sum_{j=1}^{k} (-1)^{k-j} (n-1)^{j-1}\n    # = (-1)^k sum_{j=1}^{k} (-1)^{-j} (n-1)^{j-1}\n    # = (-1)^k sum_{j=1}^{k} (-1)^{j} (n-1)^{j-1} * (-1)^{-2j}? No.\n    # (-1)^{-j} = (-1)^j.\n    # S = (-1)^k sum_{j=1}^{k} (-1)^j (n-1)^{j-1}\n    \n    # Let's just iterate if k is small? k up to 10^15, so we need closed form or matrix exp.\n    # Matrix form:\n    # [a_k]   [ -1   (n-1)^{k-1} ] ... no, the RHS varies.\n    \n    # Alternative: Solve the homogeneous + particular.\n    # x_k + x_{k-1} = C^{k-1} where C = n-1.\n    # Homogeneous: x_k = -x_{k-1} => x_k = A (-1)^k.\n    # Particular: