    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a binary array nums.\nWe call a subarray alternating if no two adjacent elements in the subarray have the same value.\nReturn the number of alternating subarrays in nums.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [0,1,1,1]\nOutput: 5\nExplanation:\nThe following subarrays are alternating: [0], [1], [1], [1], and [0,1].\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [1,0,1,0]\nOutput: 10\nExplanation:\nEvery subarray of the array is alternating. There are 10 possible subarrays that we can choose.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 10^5\nnums[i] is either 0 or 1.",
    "prediction": "MiniMax-M3",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to count subarrays where no two adjacent elements are equal.\n\n**Key Insight:** An alternating subarray of length `L` contributes exactly `L*(L+1)/2` subarrays (all its prefixes are also alternating). So I just need to find the lengths of maximal alternating segments and sum their triangular numbers.\n\n**Algorithm:**\n1. Scan through the array, tracking the current run length of alternating elements\n2. When two adjacent elements match, the current run ends; add its triangular number to the result\n3. Handle the final run after the loop\n4. Add single elements (each element alone is a length-1 alternating subarray, contributing +1 per element, which is already accounted for by counting runs of length 1)\n\nLet me verify with the examples:\n- `[0,1,1,1]`: Runs are `[0,1]` (length 2) and `[1], [1], [1]` (length 1 each). Sum: 3 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 5 ✓\n- `[1,0,1,0]`: Single run of length 4. Sum: 4·5/2 = 10 ✓\n\n```python\ndef countAlternatingSubarrays(nums):\n    result = 0\n    cur_len = 1\n    for i in range(1, len(nums)):\n        if nums[i] != nums[i-1]:\n            cur_len += 1\n        else:\n            result += cur_len * (cur_len + 1) // 2\n            cur_len = 1\n    result += cur_len * (cur_len + 1) // 2\n    return result\n```\n\nThe function runs in **O(n)** time and **O(1)** space, which is optimal for the constraints (`n ≤ 10^5`).",
