move == 'U':\n            curr_r -= 1\n        elif move == 'D':\n            curr_r += 1\n        dr[i+1] = curr_r\n        dc[i+1] = curr_c\n        \n    # Now, for a starting cell (r, c) to be valid:\n    # For all k in 0..N, S[r + dr[k]][c + dc[k]] must be '.'\n    \n    # We can iterate over all possible starting cells (r, c) that are '.'.\n    # But checking all N+1 points for each cell is slow.\n    \n    # Alternative:\n    # Mark invalid starting cells.\n    # A starting cell (r, c) is invalid if there exists some k such that S[r + dr[k]][c + dc[k]] is '#'.\n    # This is equivalent to: (r, c) is invalid if it lies in the set of cells that would hit a '#' when shifted by (-dr[k], -dc[k]).\n    \n    # Let's create a boolean grid `possible_start` initialized to True for all land cells, False for sea.\n    # Actually, starts must be on land. So initially `possible_start[r][c]` is True if S[r][c]=='.' else False.\n    # Then for each k from 0 to N:\n    #   For each cell (rr, cc) such that S[rr][cc] == '#':\n    #     The starting position (r, c) = (rr - dr[k], cc - dc[k]) would land on (rr, cc) at step k.\n    #     So if (r, c) is within bounds and is a potential start, mark it as invalid.\n    \n    # This seems efficient.\n    # Complexity: Sum over k of (number of '#' cells). In worst case, half grid is '#', so 500 * 250,000 = 125M ops. Still high.\n    # But note: k=0 is trivial (start must be '.'). We can handle k=0 separately or just include it.\n    # If S[rr][cc] is '#', then for k=0, dr[0]=0, dc[0]=0. So (rr, cc) itself is marked invalid. Correct.\n    \n    # Let's refine the complexity.\n    # We have H*W grid.\n    # For each k, we iterate over all '#' cells.\n    # Total iterations = N * (number of '#' cells).\n    # Max N=500, Max '#' cells ~ 250,000. Product 125,000,000.\n    # In Python, this might TLE.\n    \n    # Is there a faster way?\n    # We can use a 2D array `valid_start` of size H x W, init to 1 (or True).\n    # For each k, and fo