      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minimumOperations(num: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns the minimum number of operations required to make the number represented by `num` special.\n    A number is special if it is divisible by 25.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(num)\n    \n    # A number is divisible by 25 if its last two digits are 00, 25, 50, or 75.\n    # We want to find the rightmost occurrence of such a pair in the original string,\n    # keeping their relative order. The number of deletions will be the digits after the second digit of the pair,\n    # plus any digits between the first and second digit of the pair that are skipped.\n    \n    # Strategy: Find the last two digits that form one of [\"00\", \"25\", \"50\", \"75\"].\n    # Iterate from right to left to find the second digit of the pair, then look for the first digit to its left.\n    \n    def solve():\n        # Try to find a suffix that ends with 00, 25, 50, or 75\n        # We need to find indices i < j such that num[i] + num[j] is in {\"00\", \"25\", \"50\", \"75\"}\n        # and we minimize (n - 1 - j) + (j - 1 - i) = n - 2 - i ? No.\n        # Let's think about deletions.\n        # If we keep num[i] and num[j] as the last two digits (where i < j),\n        # we must delete all digits after j (count: n - 1 - j).\n        # We must delete all digits between i and j (count: j - i - 1).\n        # Total deletions = (n - 1 - j) + (j - i - 1) = n - 2 - i.\n        # Wait, this assumes we keep everything before i. That's fine because we just want *some* prefix ending with the pair.\n        # Actually, we don't necessarily keep everything before i. But since deleting digits before i doesn't help with divisibility by 25 (which depends on last two digits), and we want to minimize deletions, we should keep as many digits as possible. So we keep the prefix up to i, then the pair at i and j.\n        # So cost = (digits after j) + (digits between i and j) = n - j - 1 + j - i - 1 = n - i - 2.\n        # To minimize n - i - 2, we need to maximize i.\n        \n        # So we want to find the largest index i such that there exists j > i with num[i]+num[j] in valid pairs,\n        # and then the cost is n - i - 2.\n        # However, we also have the case where the resulting number is \"0\" or \"00\", etc.\n        # If we end up with a single digit '0', that is divisible by 25. Cost = n - 1.\n        # If we end up with empty string, it becomes 0. Cost = n.\n        \n        # Let's check for pairs first.\n        best_i = -1\n        \n        # Valid endings\n        targets = [\"00\", \"25\", \"50\", \"75\"]\n        \n        # For each target, find the rightmost occurrence\n        # We can iterate backwards\n        for t in targets:\n            d1, d2 = t[0], t[1]\n            # Find rightmost d2, then leftmost d1 to its right? No, we want largest i.\n            # Let's find all pairs (i, j) with i < j such that num[i]==d1 and num[j]==d2.\n            # We want to maximize i.\n            \n            # Find positions of d2\n            pos_d2 = [k for k in range(n) if num[k] == d2]\n            # Find positions of d1\n            pos_d1 = [k for k in range(n) if num[k] == d1]\n            \n            for p2 in reversed(pos_d2):\n                # Look for d1 before p2\n                for p1 in reversed(pos_d1):\n                    if p1 < p2:\n                        # Candidate found\n                        if p1 > best_i:\n                            best_i = p1\n                        break # Found the largest p1 for this p2, move to next p2\n                \n        if best_i != -1:\n            return n - best_i - 2\n        \n        # If no pair found, check if we can make \"0\"\n        if '0' in num:\n            return n - 1\n        \n        # If nothing, delete all digits to get 0\n        return n\n\n    return solve()\n```",
