    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef numberOfGoodPartitions(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # For each number, find its first and last occurrence\n    first_occurrence = {}\n    last_occurrence = {}\n    \n    for i, num in enumerate(nums):\n        if num not in first_occurrence:\n            first_occurrence[num] = i\n        last_occurrence[num] = i\n    \n    # Merge intervals: each number defines an interval [first_occurrence, last_occurrence]\n    # A cut can be made at index i (between i and i+1) if no interval spans across i and i+1.\n    # More precisely, we need to find all \"valid\" split points.\n    # Let's compute the rightmost boundary that must be included for each starting segment.\n    \n    # We can iterate through the array and maintain the maximum end index of all numbers seen so far.\n    # If at index i, the max end index of all numbers in nums[0..i] is exactly i, then we can make a cut after i.\n    \n    cuts = 0\n    max_end = -1\n    \n    for i in range(len(nums)):\n        num = nums[i]\n        if last_occurrence[num] > max_end:\n            max_end = last_occurrence[num]\n        \n        # If the current position i is the end of all intervals started so far\n        if max_end == i:\n            cuts += 1\n    \n    # The number of good partitions is 2^(cuts - 1) because each cut point can either be a split or not,\n    # except the last segment which doesn't have a choice.\n    # Actually, if there are 'k' valid cut positions (including the implicit end of array),\n    # we have k segments. Between k segments, there are k-1 gaps where we can choose to split or not.\n    # So the answer is 2^(k-1).\n    \n    if cuts == 0:\n        return 0\n    \n    return pow(2, cuts - 1, MOD)\n```",
