    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a binary array nums.\nA subarray of an array is good if it contains exactly one element with the value 1.\nReturn an integer denoting the number of ways to split the array nums into good subarrays. As the number may be too large, return it modulo 10^9 + 7.\nA subarray is a contiguous non-empty sequence of elements within an array.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [0,1,0,0,1]\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: There are 3 ways to split nums into good subarrays:\n- [0,1] [0,0,1]\n- [0,1,0] [0,1]\n- [0,1,0,0] [1]\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [0,1,0]\nOutput: 1\nExplanation: There is 1 way to split nums into good subarrays:\n- [0,1,0]\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 10^5\n0 <= nums[i] <= 1",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef numWays(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # Find all indices where nums[i] == 1\n    ones_indices = [i for i, x in enumerate(nums) if x == 1]\n    \n    n_ones = len(ones_indices)\n    \n    # If there are no 1s, we can't form any good subarray (each must contain exactly one 1)\n    if n_ones == 0:\n        return 0\n    \n    # If there's only one 1, there's exactly one way to split: the whole array is one good subarray\n    if n_ones == 1:\n        return 1\n    \n    # For multiple 1s, the splits between consecutive 1s determine the number of ways.\n    # Between two consecutive 1s at indices ones_indices[i] and ones_indices[i+1],\n    # there are (ones_indices[i+1] - ones_indices[i]) positions where we can cut.\n    # The number of ways is the product of these gaps.\n    \n    result = 1\n    for i in range(n_ones - 1):\n        gap = ones_indices[i+1] - ones_indices[i]\n        result = (result * gap) % MOD\n    \n    return result\n```",
