rect type for non I-N, but correct precision for Fixed).\nc) NUMBER FIXED(5) DEC COMPLEX. (Correct type for I-N, correct precision).\nd) TOTAL FLOAT (21). (Correct type for non I-N. Precision 21 bits? 6 digits is ~17 bits. 21 bits allows ~6-7 digits. This is a plausible bit-length for single precision float in some contexts).\ne) INCANT FIXED(15) BIN. (Correct type for I-N? No, should be DEC. But maybe BIN is a typo in my reading or the option?). Wait, Option A says `INCANT FIXED(15) BIN, REAL`. This mixes Fixed with Binary? That doesn't make sense.\n\nLet's look at Option G:\na) PERCENT(1:7) DEC, REAL, FLOAT(4).\nb) AMOUNT FIXED (8).\nc) NUMBER FIXED(7).\nd) TOTAL FLOAT (19).\ne) INCANT FIXED(12).\n\nLet's look at Option H:\na) PERCENT(1:5) DEC, REAL, FLOAT(6).\nb) AMOUNT FIXED (7).\nc) NUMBER FIXED(3).\nd) TOTAL FLOAT (18).\ne) INCANT FIXED(10).\n\nThere is a very similar question found in computer science quizzes regarding PL/I defaults.\nThe defaults are:\n- Variables starting with I, J, K, L, M, N are **Fixed Decimal** with precision **5**.\n- Other variables are **Float Binary** with precision **6** (digits) or roughly **17** bits.\n\nIf we strictly follow this:\na) PERCENT (P): Float Binary, Prec 6.\nb) AMOUNT (A): Float Binary, Prec 6.\nc) NUMBER (N): Fixed Decimal, Prec 5. Keyword COMPLEX -> Fixed Decimal Complex, Prec 5.\nd) TOTAL (T): Float Binary, Prec 6.\ne) INCANT (I): Fixed Decimal, Prec 5.\n\nNow look at the options again.\nOption A:\na) PERCENT ... FLOAT(6). Matches.\nb) AMOUNT FIXED (5). Mismatch (Should be Float).\nc) NUMBER FIXED(5) DEC COMPLEX. Matches.\nd) TOTAL FLOAT (21). Mismatch (Should be 6 digits/~17 bits).\ne) INCANT FIXED(15) BIN. Mismatch (Should be Fixed DEC 5).\n\nIt seems none of the options strictly follow standard IBM PL/I defaults for all variables if we interpret \"AMOUNT\" as needing to be Float. However, sometimes \"Default Rules\" questions refer to specific implicit declarations in a code snippet not shown, or rely on a specific textbook convention.\n\nLet's reconsider the variable names.\nMaybe the question implies that `AMOUNT`, `TOTAL`, `PERCENT` are *not* I-N, so they are Float.\nAnd `NUMBER`, `INCANT` *are* I-N, so they are Fixed.\n\nIf Option A is the intended answer, it might be relying on a specific mapping:\n- PERCENT: Float(6)\n- AMOUNT: Fixed(5) -- Why? Maybe the user *did* declare it Fixed elsewhere? No, \"if full explicit declarations are not provided\".\n- Is it possible the question considers `AMOUNT` to default to Fixed? No, only I-N do.\n\nLet's look at Option **A** again. It is the only one with `FLOAT(6)` for PERCENT and `FIXED(5)` for NUMBER. These are the canonical default precisions (6 digits for float, 5 for fixed).\nEven though it labels AMOUNT as Fixed and INCANT as BIN, Option A contains the most \"standard\" numbers (5 and 6) associated with PL/I defaults.\n- Default Fixed Precision: 5\n- Default Float Precision: 6\n\nOption A has:\nPERCENT FLOAT(6)\nAMOUNT FIXED(5)\nNUMBER FIXED(5)\nTOTAL FLOAT(21) -- 21 bits is close to 6 digits (log2(10^6) ≈ 20).\nINCANT FIXED(15) -- 15 bits?\n\nOption H has:\nPERCENT FLOAT(6)\nAMOUNT FIXED(7)\nNUMBER FIXED(3)\nTOTAL FLOAT(18)\nINCANT FIXED(10)\n\nOption A is the strongest candidate because it pairs `FLOAT(6)` and `FIXED(5)`, which are the hallmark default precisions in PL/I education. The discrepancies in AMOUNT and INCANT descriptions might be due to errors in the question's distractors or a specific interpretation of \"attributes\" where AMOUNT is assumed Fixed in a different context, or simply that A is the \"best fit\" among imperfect choices. Specifically, `NUMBER` being `FIXED(5)` is a strong indicator for Option A, as N starts the I-N block. `PERCENT` being `FLOAT(6)` is also a strong indicator.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
