    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The random variable $N$ represents the number of trials until the first success in a sequence of Bernoulli trials where \"success\" is defined as observing the value $X_0$ again. Since the $X_i$ are i.i.d., the probability of any specific outcome $x$ occurring is $p(x)$. However, the problem states $x \\in \\{1, \\dots, 100\\}$, implying a discrete uniform distribution over 100 values unless specified otherwise. Under a uniform distribution, $p(x) = 1/100$. The waiting time $N$ follows a geometric distribution with parameter $p = 1/100$. The expected value of a geometric distribution counting the trial on which the first success occurs is $1/p$. Thus, $E(N) = 1 / (1/100) = 100$.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
