      "generated_answer": "The cache is 2KB with 8-byte blocks, meaning there are 256 sets (2K/8 = 256 blocks, direct-mapped).\n\nArray A spans physical addresses 4096 to 4096+256*4-1 = 4096 to 5119. With 8-byte blocks, A occupies blocks 512-639 (256 addresses starting at 4096, so blocks 4096/8=512 to 5119/8=639, but since it starts at 4096 = 8*512, it's aligned to block boundaries).\n\nArray A is 1024 bytes (256*4), occupying 128 blocks (1024/8). Array B starts at 8192, spanning to 8192+2048-1 = 10239, 1024 bytes, 128 blocks.\n\nA[i] addresses: 4096 + 4i, for i=0 to 255. Block of A[i] = (4096+4i)/8 = 512 + i/2 (integer division). Since i is even, even i gives block 512+i/2; odd i gives same block (4 bytes apart).\n\nB[2i] addresses: 8192 + 8i. Block = (8192+8i)/8 = 1024 + i.\n\nThe cache has 256 sets. Both arrays fit in cache (128+128=256 blocks mapping to 256 sets).\n\nA blocks map to sets 512 to 639 (mod 256) = 0 to 127.\nB blocks map to sets 1024 to 1151 (mod 256) = 0 to 127.\n\nSo A and B blocks can conflict! But total unique blocks = 256, exactly fitting the 256 sets.\n\nAt iteration i, A[i] needs block A_i and B[2i] needs block B_i, write to A_i. If both A_i and B_i map to same set, they conflict. Since |A_i - B_i| = i/2 + 512, and mod 256, the set of A block = (512 + i/2) mod 256 = i/2 mod 256. Set of B block = (1024 + i) mod 256 = i mod 256.\n\nSince i/2 and i are different (for odd i), they map to different sets only sometimes. For even i=2k: A set = k mod 256, B set = 2k mod 256. These are different unless k=0 or 128. For i=128, both map to set 0.\n\nActually the key issue: with write-back, a block is written to memory only when evicted. If cache is large enough to hold all accessed blocks, no write-backs occur to main memory during the loop (only writes to cache).\n\nA and B together need 256 unique blocks = exactly the cache size. All blocks fit, so no evictions, no write-backs. Answer = 0 bytes.\n\nWait, but we need to track if blocks are accessed multiple times. A is written in every iteration. Total A blocks = 128, written once each = 128 writes to cache (each bringing in 8 bytes, writing 4 bytes of A, then writing back A value).\n\nActually, each A[i] is written. A block contains A[2k] and A[2k+1]. Block A_k is written when i=2k (writing 4 bytes) and again when i=2k+1 (writing other 4 bytes). So 128 blocks × 1 writeback potential each.\n\nSince no evictions occur (cache holds everything), 0 bytes written to memory.\n\nThe answer is J. 0.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
