      "generated_answer": "Looking at this question, I need to identify which option is NOT part of the root set in a typical garbage collector.\n\nThe root set typically includes things that are directly accessible to the program: local variables on the call stack, return addresses, temporary variables, actual parameters, global/static variables, and values in CPU registers. Dynamically allocated objects on the heap are NOT part of the root set because they can only be reached through references from roots (stack variables, globals, etc.), and they themselves become candidates for garbage collection if they cannot be reached from the root set.\n\nWhile \"objects referenced by CPU instructions\" might seem unusual, the other options (stack contents, registers, global/static variables) are all classic root set members. Dynamically allocated heap objects are precisely what the garbage collector is trying to determine reachability for, making them not part of the root set.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
