      "generated_answer": "To determine the correct answer, we analyze each statement based on the pipeline parameters.\n\n**Processor M-5:**\n*   Stages ($k_5$): 5\n*   Clock Cycle ($T_5$): 10 ns\n*   Latency per instruction: $5 \\times 10 = 50$ ns\n*   Max Throughput (ideal): $1 / 10$ ns$^{-1} = 0.1$ instructions/ns\n\n**Processor M-7:**\n*   Stages ($k_7$): 7\n*   Clock Cycle ($T_7$): 7.5 ns\n*   Latency per instruction: $7 \\times 7.5 = 52.5$ ns\n*   Max Throughput (ideal): $1 / 7.5$ ns$^{-1} \\approx 0.133$ instructions/ns\n\n**Evaluation of Statements:**\n\n*   **Statement I:** \"M-7’s pipeline has better maximum throughput than M-5’s pipeline.\"\n    *   Max throughput is determined by the clock cycle time (specifically $1/T_{cycle}$). Since M-7 has a shorter clock cycle (7.5 ns vs 10 ns), it can complete more instructions per unit time once the pipeline is full.\n    *   $0.133 > 0.1$, so Statement I is **True**.\n\n*   **Statement II:** \"The latency of a single instruction is shorter on M-7’s pipeline than on M-5’s pipeline.\"\n    *   Latency for a single instruction (without pipelining overlap considerations for subsequent instructions) is $k \\times T$.\n    *   M-5 Latency: 50 ns.\n    *   M-7 Latency: 52.5 ns.\n    *   $52.5 > 50$, so M-7 has *longer* latency. Statement II is **False**.\n\n*   **Statement III:** \"Programs executing on M-7 will always run faster than programs executing on M-5.\"\n    *   While M-7 has higher throughput, \"always\" is too strong. If a program consists of very few instructions, the overhead of filling the deeper pipeline (7 stages vs 5 stages) might make M-5 faster due to lower latency per instruction. Also, real-world performance depends on branch penalties, memory access, etc., which are not specified but imply \"always\" is incorrect. Even strictly looking at latency/throughput, for small instruction counts, the higher latency of M-7 hurts performance. Statement III is **False**.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nOnly Statement I 