      "generated_answer": "The question asks which receptor type transduces mechanical energy to electrochemical energy.\n\n- Thermoreceptors (A) transduce thermal energy.\n- Pressure receptors (C) transduce mechanical energy (pressure/mechanical stimuli) into electrochemical signals.\n- Photoreceptors/rods and cones (E, F) transduce light energy.\n- Nociceptors (D) respond to potentially damaging stimuli (often mechanical, thermal, or chemical).\n- Olfactory (G), taste (J), and auditory (H) receptors transduce chemical, chemical, and mechanical (sound wave) energy, respectively.\n- Muscular and glandular effectors (B, I) are effectors, not sensory receptors.\n\nThe best general answer that directly describes mechanical-to-electrochemical transduction is pressure receptors (C). However, auditory receptors (H) also transduce mechanical energy (sound waves) into electrochemical signals. Between these, pressure receptors (C) is the more direct and classical example of mechanical transduction.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
