    "prediction": "MiniMax-M3",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is \\boxed{C}.\n\nWhen the corpus callosum is cut (split-brain surgery), the two hemispheres cannot communicate. In a right-handed patient, the left hemisphere (which controls language) receives sensory information from the right side of the body and visual field. Objects placed in the left hand send tactile information to the right hemisphere, but without the corpus callosum, this information cannot transfer to the left hemisphere's language centers. Therefore, the patient cannot verbally name an object placed in the left hand if they cannot see it, though they can recognize and use it.",
