    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_108",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Use indirect truth tables to determine whether the following argument is valid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\nH ≡ (I · ~J)\nK ≡ ~H\n~(K ⊃ J) / I ≡ J\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when I, H, and K are true and J is false\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when I and H are true and J and K are false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when K is true and I, H, and J are false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine validity, we assume the premises are true and the conclusion is false. The conclusion $I \\equiv J$ is false when $I$ and $J$ have different truth values. Let's test Option B: $I=T, H=T, K=T, J=F$. Premise 1: $H \\equiv (I \\cdot \\sim J)$ becomes $T \\equiv (T \\cdot T)$, which is True. Premise 2: $K \\equiv \\sim H$ becomes $T \\equiv \\sim T$, which is $T \\equiv F$, False. Since a premise is false, this is not a counterexample. Let's test Option C: $I=T, H=T, J=F, K=F$. Premise 1: $T \\equiv (T \\cdot T)$ is True. Premise 2: $F \\equiv \\sim T$ becomes $F \\equiv F$, which is True. Premise 3: $\\sim(K \\supset J)$ becomes $\\sim(F \\supset F)$, which is $\\sim(T)$, False. This is not a counterexample because the third premise is false. Let's test Option D: $K=T, I=F, H=F, J=F$. Premise 1: $F \\equiv (F \\cdot T)$ is True. Premise 2: $T \\equiv \\sim F$ becomes $T \\equiv T$, which is True. Premise 3: $\\sim(T \\supset F)$ becomes $\\sim(T)$, which is False. Not a counterexample.\n\nLet's re-evaluate. A counterexample requires all premises to be True and the conclusion False.\nConclusion $I \\equiv J$ is false if $(I=T, J=F)$ or $(I=F, J=T)$.\n\nCase 1: $I=T, J=F$.\nPremise 3: $\\sim(K \\supset J)$ must be True, so $K \\supset J$ must be False. This implies $K=T$ and $J=F$. So far: $I=T, J=F, K=T$.\nPremise 2: $K \\equiv \\sim H$ must be True. Since $K=T$, $\\sim H$ must be True, so $H=F$. So far: $I=T, J=F, K=T, H=F$.\nPremise 1: $H \\equiv (I \\cdot \\sim J)$ must be True. Substitute values: $F \\equiv (T \\cdot \\sim F)$ which is $F \\equiv (T \\cdot T)$ which is $F \\equiv T$. This is False.\nSo, there is no counterexample with $I=T, J=F$.\n\nCase 2: $I=F, J=T$.\nPremise 3: $\\sim(K \\supset J)$ must be True, so $K \\supset J$ must be False. This implies $K=T$ and $J=F$. But we assumed $J=T$. Contradiction. $K \\supset T$ is always True, so $\\sim(K \\supset T)$ is always False. Thus, Premise 3 can never be True if $J=T$.\nTherefore, it is impossible for Premise 3 to be True while the conclusion is false via the path $J=T$.\n\nWait, let's re-read Premise 3: $\\sim(K \\supset J)$. For this to be True, $K \\supset J$ must be False. This requires $K=True$ and $J=False$.\nSo any counterexample MUST have $K=T$ and $J=F$.\nSince $J=F$, for the conclusion $I \\equiv J$ to be False, $I$ must be $T$.\nSo a counterexample requires: $K=T, J=F, I=T$.\nNow check Premise 2: $K \\equiv \\sim H$. Since $K=T$, we need $\\sim H = T$, so $H=F$.\nSo the assignment is $I=T, J=F, K=T, H=F$.\nNow check Premise 1: $H \\equiv (I \\cdot \\sim J)$.\nLHS: $H = F$.\nRHS: $I \\cdot \\sim J = T \\cdot \\sim F = T \\cdot T = T$.\n$F \\equiv T$ is False.\nSo Premise 1 is False. This assignment is NOT a counterexample because a premise is false.\n\nIs there any other way?\nFor Premise 3 to be true, we MUST have $K=T$ and $J=F$.\nFor Conclusion to be false, since $J=F$, we MUST have $I=T$.\nFor Premise 2 to be true ($K \\equiv \\sim H$), since $K=T$, we MUST have $H=F$.\nThis forces the valuation $I=T, J=F, K=T, H=F$.\nWe just showed that under this valuation, Premise 1 is False.\nThus, there is NO valuation where all premises are True and the conclusion is False.\nThe argument is Valid.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 442,
        "output_tokens": 1095,
        "total_tokens": 1537
      },
