    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_97",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion:  Use indirect truth tables to determine whether the following argument is valid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\nL ⊃ [(M ∨ ~N) ⊃ O]\n(N ⊃ O) ⊃ (~P ⊃ Q)\nR ⊃ ~Q / L ⊃ (R ⊃ P)\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when L, M, O, Q, and R are true and N and P are false\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when L, N, O, Q, and R are true and M and P are false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when L, N, and R are true and M, O, P, and Q are false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine validity, we assume the premises are true and the conclusion $L \\supset (R \\supset P)$ is false. This requires $L$ to be true and $(R \\supset P)$ to be false, which implies $R$ is true and $P$ is false. Substituting these values ($L=T, R=T, P=F$) into the premises leads to a contradiction or specific truth value assignments for $M, N, O, Q$. Checking Option B: $L=T, M=T, O=T, Q=T, R=T, N=F, P=F$. Premise 1: $T \\supset [(T \\lor T) \\supset T]$ is True. Premise 2: $(F \\supset T) \\supset (T \\supset T)$ is $T \\supset T$, which is True. Premise 3: $T \\supset F$ is False. Since Premise 3 is false, this is not a counterexample where all premises are true. Let's re-evaluate. A counterexample must have all premises True and Conclusion False.\nConclusion False: $L=T$, $R=T$, $P=F$.\nPremise 3 ($R \\supset \\sim Q$): $T \\supset \\sim Q$ must be True $\\implies \\sim Q$ is True $\\implies Q$ is False.\nSo far: $L=T, R=T, P=F, Q=F$.\nPremise 2 ($(N \\supset O) \\supset (\\sim P \\supset Q)$): $\\sim P$ is $T$, so $\\sim P \\supset Q$ is $T \\supset F$ which is False. For the implication to be True, the antecedent $(N \\supset O)$ must be False. This requires $N=T$ and $O=F$.\nSo far: $L=T, R=T, P=F, Q=F, N=T, O=F$.\nPremise 1 ($L \\supset [(M \\lor \\sim N) \\supset O]$): $L$ is True, so $[(M \\lor \\sim N) \\supset O]$ must be True. We know $N=T$, so $\\sim N = F$. The expression becomes $[(M \\lor F) \\supset F]$, which simplifies to $[M \\supset F]$. For this to be True, $M$ must be False.\nFinal assignment: $L=T, M=F, N=T, O=F, P=F, Q=F, R=T$.\nLet's check the options again.\nOption A: Valid.\nOption B: $L, M, O, Q, R$ true; $N, P$ false.\nOption C: $L, N, O, Q, R$ true; $M, P$ false.\nOption D: $L, N, R$ true; $M, O, P, Q$ false.\n\nLet's test Option D: $L=T, N=T, R=T, M=F, O=F, P=F, Q=F$.\nConclusion: $L \\supset (R \\supset P) \\equiv T \\supset (T \\supset F) \\equiv T \\supset F \\equiv F$. (Conclusion is False).\nPremise 3: $R \\supset \\sim Q \\equiv T \\supset \\sim F \\equiv T \\supset T \\equiv T$. (True)\nPremise 2: $(N \\supset O) \\supset (\\sim P \\supset Q) \\equiv (T \\supset F) \\supset (T \\supset F) \\equiv F \\supset F \\equiv T$. (True)\nPremise 1: $L \\supset [(M \\lor \\sim N) \\supset O] \\equiv T \\supset [(F \\lor F) \\supset F] \\equiv T \\supset [F \\supset F] \\equiv T \\supset T \\equiv T$. (True)\nAll premises are True and Conclusion is False. Thus, the argument is invalid, and Option D provides a valid counterexample.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
