  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_530",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: You have just redesigned your kitchen. The new floor dimensions are 7'6\" × 11'8\". Now you want to cover the floor with linoleum, which sells for $1.79 per running foot in 6' width, what is the best way to cover the floor? How much will it cost?\n\nOptions: \nA. $48.60\nB. $26.85\nC. $30.99\nD. $42.56\nE. $29.99\nF. $38.40\nG. $31.50\nH. $23.94\nI. $20.12\nJ. $35.70\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To cover the 7'6\" (7.5 ft) width, you need to use the 6' wide rolls side-by-side. One strip of 6' width covers 6' of the 7.5' dimension, requiring an additional strip or overlapping. The most efficient layout typically involves cutting strips to fit the 11'8\" (11.67 ft) length. Since the roll is 6' wide, you can place one 6' wide strip along the 11'8\" length. The remaining 1.5' width requires another strip, also cut to 11'8\". Total linear feet needed = 11.67 + 11.67 = 23.34 ft. Cost = 23.34 * $1.79 ≈ $41.78. Looking at the options, $42.56 is the closest, likely accounting for waste or specific cutting patterns (e.g., buying full 12ft lengths for both strips: 12+12=24ft, 24*1.79=$42.96, still close). Let's re-evaluate standard practices. Usually, you buy enough length to cover the longest dimension. For 7.5' width, you need two 6' widths, but they overlap or are seamed. Actually, you need to cover 7.5' width. One 6' roll leaves 1.5'. You need another piece. If you run parallel to the 11'8\" side, you need two pieces of length 11'8\". $11.67 \\times 2 = 23.34$ linear feet. $23.34 \\times 1.79 = 41.78$. Option D is $42.56. Let's check if we run parallel to the 7.5' side. Length needed is 7.5'. Width covered is 11.8'. You need 2 strips of 7.5' length? No, the roll is 6' wide. To cover 11'8\" width, you need $\\lceil 11.67 / 6 \\rceil = 2$ strips. Each strip must be 7.5' long. Total linear feet = $7.5 \\times 2 = 15$ feet. Cost = $15 \\times 1.79 = 26.85$. This matches Option B exactly.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 471,
        "output_tokens": 552,
        "total_tokens": 1023
      },
      "provider": "agnes",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 9.693e-05,
    "accuracy": 1.0,
    "for_optimality": false
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_227",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Mrs. Bracken is a widow who maintains a home for herself, her dependent father, and her two children. (a) What filing status should she claim? (b) How many exemptions is she allowed?\n\nOptions: \nA. Single, four exemptions\nB. Qualifying widow(er), three exemptions\nC. Qualifying widow(er), six exemptions\nD. Married filing jointly, three exemptions\nE. Qualifying widow(er), five exemptions\nF. Married filing jointly, four exemptions\nG. Married filing separately, two exemptions\nH. Single, three exemptions\nI. Head of household, three exemptions\nJ. Head of household, four exemptions\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Mrs. Bracken qualifies as \"Head of Household\" because she is unmarried, pays more than half the cost of maintaining a home for herself and her dependents (father and children). Under tax laws applicable when exemptions were claimed, she is entitled to one exemption for herself and one for each dependent, totaling four exemptions. Therefore, she should file as Head of Household with four exemptions.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
