    "global index": "MMLUPro_business_141",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Assume the Black-Scholes framework. For $t \\ge 0$, let $S(t)$ be the time-$t$ price of a nondividend-paying stock. You are given:\n(i) $S(0)=0.5\n(ii) The stock price process is $\\frac{{dS(t)}}{{S(t)}} = 0.05dt+0.2dZ(t)$ where $Z(t)$ is a standart Brownian motion.\n(iii) $E[S(1)^\\alpha]=1.4$, where $\\alpha$ is a negative constant.\n(iv) The continuously compounded risk-free interest rate is $3%$.\nConsider a contingent claim that pays $S(1)^\\alpha$ at time 1. What is the time-0 price of the contigent claim?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1.450\nB. 1.475\nC. 1.415\nD. 1.250\nE. 1.360\nF. 1.390\nG. 1.325\nH. 1.500\nI. 1.287\nJ. 1.372\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "MiniMax-M3",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We need to find the time-0 price of a contingent claim paying $S(1)^\\alpha$ at time 1.\n\nUnder Black-Scholes, the price is $e^{-r}E^Q[S(1)^\\alpha]$ where the expectation is under the risk-neutral measure.\n\nThe risk-neutral drift is $r = 0.03$, so under Q:\n$$E^Q[S(1)^\\alpha] = S(0)^\\alpha \\exp\\left(\\alpha(r - \\tfrac{1}{2}\\sigma^2) + \\tfrac{1}{2}\\alpha^2\\sigma^2\\right)$$\n\nWith $S(0) = 0.5$, $\\alpha r - \\frac{1}{2}\\alpha\\sigma^2 + \\frac{1}{2}\\alpha^2\\sigma^2 = 0.03\\alpha + 0.02\\alpha^2$.\n\nFrom condition (iii): $E[S(1)^\\alpha] = S(0)^\\alpha \\exp\\left(\\alpha(\\mu - \\tfrac{1}{2}\\sigma^2) + \\tfrac{1}{2}\\alpha^2\\sigma^2\\right) = 1.4$\n\nWith $\\mu = 0.05$: $0.05\\alpha - 0.02\\alpha + 0.02\\alpha^2 = 0.03\\alpha + 0.02\\alpha^2 = \\ln(1.4/0.5^\\alpha) = \\ln(1.4) - \\alpha\\ln(0.5)$\n\nSo: $0.03\\alpha + 0.02\\alpha^2 = \\ln(1.4) + 0.6931\\alpha$\n\n$0.02\\alpha^2 - 0.6631\\alpha - 0.3365 = 0$\n\n$\\alpha = \\frac{0.6631 \\pm \\sqrt{0.4397 + 0.02692}}{0.04} = \\frac{0.6631 \\pm 0.6826}{0.04}$\n\nTaking negative root: $\\alpha = \\frac{0.6631 - 0.6826}{0.04} = \\frac{-0.0195}{0.04} = -0.4875$\n\nThen risk-neutral expectation: $E^Q[S(1)^\\alpha] = 1.4 \\cdot \\exp((0.03-0.05)\\alpha) = 1.4 \\cdot e^{-0.02\\alpha} = 1.4 \\cdot e^{0.00975} \\approx 1.4 \\times 1.009798 \\approx 1.4137$\n\nPrice = $e^{-0.03} \\times 1.4137 = 0.9704 \\times 1.4137 \\approx 1.372$\n\nAnswer: \\boxed{J}",
