    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5659",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which of the following features of financial asset return time-series could be captured using a standard GARCH(1,1) model?\n\ni) Fat tails in the return distribution\n\n\nii) Leverage effects\n\n\niii) Volatility clustering\n\n\niv) Volatility affecting returns\n\nOptions: \nA. (ii) and (iv) only\nB. (iii) and (iv) only\nC. (i) and (iii) only\nD. (i), (ii), and (iv) only\nE. (ii) and (iii) only\nF. (i) and (iv) only\nG. (i), (ii), and (iii) only\nH. (i), (ii), (iii), and (iv)\nI. (ii), (iii), and (iv) only\nJ. (i), (iii), and (iv) only\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The standard GARCH(1,1) model is primarily designed to capture **volatility clustering** (iii), where large changes tend to be followed by large changes and small by small. While the conditional distribution can exhibit **fat tails** (i) if the error terms are non-normal (e.g., Student's t), the GARCH structure itself models the variance dynamics that lead to this appearance. It does not inherently model **leverage effects** (ii) (asymmetric response to positive/negative shocks) or **volatility affecting returns** (iv) (feedback from volatility to mean returns) without specific modifications like EGARCH or GJR-GARCH. Therefore, the most accurate features captured are fat tails (in the resulting distribution) and volatility clustering.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 493,
        "output_tokens": 161,
        "total_tokens": 654
      },
      "provider": "agnes",
