    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_6317",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What are the dimensions of $\\hat{{u}}^t \\hat{{u}}?\n\nOptions: \nA. k x T\nB. T x k\nC. T x k x 1\nD. T x T\nE. 1 x 1\nF. 1 x T\nG. k x k\nH. 1 x k\nI. T x 1\nJ. k x 1\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The notation $\\hat{u}^t$ typically denotes the transpose of a vector $\\hat{u}$ with $T$ elements (time periods) or potentially $k$ elements depending on context, but in standard linear algebra involving residuals or vectors indexed by time $T$, if $\\hat{u}$ is a $T \\times 1$ column vector, then $\\hat{u}^t$ is $1 \\times T$. The product of a $1 \\times T$ row vector and a $T \\times 1$ column vector results in a scalar, which has dimensions $1 \\times 1$. Even if interpreted as an outer product (which would be written differently), the inner product notation $\\hat{u}^t \\hat{u}$ yields a scalar sum of squares. Therefore, the resulting dimension is a single value.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
