  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5976",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Imagine someone who is not looking for work because they are embarrassed in the interview process when their inability to read is revealed. However this person would take just about any job that was offered. According to the Bureau of Labor Statistics this person is\n\nOptions: \nA. employed but seeking better employment.\nB. in the labor force but not actively seeking employment.\nC. in the labor force and employed.\nD. in the labor force but not counted as unemployed.\nE. not in the labor force but counted as unemployed.\nF. not in the labor force but considered underemployed.\nG. not in the labor force but seeking employment.\nH. in the labor force but considered underemployed.\nI. in the labor force and unemployed.\nJ. not in the labor force.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "agnes-2.0-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "According to the Bureau of Labor Statistics, individuals who want a job but are not actively looking because they believe no jobs are available for them (or face barriers like lack of skills/education that prevent them from applying) are classified as \"discouraged workers.\" Discouraged workers are **not in the labor force** and are **not counted as unemployed**. Since this person is not actively seeking work due to embarrassment (a barrier to application), they fall into the category of being out of the labor force.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
      "success": true,
