hich is identification. Diagnostic testing confirms if the chosen order is sufficient. If residuals have structure, model is too small. If we use overfitting and extra coeffs are insignificant, model is not too large. So (i) is a conclusion of the tests. Let's consider if (ii) is false. No, it's not false. Is it possible the answer is C? Some sources distinguish \"Diagnostic Checking\" (white noise) from \"Model Validation\" (assumptions). But Box-Jenkins includes checking assumptions. Let's assume the most comprehensive correct answer. G includes all valid points.\n\nWait, let's look at statement (i) again. \"The tests will show whether the identified model is either too large or too small\". Diagnostic checking primarily detects if the model is too *small* (residuals still correlated). It doesn't directly prove a model is too *large* unless you use the overfitting approach (iv). So (i) is true *in conjunction* with methods like (iv). Statement (iii) is the direct result of a good fit. Statement (iv) is a specific technique. Statement (ii) is about residual properties. All seem true.\n\nLet's double check if there's a trick. Is (ii) false because Box-Jenkins assumes normality? No, you check if it holds. Is (i) false because identification determines size, diagnostics checks validity? Diagnostics tells you if the *identified* model is adequate. If not, you go back. So it helps identify if it's too small/large.\n\nLet's compare C and G. If I must choose, G is factually more complete. However, in some strict academic contexts, \"Box-Jenkins diagnostic checking\" is synonymous with \"checking if residuals are white noise\". In that narrow view, (ii) might be considered outside the core scope (which is autocorrelation). But (ii) lists \"autocorrelation, heteroscedasticity, and non-normality\". Autocorrelation is the main one. The others are often checked. If the question comes from a source that emphasizes the Ljung-Box test only, then (ii) might be seen as \"including 