    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The standardized residuals from a GARCH model are typically assumed to follow a specific distribution (often normal or Student's t), but in practice, they often exhibit fat tails (iii). If the GARCH model is correctly specified, the standardized residuals should be independent and identically distributed, meaning their squares should *not* be autocorrelated (making ii false). Raw residuals differ from standardized residuals because standardized ones are divided by the conditional volatility (making iv false). Therefore, only statement (iii) is practically true among the choices, but looking at the options, statement (i) is the standard *assumption* made during estimation, even if reality deviates. However, strictly speaking, (ii) is false for an adequate model, and (iv) is false by definition. Let's re-evaluate (i). Standard GARCH models often assume normality for the standardized errors $z_t$, so (i) is a common assumption. Statement (ii) claims squares are related to lagged squares if the model is appropriate; this is incorrect because appropriateness implies no remaining ARCH effects (no relation). Thus, (ii) is false. Statement (iii) is true in practice. Statement (iv) is false. So we look for options containing (i) and/or (iii) but excluding (ii) and (iv). Option E includes (i) and (iii).\n\n\\boxed{E}",
