      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is \\boxed{I}.\n\nUnder the test articulated in *United States v. Kahriger* (1953), a tax is constitutionally valid so long as it produces some revenue, regardless of its regulatory purpose, and the dominant intent of Congress was to raise revenue rather than impose an indirect penalty or prohibition. Therefore, the tax would survive a constitutional challenge if the dominant purpose was revenue generation, even if it had the incidental effect of burdening handgun ownership.",
