      "generated_answer": "\nThe first equation, \\(\\sqrt{\\log_b n} = \\log_b \\sqrt{n}\\), simplifies to \\(\\sqrt{x} = \\frac{x}{2}\\) where \\(x = \\log_b n\\). Solving this gives \\(x = 4\\) (since \\(x = 0\\) is invalid due to the second equation). Thus, \\(\\log_b n = 4\\), so \\(n = b^4\\).\n\nThe second equation, \\(b \\cdot \\log_b n = \\log_b (b n)\\), simplifies to \\(b x = 1 + x\\). Substituting \\(x = 4\\) gives \\(b \\cdot 4 = 5\\), so \\(b = \\frac{5}{4}\\).\n\nThen, \\(n = \\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)^4 = \\frac{625}{256}\\). Since 625 and 256 are relatively prime, \\(j = 625\\), \\(k = 256\\), and \\(j + k = 881\\).\n\nVerification shows that both equations are satisfied with \\(n = \\frac{625}{256}\\) and \\(b = \\frac{5}{4}\\).\n\n\\boxed{881}",
