. What about 1x25 + 3x10 + 0x1 = 5 coins? 25+30=55. Yes. So 55 fails (Greedy 7, Optimal 5).\nActually, a simpler approach: The greedy algorithm fails for $N$ if the optimal representation uses fewer coins. This typically happens when we replace a 25-cent coin with smaller coins? No, replacing larger with smaller increases count. We replace smaller with larger to decrease count. The greedy algorithm picks the largest possible. Failures occur when picking the largest *prevents* a subsequent large pick that would reduce the total count significantly.\nFor the system $\\{1, 10, 25\\}$, it is known that the greedy algorithm is optimal for all $N$ except those congruent to $30, 40, 55, 65, 80, 95 \\pmod{100}$? Let's verify.\nLet's check small numbers.\n1-9: Greedy 1x1..9x1. Optimal same. Success.\n10-19: Greedy 1x10 + kx1. Optimal same. Success.\n20-24: Greedy 2x10. Optimal same. Success.\n25-29: Greedy 1x25 + kx1. Optimal same. Success.\n30: Greedy 1x25 + 5x1 (6 coins). Optimal 3x10 (3 coins). Fail.\n31-39: Greedy 1x25 + 6..14x1. E.g., 39: 25+14x1 (15 coins). Optimal: 3x10+9x1 (12 coins) or 1x25+14x1. Can we do better? 2x10+19x1 (21). 3x10+9x1 (12). 1x25+14x1 (15). So 39 fails.\nGenerally, for $N \\in [30, 49]$, let's see.\n$N=40$: Greedy 25+10+5x1 (7 coins). Optimal 4x10 (4 coins). Fail.\n$N=41$: Greedy 25+10+6x1 (8 coins). Optimal 4x10+1x1 (5 coins). Fail.\n...\n$N=49$: Greedy 25+10+14x1 (16 coins). Optimal 4x10+9x1 (13 coins). Fail.\n$N=50$: Greedy 2x25 (2 coins). Optimal 5x10 (5 coins). Success.\n$N=51$: Greedy 2x25+1x1 (3 coins). Optimal 5x10+1x1 (6 coins). Success.\n...\n$N=59$: Greedy 2x25+9x1 (11 coins). Optimal 5x10+9x1 (14 coins). Or 1x25+3x10+4x1 (8 coins)? 25+30+4=59. Coins: 1+3+4=8. Greedy: 25+25+9=16 coins. Wait. Greedy for 59: First 25 (rem 34). Next 25 (rem 9). Then 9x1. Total 2+9=11 coins.\nIs there a better way? 5x10 + 9x1 = 14 coins. 2x25 + 9x1 = 11 coins. 1x25 + 3x10 + 4x1 = 8 coins. 8 < 11. So 59 FAILS.\nMy previous assumption that 25s are always good is wrong because mixing 10s can be more efficient for the remainder.\nLet's formalize. We want to minimize $n_1 + n_{10} + n_{25}$ subject to $n_1 + 10n_{10} + 25n_{25} = N$.\nGreedy strategy: Maximize $n_{25}$, then max $n_{10}$, then $n_1$.\nLet $N = 25q + r$, where $0 \\le r < 25$.\nGreedy coins: $q + \\lfloor r/10 \\rfloor + (r \\bmod 10)$.\nOptimal coins: We can try reducing $q$ by 1 (or more) and adjusting $n_{10}, n_1$.\nIf we reduce $n_{25}$ by 1, we have $r+25$ to cover with 10s and 1s.\nLet $M = r+25$. We want to represent $M$ with min $n_{10} + n_1$.\nSince 10 divides 10, we use as many 10s as possible: $\\lfloor M/10 \\rfloor$ tens and $M \\bmod 10$ ones.\nCost for remainder part if $n_{25}=k$ (where $k < q$): $(q-k) + (\\text{cost to represent } N - 25k \\text{ with 10s and 1s})$.\nLet $R_k = N - 25k$. Cost $C_k = (q-k) + \\lfloor R_k/10 \\rfloor + (R_k \\bmod 10)$.\nWe compare $C_q$ (Greedy) with $\\min_{k < q} C_k$.\nNote that $R_q = r$. $C_q = q + \\lfloor r/10 \\rfloor + (r \\bmod 10)$.\nConsider $k=q-1$. $R_{q-1} = r+25$.\n$C_{q-1} = (q-1) + \\lfloor (r+25)/10 \\rfloor + ((r+25) \\bmod 10)$.\nLet $r = 10a + b$, with $0 \\le a \\le 2, 0 \\le b \\le 9$.\n$C_q = q + a + b$.\n$r+25 = 10a + b + 25 = 10(a+2) + (b+5)$ if $b+5 < 10$, i.e., $b \\le 4$.\nIf $b \\le 4$:\n$\\lfloor (r+25)/10 \\rfloor = a+2$.\n$(r+25) \\bmod 10 = b+5$.\n$C_{q-1} = q - 1 + a + 2 + b + 5 = q + a + b + 6$.\n$C_{q-1} - C_q = 6 > 0$. So reducing 25s by 1 is worse if $b \\le 4$.\nIf $b \\ge 5$:\n$r+25 = 10a + b + 25 = 10(a+3) + (b-5)$.\n$\\lfloor (r+25)/10 \\rfloor = a+3$.\n$(r+25) \\bmod 10 = b-5$.\n$C_{q-1} = q - 1 + a + 3 + b - 5 = q + a + b - 3$.\n$C_{q-1} - C_q = -3 < 0$. So reducing 25s by 1 is BETTER if $b \\ge 5$.\nSo for any $N$, if the units digit of the remainder $r$ (when divided by 25) is $\\ge 5$, the greedy algorithm is NOT optimal (we can swap one 25 for two 10s and adjust 1s to save 3 coins).\nWait, let's check $N=30$. $30 = 1 \\times 25 + 5$. $r=5, a=0, b=5$.\n$b 